---@overload fun(handle: Cutscene, event: "end", filter?: fun(event: CutsceneEndEvent): boolean): CutsceneEndEvent
---@overload fun(handle: Cutscene, event: "cue", filter?: fun(event: CutsceneCueEvent): boolean): CutsceneCueEvent
function nf.wait_for(handle, event, filter) end

--- A saved table for the whole project, by name: `nf.data("shop")` is the same table in every script, so a module keeps its own under its own name and two modules never collide. Each name is its own file in the data directory, kept across restarts. It may hold strings, numbers, booleans, tables of them (keyed by strings, or a list numbered from 1), and the API's own values: `Vec3`s, `Location`s, `Item` tables, and `Player`, `Entity`, `Centity` and `World` handles, which come back as the same types (a handle to something gone answers `nil` or `false`, as handles do). Anything else (a function, a `Menu` window, a `Subscription`) is skipped when it's saved, with a line in the log naming where it was (`data.inventory[3]: a function can't be saved`). The table is live: change it and the change is saved, at the next autosave (every five minutes), when the project reloads and when the server stops. It stays in memory across hot reloads rather than being read again, so a script that reloads finds it as it left it. A table that would take more than 1 MiB isn't saved (the last save stands), and the log says so. A name that isn't lowercase letters, digits and `_` (starting with a letter or digit, at most 64 characters) is an error.
---@param name string Which table, like `"shop"`.
---@return table
function nf.data(name) end

--- A database. With no name it is this package's own: a SQLite file that belongs to it alone (each package, a dependency included, has its own, under the server's `plugins/NetherForge/databases/`), whose tables come from the package's `migrations/NNN_name.sql`, run in order when the project loads. With a name it is one of the MySQL or PostgreSQL connections the server's owner set up under `databases:` in the plugin's `config.yml`: the script names it and never sees a host, a user or a password, and the connection opens only to the packages the owner lists for it. Either way it's the same `Database`, so the same calls work (the SQL is the server's own dialect; see `Database` for what differs). A name that isn't set up, or isn't open to this package, is an error.
--- Needs `"requires": { "db": true }` in `netherforge.json`: without it, calling it is an error.
---@param name? string The connection, as the server owner named it in `config.yml`, like `"network"`. Leave it out for the package's own SQLite database.
---@return Database
function nf.db(name) end

--- One of this package's settings: what whoever runs the server set it to, or its default when they haven't. Settings are declared in `netherforge.json`'s `settings` (each with a type, a default and a description) and changed by the server's owner, never by scripts; a package reads only its own. The value is a `boolean`, an `integer`, a `number` or a `string`, by the setting's type. A script that reads a setting and doesn't listen for `setting_changed` is restarted when that setting changes; one that listens hears the change instead. A name the package doesn't declare is an error.
---@param name SettingName The setting's name, as `netherforge.json` declares it.
---@return any
function nf.config(name) end

--- Lua instructions this call may still run before it's stopped. A loop over a lot of work can check it and leave the rest for the next tick. Coarse: it's updated every hundred instructions or so.
---@return integer
function nf.instructions_left() end

--- The server itself: its clock, its performance, its version, its settings (players, message of the day, whitelist), and saying or running things as the console.
---@class nf.server
nf.server = {}

--- Server ticks since NetherForge started (20 a second): the clock to measure durations with. Use `nf.server.unix_time()` only for timestamps that must mean something after a restart.
---@return integer
function nf.server.tick() end

--- The wall clock: milliseconds since 1970-01-01 UTC. For timestamps that outlive a restart (daily rewards, cooldowns saved to a file); measure durations in ticks.
---@return integer
function nf.server.unix_time() end

--- How many ticks the server managed in the last second or so: 20 when it keeps up, less when it lags.
---@return number
function nf.server.ticks_per_second() end

--- How long a tick has been taking lately, in milliseconds, averaged over the last few seconds. Over 50 means the server is lagging.
---@return number
function nf.server.tick_milliseconds() end

--- The running server's Minecraft version, like `"26.3"`.
---@return string
function nf.server.minecraft_version() end

--- Sends a line to everyone online and the console, as the server.
---@param text Text MiniMessage, like `"<gold>The gate opens"`.
function nf.server.broadcast(text) end

--- Runs a server command as the console, with the console's permissions. The leading slash is optional.
---@param command string One command line.
---@return boolean # Whether the server ran it.
function nf.server.run(command) end

--- How many players the server lets in at once: what the server list shows as its maximum.
---@return integer
function nf.server.max_players() end

--- Changes how many players the server lets in at once, until it restarts (`server.properties` is left alone). Nobody online is kicked when it drops below how many there are.
--- Needs `"requires": { "moderation": true }` in `netherforge.json`: without it, calling it is an error.
---@param count integer At least 0.
function nf.server.set_max_players(count) end

--- The message of the day: the text under the server's name in the server list, as MiniMessage.
---@return Text
function nf.server.motd() end

--- Changes the message of the day, until the server restarts (`server.properties` is left alone). To change it for one ping only, listen to `server_list_ping`.
--- Needs `"requires": { "moderation": true }` in `netherforge.json`: without it, calling it is an error.
---@param text Text MiniMessage; a line break starts the second line.
function nf.server.set_motd(text) end

--- Whether the whitelist is on: only players on it (`player:is_whitelisted()`) and operators can join.
---@return boolean
function nf.server.is_whitelist_enabled() end

--- Turns the whitelist on or off. Turning it on doesn't kick anyone online who isn't on it.
--- Needs `"requires": { "moderation": true }` in `netherforge.json`: without it, calling it is an error.
---@param enabled boolean
function nf.server.set_whitelist_enabled(enabled) end

--- Slash commands a module adds to the server for as long as it runs, with typed arguments, tab completion and usage messages from their declaration.
---@class nf.commands
nf.commands = {}

--- Adds `/name` to the server for as long as the module runs. Only modules can register commands, because a command outlives any one centity. The definition declares its arguments and subcommands; NetherForge checks what's typed against them, answers mistakes with a usage message, completes them as players type, and hands the handler typed values. Returns `false` when the name is already a command, the server's or another module's. A handler that errors is logged and whoever typed the command is told; the command stays. A name that isn't usable, or a definition with a mistake in it (an unknown key, an unknown argument type, an optional argument before a required one), is an error.
---@param name string Lowercase letters, digits, `_` and `-`, starting with a letter. Without the slash.
---@param definition? CommandDefinition|fun(event: CommandEvent) Description, permission, aliases, arguments and subcommands. Can be left out, with the handler in its place: `nf.commands.register(name, handler)`.
---@param handler? fun(event: CommandEvent) Runs when someone types the command (with no subcommand). Can be left out when the definition has subcommands: typing the bare command then shows its usage.
---@return boolean # `true` once the command is registered.
function nf.commands.register(name, definition, handler) end

--- Players: everyone online, or anyone who has ever joined.
---@class nf.players
nf.players = {}

--- Everyone online.
---@return Player[]
function nf.players.online() end

--- A player by name (any case) or UUID: anyone who has ever joined this server, online or not, or `nil` for someone who never has. For someone offline, `exists()` is `false` and what needs them in the world (`position()`, `send_message`) answers `nil` or `false` until they come back.
---@param name_or_id string A name, or a UUID like `player:id()`.
---@return Player?
function nf.players.get(name_or_id) end

--- Everyone who has ever joined this server, online or not, in no particular order. On a server that's been running a long time that's a lot of players, so call it when you need it (building a leaderboard) rather than every tick.
---@return Player[]
function nf.players.known() end

--- Everyone who's banned, whose ban hasn't run out.
---@return Player[]
function nf.players.banned() end

--- Everyone on the server's whitelist, whether it's on or not.
---@return Player[]
function nf.players.whitelisted() end

--- The server's worlds: finding them, and making, loading and copying them.
---@class nf.worlds
nf.worlds = {}

--- Every loaded world, the default one first.
---@return World[]
function nf.worlds.all() end

--- A world by name, or `nil` when the server has none by that name loaded.
---@param name string Its name: `"world_nether"`.
---@return World?
function nf.worlds.get(name) end

--- Makes a new world, and loads it: from then on it's the project's, so it can unload it (`world:unload`), and it stays the project's across reloads and restarts. A name that isn't an id (lowercase letters, digits and `_`), or that a world already has (loaded, or saved on the server), is an error: `nf.worlds.load` loads a saved one. A `terrain` that isn't one of the project's terrains is an error, and so are both a `generator` and a `terrain`, a `terrain` with another environment than `"normal"`, and a `dimension_type` that isn't one of the project's dimension types or that the server didn't start with. Making a world takes the server a moment.
---@param name string Its name, which is also its folder: `"arena_1"`.
---@param options? WorldCreateOptions
---@return World? # The new world, or `nil` when the server couldn't make it.
function nf.worlds.create(name, options) end

--- Loads a world the server has saved but not loaded (one a script created and unloaded, or any other world the server has), and returns it; one already loaded is just returned. `nil` when the server has no world saved by that name. A name with characters a world's can't have is an error.
---@param name string Its name: `"lobby"`.
---@return World?
function nf.worlds.load(name) end

--- Makes a new world from one of the project's maps (`maps/<map>/`): its files are copied, then the server loads the copy as a world named `name`, the project's from then on (as for `nf.worlds.create`). A map there's none of, or a `name` that isn't an id or that a world already has (or that another copy is being made into), is an error at once. A copy that can't be made (the disk, or the server refusing it) fails, and the server's log says so too. It works off the main thread, so its world arrives later: give `callback`, which is called with `world, err` once it's done, or call it inside a task (`nf.task`) without one, and it waits and returns `world, err`. Without a callback outside a task, it's an error. When it fails, `world` is `nil` and `err` says why.
---@param map MapId Which map.
---@param name string The new world's name: `"arena_2"`.
---@param callback? fun(world: World?, err: string?) Called on the main thread once it's done, as this script's code, with the new world (or `nil` and why not). Not called if the script has stopped by then.
---@return World? # Only without a callback, in a task: the new world, or `nil`.
---@return string? # Why it failed, when it did; `nil` when it worked.
---@overload async fun(map: MapId, name: string): World?, string?
---@overload fun(map: MapId, name: string, callback: fun(world: World?, err: string?))
function nf.worlds.copy(map, name, callback) end

--- The server's main world: where things go when nothing says which.
---@return World
function nf.worlds.default() end

--- Structures: saved boxes of blocks that `world:place_structure` places. The project's are `structures/<id>.nbt`; scripts save more with `world:save_structure`.
---@class nf.structures
nf.structures = {}

--- Whether there's a structure by that id: one of the project's, or one a script saved.
---@param id StructureId
---@return boolean
function nf.structures.exists(id) end

--- How big a structure is, in blocks along x, y and z (before it's turned), or `nil` when there's none by that id. One whose file can't be read is an error.
---@param id StructureId
---@return Vec3?
function nf.structures.size(id) end

--- Centity instances: spawning new ones and finding live ones.
---@class nf.centities
nf.centities = {}

--- Puts a new instance of a centity into the world. Its entities exist, its script's body has run and its `spawn` handlers have heard it by the time this returns. A centity the project doesn't have is an error.
---@param kind CentityId Which centity: its folder name under `centities/`.
---@param location_or_position Location|Vec3 Where its position (the anchor) goes: a `Location` (it faces the location's `yaw`, when it has one), or a position in the calling centity's world (the server's first world, in a module). Without a yaw it faces south (yaw 0).
---@return Centity? # The new instance, or `nil` when the world doesn't exist.
function nf.centities.spawn(kind, location_or_position) end

--- The live centity instance with this id, or `nil` once it's gone. Ids come from `centity:id()` and are fine to keep.
---@param id string An instance id.
---@return Centity?
function nf.centities.get(id) end

--- Every live centity instance, or those the filter picks. Includes the caller's own instance. A filter key that isn't one of `CentityFilter`'s is an error.
---@param filter? CentityFilter Only instances of one kind, in one world, or near a point.
---@return Centity[]
function nf.centities.all(filter) end

--- The project's menus (`menus/<id>/`), and menus scripts make.
---@class nf.menus
nf.menus = {}

--- The window of a menu whose file says `"shared": true`: the one every player sees. A menu that isn't shared has a window per player and no one to hand back, so this is `nil`; open one with `player:open_menu`. A menu the project doesn't have is an error.
---@param menu MenuId Its folder name under `menus/`.
---@return Menu?
function nf.menus.shared(menu) end

--- Makes a menu from a table instead of a file: a `MenuTemplate`, which `player:open_menu(template)` opens a new window of, as often as you like. The table says what `menu.json` would, in Lua spelling: `type` (`"chest"`, the default, `"barrel"`, `"shulker_box"`, `"hopper"`, `"dispenser"`, `"dropper"` or `"crafter"`), `rows` (a chest's 1 to 6, default 3), `title` (MiniMessage), `skin` (`<pack>/<key>`), `locked` (default `true`) and `slots`, items by slot number from 0. A template has no script of its own: put handlers on it (`template:on("click", ...)`), and they hear every window made from it. It lasts as long as the script that created it. A key it doesn't take, a value of the wrong type, a slot outside the window, an item the server doesn't have or a skin the project's resource packs don't have is an error, checked by the same rules as `menu.json`.
---@param definition table `{ type?, rows?, title?, skin?, locked?, slots? }`: what the menu is, as `menu.json` would say it, with `slots` as `{ [index] = item }`.
---@return MenuTemplate
function nf.menus.create(definition) end

--- The project's dialogs (`dialogs/<id>/`), and dialogs scripts make.
---@class nf.dialogs
nf.dialogs = {}

--- A dialog by id. There's one of each, so this always finds it; a dialog the project doesn't have is an error.
---@param dialog DialogId Its folder name under `dialogs/`.
---@return Dialog
function nf.dialogs.get(dialog) end

--- Makes a dialog from a table instead of a file. The table says what `dialog.json` would, in Lua spelling (`after_action`, `can_close_with_escape`, an input's `max_length`, items as `Item` tables): `title` (required), `type`, `body`, `inputs`, `buttons`, `columns`, `dialogs`, and the rest of the file's fields except `script`. The `Dialog` it returns works like any other: `open_for`, `ask`, `button(key)`, and `press` and `close` handlers on it and its buttons. Its id is made up and never names a folder under `dialogs/`. It lasts as long as the script that created it; after that its methods answer `nil` or `false` and its handlers are gone. A key it doesn't take, a value of the wrong type, or anything `dialog.json` would have an error for (two buttons with one key, a `dialog_list` naming a dialog there isn't) is an error.
---@param definition table What the dialog is, as `dialog.json` would say it, with its keys in snake_case.
---@return Dialog
function nf.dialogs.create(definition) end

--- The project's items (`items/<id>/`): item kinds of its own, whose stacks the server can always tell apart from any other stack of the same Minecraft kind.
---@class nf.items
nf.items = {}

--- A stack of a project item, as an `Item` table with every field its file sets, `item` naming it, and `count` 1: hand it to `player:give_item`, `menu:set_item` or anything else that takes an item. `overrides` sets fields of this stack: its `count`, its own `data`, or any other field. A field the file sets too is the stack's only until the file changes: then the stack takes the file's look again, keeping its count, damage and `data` (see the item format). A project item the project doesn't have is an error.
---@param item ItemId Its folder name under `items/`, or `namespace:id` for one a package it depends on exports (see [Names across packages](packages.md)).
---@param overrides? table `Item` fields for this stack, like `{ count = 3 }`. Any `Item` field but `kind` and `item`.
---@return Item
function nf.items.create(item, overrides) end

--- Which project item a stack is (its `item` field), or `nil` for a stack that isn't one. A stack carries its project item wherever it goes, so this tells a ruby from any other paper.
---@param stack Item An item table, as any event or inventory hands out.
---@return string?
function nf.items.id(stack) end

--- A project item by id, to listen to what players do with it (`nf.items.get("ruby"):on("use", ...)`), or `nil` when the project has no such item.
---@param item ItemId Its folder name under `items/`, or `namespace:id` for one a package it depends on exports (see [Names across packages](packages.md)).
---@return ProjectItem?
function nf.items.get(item) end

--- Every item the calling script can use: its own package's, and those the packages it depends on export.
---@return ProjectItem[]
function nf.items.all() end

--- The project's blocks (`blocks/<id>/`): blocks of its own, drawn by its resource pack, with their own hardness, drops and sounds, held in the world as note block states. A player places one with an item whose `block` names it, or a script with `ProjectBlock:place`.
---@class nf.blocks
nf.blocks = {}

--- A project block by id, to listen to what players do with it (`nf.blocks.get("ruby_ore"):on("break", ...)`) or to place one, or `nil` when the project has no such block.
---@param block BlockId Its folder name under `blocks/`, or `namespace:id` for one a package it depends on exports (see [Names across packages](packages.md)).
---@return ProjectBlock?
function nf.blocks.get(block) end

--- Every block the calling script can use: its own package's, and those the packages it depends on export.
---@return ProjectBlock[]
function nf.blocks.all() end

--- The project's recipes: those in `recipes/<id>.json`, and those scripts register. A recipe can take the project's own items as ingredients (`{ item = "ruby" }`), which only a stack of that item fits, however it looks; and a recipe takes a project item only where it names it, so a ruby built on paper never crafts as paper. Players see a recipe in their recipe book once they've discovered it (`player:discover_recipe`), but can craft it either way.
---@class nf.recipes
nf.recipes = {}

--- Adds a recipe the server learns at once, sent to everyone online. `definition` says what `recipes/<id>.json` would, in Lua spelling: `type` (`"shaped"`, `"shapeless"`, `"furnace"`, `"blasting"`, `"smoking"`, `"campfire_cooking"`, `"smithing_transform"` or `"stonecutting"`), then what that type takes: `pattern` and `key`, `ingredients`, `ingredient`, or `template`, `base` and `addition`; `result` (an `Item`), and `experience`, `cooking_time`, `group` and `category`. An ingredient is an item id (`"minecraft:stick"`), a tag (`"#minecraft:planks"`) or a project item (`{ item = "ruby" }`). Registering an id again replaces the recipe. It lasts as long as the script that registered it (so a reloaded script registers it again in its body). An id a `recipes/` file has, or anything that file would have an error for, is an error.
---@param id string Its id: lowercase letters, digits and `_`, like a file's name. Players discover it by this id.
---@param definition table What the recipe is, as `recipes/<id>.json` would say it, with its keys in snake_case.
function nf.recipes.register(id, definition) end

--- Takes away a recipe a script registered, from the server and from everyone's recipe book. `false` when there's no such recipe; one from `recipes/` is the file's, so removing it is an error (delete the file instead).
---@param id RecipeId Its id.
---@return boolean
function nf.recipes.remove(id) end

--- Every recipe's id: the project's files', then those scripts registered.
---@return string[]
function nf.recipes.all() end

--- Loot tables: the project's own (`loot/<id>.json`) and the game's. A roll picks from each of a table's pools, weighing its entries against each other, and gives the items it picked. The same `seed` always gives the same items (the editor rolls the same way). A table the project doesn't have, or that the game doesn't, is an error; so is a game table that needs something the roll doesn't say (a mob's table needs the mob, as `looted`).
---@class nf.loot
nf.loot = {}

--- Rolls a loot table and gives the items it picked, in the order it picked them: a stack each, more than one when a pick gives more than a stack holds. Nothing is put anywhere: give them to a player, drop them or fill a chest with them. A game table is rolled by the server where the context says (`location`, else its player's or looted entity's place), and needs one of them.
---@param table LootTableId A loot table of the project's (`"treasure"`, `loot/treasure.json`; a bare id is the calling package's own), one a package exports (`"acme:treasure"`), or one of the game's by its id (`"minecraft:chests/simple_dungeon"`).
---@param context? LootContext Who and what the roll is for: its player, tool, luck and seed. Leave it out for a roll with none of them.
---@return Item[]
function nf.loot.roll(table, context) end

--- Rolls a loot table into an inventory the way the game fills a chest: each stack into an empty slot picked at random (from the roll's seed), leaving what's there. In a player's inventory only the hotbar and main inventory take loot. What finds no empty slot is left out. A game table is rolled where the inventory is, unless the context says otherwise.
---@param table LootTableId A loot table of the project's (`"treasure"`, `loot/treasure.json`; a bare id is the calling package's own), one a package exports (`"acme:treasure"`), or one of the game's by its id (`"minecraft:chests/simple_dungeon"`).
---@param inventory Inventory
---@param context? LootContext Who and what the roll is for: its player, tool, luck and seed. Leave it out for a roll with none of them.
---@return integer? # How many items didn't fit: 0 when all of it went in. `nil` once the inventory's gone, and then nothing is rolled.
function nf.loot.fill(table, inventory, context) end

--- Boss bars: bars across the top of players' screens.
---@class nf.bossbars
nf.bossbars = {}

--- Makes a boss bar, shown to no one yet (`bar:show_to(player)`). It belongs to this script and goes when it unloads. A key the options don't have is an error.
---@param options? BossBarOptions
---@return BossBar
function nf.bossbars.create(options) end

--- The project's scoreboard teams: prefixes and suffixes on names, name colours and glow colours, friendly fire, name tags and collision.
---@class nf.teams
nf.teams = {}

--- Makes a team on the server's scoreboard, with no members yet (`team:add_member`). It belongs to this script and goes when the script unloads, so make it in the script's body. A name that isn't letters, digits and `_`, `-`, `.` or `+`, a name one of the project's teams already has, or a key the options don't have is an error; to share a team between scripts, find it with `nf.teams.get` in the others.
---@param name string What the project calls it: `"red"`.
---@param options? TeamOptions
---@return Team
function nf.teams.create(name, options) end

--- One of the project's teams by name, or `nil` when it has none by that name.
---@param name string
---@return Team?
function nf.teams.get(name) end

--- Every team the project has now, in the order they were made. Teams made with `/team` or by other plugins aren't among them.
---@return Team[]
function nf.teams.all() end

--- Runs a function at a time of day, every day or week, or by a cron expression: real time, as opposed to ticks (`nf.after`, `nf.every`). Times are in the server owner's time zone (`schedules.time-zone` in `config.yml`, the server's own zone by default), not the player's. Daylight saving follows the zone's rules. A daily or weekly time the clocks skip runs the same distance after the gap begins (`"02:30"` runs at 03:30 on the day clocks jump from 02:00 to 03:00), and one that happens twice runs the first time only. A cron expression matches the clock as it reads: a time the clocks skip doesn't happen that day, one for a single time of day that happens twice (`"30 1 * * *"`) runs the first time only, and one that matches every half hour keeps matching through an hour that repeats. A schedule belongs to the script that made it and ends when that script unloads (a reload, the server stopping), so the script's body makes it again. It's checked once a tick, so a callback runs within a tick (50 ms) of its time. A run the server missed (it was off at the time) is skipped, unless the schedule has `catch_up = true` and an `id`: then it runs once, on start. What a schedule last ran is kept in the server's store by `id`, per package.
---@class nf.schedule
nf.schedule = {}

--- Calls `callback` every day at a time of day. Runs on the main thread, in the tick that finds the time has come, like an `nf.every` callback. One that errors is logged with its file and line; the schedule carries on.
---@param time string The time of day on a 24-hour clock, `"HH:mm"`: `"18:00"`, `"06:30"`.
---@param callback fun()
---@param options? ScheduleOptions A table: `id` (a name that survives restarts, which `catch_up` needs) and `catch_up` (run once on start if a run was missed).
---@return Schedule
function nf.schedule.daily(time, callback, options) end

--- Calls `callback` once a week, on a day at a time of day. Runs on the main thread, in the tick that finds the time has come, like an `nf.every` callback. One that errors is logged with its file and line; the schedule carries on.
---@param day string The weekday: `"mon"`, `"tue"`, `"wed"`, `"thu"`, `"fri"`, `"sat"` or `"sun"` (or written out, `"saturday"`), in any case.
---@param time string The time of day, as for `nf.schedule.daily`.
---@param callback fun()
---@param options? ScheduleOptions A table: `id` (a name that survives restarts, which `catch_up` needs) and `catch_up` (run once on start if a run was missed).
---@return Schedule
function nf.schedule.weekly(day, time, callback, options) end

--- Calls `callback` whenever a cron expression matches. Runs on the main thread, in the tick that finds the time has come, like an `nf.every` callback. One that errors is logged with its file and line; the schedule carries on.
---@param expression string Five fields, as Unix cron has them: minute, hour, day of month, month and day of week (`0` or `7` is Sunday; `MON`-`SUN` and `JAN`-`DEC` work), each `*`, a number, a list (`1,15`), a range (`9-17`) or a step (`*/10`, `0-30/5`). `"*/30 * * * *"` is every half hour; `"0 4 * * 1-5"` is 04:00 on weekdays. An expression that isn't valid is an error.
---@param callback fun()
---@param options? ScheduleOptions A table: `id` (a name that survives restarts, which `catch_up` needs) and `catch_up` (run once on start if a run was missed).
---@return Schedule
function nf.schedule.cron(expression, callback, options) end

--- Players' money, as the server's economy plugin keeps it, through [Vault](https://www.spigotmc.org/resources/vault.34315/): whichever economy is registered with it (EssentialsX, CMI, …) answers, so a project never names one. Needs Vault and an economy plugin. The plugin has to be on the server and enabled: a package that declares it (`requires`) while it isn't is a problem on the project's problem list until it is, and calling without it is an error. A plugin that is enabled after NetherForge counts from then on. The economy is looked up when it's asked, so one that registers late (after NetherForge starts) works from then on. Amounts are plain numbers in the economy's own currency; a player who is offline works as well as one who is online.
---@class nf.economy
nf.economy = {}

--- How much money the player has: `0` for someone the economy has no account for.
--- Needs `"requires": { "plugins": ["vault"] }` in `netherforge.json`: without it, calling it is an error.
---@param player Player Online or not.
---@return number
function nf.economy.balance(player) end

--- Gives the player money. Returns their new balance, or `nil` when the economy refused (it has no account for them, or can't hold that much). An amount that isn't positive and finite is an error.
--- Needs `"requires": { "plugins": ["vault"] }` in `netherforge.json`: without it, calling it is an error.
---@param player Player Online or not.
---@param amount number More than 0.
---@return number? # their balance afterwards
function nf.economy.deposit(player, amount) end

--- Takes money from the player. Returns their new balance, or `nil` when the economy refused, usually because they don't have that much (nothing is taken then). An amount that isn't positive and finite is an error.
--- Needs `"requires": { "plugins": ["vault"] }` in `netherforge.json`: without it, calling it is an error.
---@param player Player Online or not.
---@param amount number More than 0.
---@return number? # their balance afterwards
function nf.economy.withdraw(player, amount) end

--- Placeholders, through [PlaceholderAPI](https://www.spigotmc.org/resources/placeholderapi.6245/): fill in the `%player_name%`-style placeholders every plugin offers, and offer your own as `%<namespace>_<key>%` to every plugin that reads them (scoreboards, chat formats, holograms). Needs PlaceholderAPI. The plugin has to be on the server and enabled: a package that declares it (`requires`) while it isn't is a problem on the project's problem list until it is, and calling without it is an error. A plugin that is enabled after NetherForge counts from then on.
---@class nf.placeholders
nf.placeholders = {}

--- Replaces every placeholder in the text with its value, as PlaceholderAPI does: `"Hello %player_name%"` becomes `"Hello Alex"`. A placeholder nobody answers is left as written. Placeholders registered with `nf.placeholders.register` are answered too. A value is plain text, not MiniMessage: if it comes from players, `nf.text.escape` the result before sending it as MiniMessage.
--- Needs `"requires": { "plugins": ["placeholderapi"] }` in `netherforge.json`: without it, calling it is an error.
---@param text string Text with `%placeholders%` in it.
---@param player? Player Whom the placeholders are about (`%player_name%`); without one, only placeholders that don't need a player have a value.
---@return string
function nf.placeholders.parse(text, player) end

--- Makes `%<namespace>_<key>%` a placeholder for the whole server: PlaceholderAPI asks `callback` for the value whenever any plugin parses it. `callback` is called with the `key` (everything after the first `_`, so `%shop_price_apple%` is `"price_apple"`) and the player it's for, if there is one, and returns the text (or a number) to put there, or `nil` when it has no value for that key (the placeholder is then left as written). It runs on the main thread when the server asks from the main thread; PlaceholderAPI is also asked from other threads (async chat, scoreboard plugins), where scripts can't run: there the placeholder answers the value `callback` last gave for that same key and player, and `nil` before it ever has. The placeholder belongs to the script that registered it and goes when that script unloads (a reload, the server stopping). A namespace another script or plugin already uses is an error.
--- Needs `"requires": { "plugins": ["placeholderapi"] }` in `netherforge.json`: without it, calling it is an error.
---@param namespace string What comes before the first `_`: lowercase letters and digits, starting with a letter, at most 32 characters, like `"shop"`.
---@param callback fun(key: string, player: Player?): string|number|nil Gives the value. An error in it is logged with its file and line, and the placeholder is left as written for that request.
function nf.placeholders.register(namespace, callback) end

--- The project's particle effects (`particles/<id>/effect.json`): timelines of particles authored in the editor, played at a place by scripts.
---@class nf.particles
nf.particles = {}

--- Starts playing an effect at a place, from its first tick. It plays for its `duration` (or, looping, until it's stopped) and belongs to the script that played it: when that script unloads, the effect ends (reason `"unloaded"`). Only players within 32 blocks see it (128 for an emitter with `force`), and of those only `viewers` when given. An effect the project doesn't have, a key `options` doesn't take, or an option out of range is an error, and so is an effect whose file has errors and never had a good version (one that did plays its last good version). More than 1024 effects playing on the server at once is an error too: stop the ones you're done with.
---@param effect ParticleEffectId Which effect: its folder name under `particles/`.
---@param location_or_position Location|Vec3 Where it plays: a `Location`, whose `yaw` and `pitch` turn the effect (nil is 0), or a position in the calling centity's world (the server's first world, in a module or a menu or dialog script).
---@param options? ParticlePlayOptions Who sees it, how big it is, whether it loops, and what it follows.
---@return Effect? # The playing effect, or `nil` when the world isn't loaded.
function nf.particles.play(effect, location_or_position, options) end

--- The project's cutscenes (`cutscenes/<id>.json`): camera paths keyed in the editor, played for one player at a time.
---@class nf.cutscenes
nf.cutscenes = {}

--- Starts a cutscene for a player, from its first moment. While it plays, the player spectates a camera that follows the cutscene's path, and can't move or interact. Their game mode, position, flying state and anything riding are put back when it ends, however it ends (including when they leave, a reload or the server stopping); someone who was riding something is dismounted and isn't put back on it. It belongs to the script that played it: when that script unloads, it ends (reason `"unloaded"`). A player who is already watching one has it end (reason `"replaced"`) and the new one start without putting them back first, so cutscenes chain without a flash of the world they were in. A cutscene the project doesn't have, a key `options` doesn't take, or one whose file has errors and never had a good version (one that did plays its last good version) is an error. A player who is offline, or a dead one, gets `nil`.
---@param player Player Who watches.
---@param cutscene CutsceneId Which cutscene: its file name under `cutscenes/`, without `.json`.
---@param options? CutscenePlayOptions Where the path is, and whether the player may skip it.
---@return Cutscene? # The playing cutscene, or `nil` when the player can't watch one.
function nf.cutscenes.play(player, cutscene, options) end

--- Ends the cutscene a player is watching now, with reason `"stopped"`, and puts them back.
---@param player Player Whose cutscene to end.
---@return boolean # `false` when they were not watching one.
function nf.cutscenes.stop(player) end

--- The cutscene a player is watching now.
---@param player Player Who.
---@return Cutscene? # `nil` when they are not watching one.
function nf.cutscenes.current(player) end

--- Files in the one directory scripts may keep things in: the only state a script has that survives a restart.
---@class nf.files
nf.files = {}

--- A file or folder in the data directory, which survives restarts and reloads. A handle is a path, not an open file: nothing is read until you ask. Paths that would leave that directory (`..`, backslashes, names starting with a dot) give `nil` rather than an error. No argument is the directory itself.
---@param path? string Relative, with `/` between folders.
---@return File?
function nf.files.get(path) end

--- Helpers for the MiniMessage text players see: escaping what players typed, stripping tags, resource pack glyphs, and measuring.
---@class nf.text
nf.text = {}

--- Escapes text so MiniMessage shows it as typed rather than reading tags in it. Use it on anything a player wrote (a name they chose, a dialog answer, a chat line) before putting it into MiniMessage: otherwise they can add colours, or worse, `<click:run_command:...>`.
---@param text string Plain text.
---@return Text # MiniMessage that reads as exactly that text.
function nf.text.escape(text) end

--- MiniMessage with its tags taken out: the plain text a player would read, for logs and comparisons.
---@param text Text MiniMessage.
---@return string
function nf.text.strip(text) end

--- The character a resource pack glyph is drawn as, for building text by hand. In MiniMessage text the tag `<glyph:ui/coin>` is usually simpler: it works anywhere text does (names, lore, chat, titles, dialogs, text displays) and draws the picture in its own colours, while this character is tinted by the text colour around it like any other (wrap it in `<white>` to avoid that). `nil` while the project's resource packs have errors (nothing is built then); a glyph no resource pack has is an error.
---@param glyph GlyphRef `<pack>/<key>`, like `"ui/coin"`.
---@return string?
function nf.text.glyph(glyph) end

--- How wide a line of MiniMessage text draws in the game's default font, in pixels (GUI pixels: the font is 8 tall), for laying out menu titles and text displays. Tags draw nothing, except `<bold>` (a pixel more per character) and `<glyph:ui/coin>` (the glyph's own width); text with line breaks measures as its widest line. It reads the default font's widths from `fonts/default.json`, which the editor writes into the project from your Minecraft install. `nil` when it can't be measured exactly: that file is missing (open the project in the editor with Minecraft imported), or the text uses a character it doesn't cover.
---@param text Text MiniMessage.
---@return integer?
function nf.text.width(text) end

--- JSON text to and from Lua values.
---@class nf.json
nf.json = {}

--- A value as JSON text, written the way a `data()` table is saved: tables numbered from 1 become arrays, other tables objects (with string keys, in order), and the API's own values one-key objects that `nf.json.decode` turns back into them: `vec3(1, 2, 3)` is `{"$vec3":[1,2,3]}`, and `Location`s and `Player`, `Entity`, `Centity` and `World` handles have tags of their own. A key of yours that would read as a tag (`"$vec3"`) is written with one more `$`; any other key (`"$ref"`) is written as it is. A value JSON can't hold (a function, another kind of handle, a table that contains itself, a number key outside a list) is an error naming where it was (`value.callback: a function can't be written as JSON`).
---@param value any
---@return string
function nf.json.encode(value) end

--- JSON text as a Lua value: objects and arrays become tables, and what `nf.json.encode` tags becomes a `Vec3`, `Location` or handle again. `nil` for text that isn't JSON (and for `null`), so text from a player or a file can be tried without guarding.
---@param text string
---@return any
function nf.json.decode(text) end

--- Requests to web services (a Discord webhook, a status API). A package declares the hosts it talks to in `netherforge.json`'s `requires` (`"http": ["discord.com"]`, or `"*.discord.com"` for every name under it), and the server owner sees every host the project and its packages declare when it starts (`/nf requires`); a request to any other host is an error. Every request is held to the package whose code makes it. The server looks a name up once, checks the addresses it leads to and connects to exactly the address it checked: a loopback, private, link-local, shared (`100.64.0.0/10`), unique-local or IPv4-mapped address is refused even when its name is declared, unless the server owner turned on `http.allow-private-addresses` in `config.yml` (for a service on the same machine or network). Redirects (at most 5) are followed, and each one is held to the same rules, host declaration included. The server's proxy settings are never used. Only `http` and `https` URLs work. The server owner also limits requests in `config.yml`'s `http:` section: how large a request and a response may be, how long a request may take, and how many a package may make a minute.
---@class nf.http
nf.http = {}

--- Sends an HTTP request and gives the response. Any response is a result, whatever its status: a `404` or `500` is `response.status`, not an error, so check it. `nil, err` is for a request that couldn't be made or finished: a name that doesn't resolve, an address that isn't allowed, a refused connection, a timeout, a response over the size limit, too many requests this minute, a redirect to a host the package didn't declare, or more than 5 redirects. A mistake in the call itself (a URL that isn't `http` or `https`, a host the package didn't declare, a body over the limit, a header that can't be sent) is an error at the script's line. It works off the main thread, so its response arrives later: give `callback`, which is called with `response, err` once it's done, or call it inside a task (`nf.task`) without one, and it waits and returns `response, err`. Without a callback outside a task, it's an error. When it fails, `response` is `nil` and `err` says why.
--- Needs each host it calls in `"requires": { "http": [...] }` in `netherforge.json`: without it, calling it is an error.
---@param options HttpRequestOptions The request: its `url`, and optionally its `method`, `headers` and `body`.
---@param callback? fun(response: HttpResponse?, err: string?) Called on the main thread once it's done, as this script's code, with the response: its status, headers and body (or `nil` and why not). Not called if the script has stopped by then.
---@return HttpResponse? # Only without a callback, in a task: the response: its status, headers and body, or `nil`.
---@return string? # Why it failed, when it did; `nil` when it worked.
---@overload async fun(options: HttpRequestOptions): HttpResponse?, string?
---@overload fun(options: HttpRequestOptions, callback: fun(response: HttpResponse?, err: string?))
function nf.http.request(options, callback) end

--- Dates and durations as text, in the same unit as `nf.server.unix_time()`: milliseconds. For timers, use ticks (`nf.after`, `nf.wait`); this is for showing and reading clock times and lengths of time.
---@class nf.time
nf.time = {}

--- A moment as clock time, in English, written the way a pattern says.
---@param time integer Milliseconds since 1970-01-01 UTC, like `nf.server.unix_time()` returns.
---@param pattern string Letters stand for parts of the date: `yyyy` the year, `MM` the month as a number (`MMM` `Oct`, `MMMM` `October`), `dd` the day, `EEE` the weekday (`Sat`; `EEEE` `Saturday`), `HH` the hour from 00 to 23 (`hh` 01 to 12, with `a` for AM or PM), `mm` minutes, `ss` seconds, `XXX` the zone's offset (`+01:00`, or `Z`). Text in single quotes is kept as it is (`'at'`); punctuation and spaces needn't be. A letter that isn't a pattern letter is an error.
---@param options? TimeOptions
---@return string
function nf.time.format(time, pattern, options) end

--- Reads a clock time written the way a pattern says, as milliseconds since 1970-01-01 UTC. A pattern with no time of day reads as midnight; one with an offset (`XXX`) in it uses the offset it reads rather than `zone`. `nil` when the text doesn't match the pattern (it may come from a player); a pattern without a year, month and day, or with a letter that isn't a pattern letter, is an error.
---@param text string Like `"2026-10-03 18:00"`.
---@param pattern string As for `nf.time.format`.
---@param options? TimeOptions
---@return integer?
function nf.time.parse(text, pattern, options) end

--- A length of time as short text, largest unit first and units that are 0 left out: `"1h 2m 5s"`, `"3d 4h"`, `"45s"`. Less than a second is `"0s"`; a negative length starts with `-`.
---@param milliseconds integer How long.
---@return string
function nf.time.duration(milliseconds) end

--- Reads a length of time written as numbers with units, as milliseconds: `"1h30m"`, `"1h 30m"`, `"90s"`, `"2d"`, `"1.5h"`, `"250ms"`. The units are `d`, `h`, `m`, `s` and `ms`, in any order, each at most once; spaces between them are fine. `nil` for text that isn't a duration (it may come from a player).
---@param text string
---@return integer?
function nf.time.parse_duration(text) end

--- Randomness `math.random` doesn't give: generators of your own that repeat for a seed, smooth noise, and random ids.
---@class nf.random
nf.random = {}

--- A random generator of its own, apart from `math.random` and every other generator. Two made with the same seed give the same numbers in the same order, on every server, so a seed can stand for a whole dungeon layout or loot roll. One the script lets go of is collected like any table.
---@param seed? integer Leave it out for a generator seeded at random.
---@return Random
function nf.random.new(seed) end

--- Smooth 2D noise (simplex): a number from -1 to 1 that changes gradually as `x` and `z` do, the same every time for the same arguments. For terrain heights, patches of blocks, wobble over time.
---@param x number
---@param z number
---@param options? NoiseOptions
---@return number
function nf.random.noise2(x, z, options) end

--- Smooth 3D noise (simplex), like `noise2` with a third coordinate: caves, clouds, or 2D noise that drifts over time (`y` as the tick).
---@param x number
---@param y number
---@param z number
---@param options? NoiseOptions
---@return number
function nf.random.noise3(x, y, z, options) end

--- A new random UUID, like `"3f2b8c1e-7a4d-4e0b-9c55-0e8a6f1d2b7c"`: an id no one else will make.
---@return string
function nf.random.uuid() end

--- Small arithmetic `math` doesn't have, for animating by hand: blending, clamping, rescaling and easing.
---@class nf.math
nf.math = {}

--- The number `fraction` of the way from `from` to `to`: 0 is `from`, 1 is `to`, and outside that it carries on past either end. (`Vec3` has its own `lerp`.)
---@param from number
---@param to number
---@param fraction number
---@return number
function nf.math.lerp(from, to, fraction) end

--- `value`, kept from `min` to `max`. `min` greater than `max` is an error.
---@param value number
---@param min number
---@param max number
---@return number
function nf.math.clamp(value, min, max) end

--- Moves `value` from one range to another: `from_min` becomes `to_min`, `from_max` becomes `to_max`, and everything else in proportion (beyond the ends too; clamp it if it mustn't). A range from a number to itself is an error.
---@param value number
---@param from_min number
---@param from_max number
---@param to_min number
---@param to_max number
---@return number
function nf.math.remap(value, from_min, from_max, to_min, to_max) end

--- Reshapes a fraction from 0 to 1 by one of the curves animation keyframes use: `"linear"` as it is, `"step"` 0 until 1, `"ease_in"` starting slow, `"ease_out"` ending slow, `"ease_in_out"` both. Every curve takes 0 to 0 and 1 to 1; a fraction outside 0 to 1 is clamped first.
---@param easing "linear"|"step"|"ease_in"|"ease_out"|"ease_in_out" Which curve.
---@param fraction number How far along, 0 to 1.
---@return number
function nf.math.ease(easing, fraction) end

--- What a test file runs on. A project's tests are the `*_test.lua` files anywhere in it (`tests/shop_test.lua`), run by `netherforge test` (or the editor's Run tests) on a fake server: no Minecraft, no network, a world that holds only what the test puts in it. A test file runs like a module (it can `require` the project's modules, and files beside it) and registers its tests with `nf.test.case`; each test runs alone, on a fresh server with the project loaded and its scripts started, so one can never leave something behind for the next. The file's own code runs once for every test, before it, so it's where to `require` and build what the tests share. Nothing here exists on a real server: `nf.test` is `nil` there.
---@class nf.test
nf.test = {}

--- Registers a test. It passes when `callback` returns, and fails at the line where an `assert` or `error` in it (or in something it calls) went wrong. A script of the project that errors while the test runs fails it too, naming that script and line. Two tests in one file can't have the same name.
---@param name string What the test checks, as a sentence: `"greets a new player"`.
---@param callback fun() The test. It runs synchronously: `nf.test.advance` is how time passes in it.
function nf.test.case(name, callback) end

--- Runs the server forward `ticks` ticks (20 are a second), whole ticks at a time: timers fall due, `tick` handlers run, centities tick and mobs think, exactly as on a server, and everything scripts started on other threads finishes first. When it returns, the last tick is done. Script errors during it are collected and fail the test when it ends.
---@param ticks integer At least 1.
function nf.test.advance(ticks) end

--- Joins a fake player and returns them: the server raises the join events (`player_join`) as it does for a real one, and the player can then be sent messages, given items, moved and clicked at like any other `Player`. Names are unique: a name already joined gives that player.
---@param name string Their name, 3 to 16 letters, digits and `_`: `"Alex"`.
---@return Player
function nf.test.player(name) end

--- Raises a server-wide event as the server would, to the scripts: whoever listens to `event` with `nf.on` hears it, and, for an event the server raises about a player, an entity or a world, the handlers on that handle (`player:on("chat")`) hear it first, in the order the server uses. Each can `stop()` or `cancel()` it or set its writable fields. The few events the runtime stages itself (a death, damage, a click on a centity) reach `nf.on` handlers only. `payload` is what the event carries, the same table its handlers read (`event.player`, `event.message`): a field the event does not have is an error naming the fields. What the server would then do with a cancelled event is not simulated: read the result.
---@param event "tick"|"player_join"|"player_quit"|"player_chat"|"player_interact"|"player_interact_entity"|"player_move"|"player_teleport"|"player_change_world"|"player_death"|"player_respawn"|"player_drop_item"|"player_pickup_item"|"player_use_item"|"player_consume_item"|"player_swap_hands"|"player_sneak"|"player_command"|"block_break"|"block_place"|"entity_damage"|"entity_death"|"entity_spawn"|"player_input"|"player_change_slot"|"player_swing"|"player_sprint"|"player_fly"|"player_jump"|"player_change_game_mode"|"player_change_food"|"player_gain_experience"|"player_change_level"|"player_kick"|"player_complete_advancement"|"player_stop_using_item"|"player_break_item"|"entity_heal"|"projectile_launch"|"projectile_hit"|"entity_shoot_bow"|"entity_target"|"entity_explode"|"block_explode"|"entity_combust"|"entity_change_effect"|"block_ignite"|"block_burn"|"block_spread"|"block_flow"|"block_grow"|"block_fade"|"block_form"|"leaves_decay"|"piston_extend"|"piston_retract"|"block_redstone"|"sign_change"|"block_start_break"|"block_drop_item"|"weather_change"|"thunder_change"|"lightning_strike"|"chunk_load"|"chunk_generated"|"chunk_unload"|"world_load"|"world_unload"|"entity_mount"|"entity_dismount"|"vehicle_move"|"vehicle_damage"|"vehicle_destroy"|"player_open_inventory"|"player_close_inventory"|"player_craft"|"player_prepare_craft"|"player_prepare_anvil"|"player_prepare_smithing"|"player_prepare_grindstone"|"player_enchant_item"|"furnace_smelt"|"server_list_ping"|"centity_click"|"centity_natural_spawn"|"menu_open"|"menu_close"|"menu_click"|"dialog_press"|"setting_changed" The event's name, as `nf.on` takes it.
---@param payload? table The payload: its fields by name. Leave it out for an event that has none.
---@return RaisedEvent # Whether a handler cancelled it, and the event as the handlers left it.
function nf.test.raise(event, payload) end

--- One spawned centity: `this` in the centity's script, and what `nf.centities.spawn`, `nf.centities.get` and `nf.centities.all` return. A handle stays valid as long as the instance exists; after it's removed its methods return `nil` or `false` rather than erroring.
--- A `data()` table, `Item.data` and `nf.json` can keep one: it is saved as what identifies it and comes back as a handle.
---@class Centity
local Centity = {}

--- The instance's id, unique and stable across restarts. Keep this rather than the handle if you need to find it later.
---@return string
function Centity:id() end

--- Which centity it is: its folder name under `centities/`. `nil` once removed.
---@return string?
function Centity:kind() end

--- Whether the instance is still in the world.
---@return boolean
function Centity:exists() end

--- Whether it appeared by itself, from its `spawning` rules in `centity.json`, and is still temporary: it isn't saved, it's removed when no player is near, and it counts against its cap. `false` for one a script spawned, one `keep` has kept, and once it's removed.
---@return boolean
function Centity:is_natural() end

--- Makes a natural centity a normal one: it is saved from now on (it comes back after a restart), is never removed for being far from players, and no longer counts against its cap. Nothing happens to one that is already normal.
---@return boolean # Whether it was natural: `false` for one that was already normal, and once it is removed.
function Centity:keep() end

--- One of its nodes by name, or `nil` if it has none by that name.
---@param name NodeName The node's name in `centity.json`.
---@return Node?
function Centity:node(name) end

--- Every node, parents before their children.
---@return Node[]
function Centity:nodes() end

--- Its root nodes (those with no parent), in the file's order. Every other node hangs from one of them.
---@return Node[]
function Centity:roots() end

--- Adds a root node: one with no parent, whose translation is in the centity's own space, as if `centity.json` had one more. A node a script adds is **runtime state**, like a rest pose a script sets: it takes part in everything a declared node does (its transform follows its parent, a display and an interaction entity are spawned for it and sent to players, a hitbox takes clicks and `click` bubbles up through its parents, a `physics` body is simulated and collides, `raycast` hits it) except what a file names: an animation clip can't drive it, because clips are written against the file's nodes, though it follows its animated parent. It is **not saved**: after a restart, and when its centity is reloaded, it's gone, and the script's body, which runs again, is where to add it. Only nodes a script added can be removed; the nodes `centity.json` declares can't be. A name that's already a node's, or isn't a usable node name (letters, digits, `_` and `-`, at most 64 characters), is an error, and so is a definition the file's rules would refuse. Returns `nil` once the centity is removed.
---@param name string A new name, unique among the centity's nodes.
---@param definition? table A table in the shape of one node of `centity.json`, minus its `parent` (it hangs from the node you call this on), in Lua spelling: `transform` (`{ translation?, rotation?, scale? }`, each a `Vec3`: where it starts, as a rest pose a script can change afterwards), `display` (`{ type = "block", block = "minecraft:stone" }`, `{ type = "item", item = "minecraft:diamond", item_transform? }` or `{ type = "text", text = "<red>Hi", billboard?, alignment?, background?, line_width?, see_through?, shadow? }`), `hitbox` (`{ boxes?, shape?, raycast?, fitted_to? }`, a box being `{ min = Vec3, max = Vec3 }`) and `physics` (`{ gravity?, mass?, bounciness?, friction?, drag?, angular_drag?, max_speed?, max_spin?, collider?, shape?, rotates?, sleeps?, blocks?, entities? }`). Every field is optional: a node with none is a pivot to hang others from. Held to the same rules as `centity.json`, by the same validator: a key it does not take, a value of the wrong type, or a block, item or hitbox the file would be refused for is an error.
---@return Node?
function Centity:add_node(name, definition) end

--- Where it stands, in which world, and which way it faces, or `nil` once it's removed. The location's `yaw` is the centity's (`centity:yaw()`); its `pitch` is `nil`, because a centity only turns about the vertical.
---@return Location?
function Centity:location() end

--- Where it stands in its world: the anchor every node is placed relative to. `nil` once it's removed.
---@return Vec3?
function Centity:position() end

--- The world it's in, or `nil` once it's removed.
---@return World?
function Centity:world() end

--- Which way it faces, in degrees, or `nil` once it's removed. Minecraft's convention: 0 faces south (+z), 90 west (−x), 180 north, −90 east, always given back between −180 and 180. Its +Z axis points that way, and every node is turned with it about its position.
---@return number?
function Centity:yaw() end

--- Turns the whole centity to face a yaw, about the vertical through its position: every node, its hitboxes and its physics bodies turn with it (a moving body keeps going, turned by as much). The turn is saved. Pitch and roll aren't a centity's: rotate a root node for those. Does nothing once it's removed.
---@param degrees number Minecraft's convention (0 south, 90 west); any number, wrapped into −180 to 180.
function Centity:set_yaw(degrees) end

--- Turns it to face a world position, as `set_yaw` does: only about the vertical, so how far above or below the point is makes no difference. A point straight above or below its position leaves it as it is.
---@param point Vec3 A position in the world.
---@return boolean # `false` once it's removed.
function Centity:look_at(point) end

--- A world position in the centity's own space: what a root node's translation would be to stand there. The centity's own space has its origin at the centity's position (`centity:position()`) and is turned by its yaw (`centity:yaw()`): its +Z is the way the centity faces, its Y is the world's. It's the space a root node's translation is in. `nil` once it's removed.
---@param point Vec3 A position in the world.
---@return Vec3?
function Centity:to_local(point) end

--- A position in the centity's own space as a world position. The centity's own space has its origin at the centity's position (`centity:position()`) and is turned by its yaw (`centity:yaw()`): its +Z is the way the centity faces, its Y is the world's. It's the space a root node's translation is in. `nil` once it's removed.
---@param point Vec3 A position in the centity's space.
---@return Vec3?
function Centity:to_world(point) end

--- Moves the whole centity: to a `Location` (turning to face its `yaw`, when it has one), or to a position in its current world. It keeps its own facing when the destination has none; a location's `pitch` is ignored.
---@param location_or_position Location|Vec3 Where its position (the anchor) goes.
---@return boolean # Whether it moved: `false` once it's removed, or for a world that doesn't exist.
function Centity:teleport(location_or_position) end

--- Makes the whole centity walk somewhere, finding its way round the blocks in the way: to a place in its own world, or after an entity or another centity, looking for a new path to wherever that is every 10 ticks. When it stops, its `path_end` handlers hear whether it got there. A walk goes by the centity's **feet**: the middle of the bottom of its hitboxes as they're placed when it sets off (its position, for a centity without any). The way is found over the blocks' collision shapes, sized to its hitboxes (other centities aren't in the way), and walked a little each tick, before physics and scripts: it steps up and drops down as the options allow, falls when nothing holds it up, and finds another way when a block appears across its path. With `fly = true` it flies instead, through the air in any direction. A new `move_to`, `stop_pathing` and `teleport` end the walk it was on, and so does a reload of its centity (its script's body can set off again). Walks aren't saved, and a centity whose chunk unloads waits where it is until it loads again. Its physics bodies are carried along with it.
---@param target Location|Vec3|Entity|Centity Where its feet go: a position in its world, a `Location` (in its world: another world is `false`), or an entity or centity to go after.
---@param options? CentityPathOptions
---@return boolean # Whether it found a way and set off, which may only take it as close as it can get: `false` when it can't get any closer than it is (walking, it needs ground under it, within 64 blocks), for a target further away than `range`, in another world or gone, or once it's removed.
function Centity:move_to(target, options) end

--- Stops it where it is, ending its walk without a `path_end`. One that was falling stays in the air: a centity only falls while it walks.
---@return boolean # Whether it was walking: `false` when it wasn't, or once it's removed.
function Centity:stop_pathing() end

--- Whether it's walking somewhere now (`move_to`). `false` once it's removed.
---@return boolean
function Centity:has_path() end

--- Where the path it's walking ends, as where its feet will be: where it was sent, or as close as it can get when there's no way all the way there. A walk goes by the centity's **feet**: the middle of the bottom of its hitboxes as they're placed when it sets off (its position, for a centity without any). `nil` when it isn't walking, or once it's removed.
---@return Vec3?
function Centity:path_target() end

--- This instance's own saved table, kept with the instance across restarts and removed with it. Every script that holds the instance (its own, a module's) gets the same table. It may hold strings, numbers, booleans, tables of them (keyed by strings, or a list numbered from 1), and the API's own values: `Vec3`s, `Location`s, `Item` tables, and `Player`, `Entity`, `Centity` and `World` handles, which come back as the same types (a handle to something gone answers `nil` or `false`, as handles do). Anything else (a function, a `Menu` window, a `Subscription`) is skipped when it's saved, with a line in the log naming where it was (`data.inventory[3]: a function can't be saved`). The table is live: change it and the change is saved, at the next autosave (every five minutes), when the project reloads and when the server stops. It stays in memory across hot reloads rather than being read again, so a script that reloads finds it as it left it. A table that would take more than 1 MiB isn't saved (the last save stands), and the log says so. `nil` once it's removed.
---@return table?
function Centity:data() end

--- Takes it out of the world for good. Its `remove` handlers hear it, then its script unloads.
function Centity:remove() end

--- Starts one of its animations, from the beginning (or `from_tick`), restarting it if it's already playing. Its `animation_start` handlers hear it before this returns. An animation it doesn't have is an error.
---@param name AnimationName The animation's name in `centity.json`.
---@param options? AnimationOptions How to play it this time. A key it doesn't take is an error.
---@return boolean # `false` once it's removed.
function Centity:play_animation(name, options) end

--- Stops one animation, or all of them. The nodes it was moving go back to rest.
---@param name? AnimationName Leave out to stop everything.
function Centity:stop_animation(name) end

--- Holds a playing animation where it is: its nodes keep the pose it had reached, until `resume_animation`. An animation it doesn't have is an error.
---@param name AnimationName
---@return boolean # `false` when it isn't playing.
function Centity:pause_animation(name) end

--- Carries on with a paused animation from where it stopped. An animation it doesn't have is an error.
---@param name AnimationName
---@return boolean # `false` when it isn't playing.
function Centity:resume_animation(name) end

--- Whether an animation is playing (a paused one still is, and so is a `hold` animation that reached its end).
---@param name AnimationName
---@return boolean
function Centity:is_animation_playing(name) end

--- The animations playing now, oldest first.
---@return string[]
function Centity:playing_animations() end

--- Every animation the centity has.
---@return string[]
function Centity:animations() end

--- How far into its clip a playing animation is, in ticks (20 a second): within the clip's length, so a looping one starts again from 0 each time round. Not always whole, at a speed other than 1. `nil` when it isn't playing; an animation the centity doesn't have is an error.
---@param name AnimationName
---@return number?
function Centity:animation_tick(name) end

--- Jumps a playing animation to a point in its clip, and poses the nodes there at once. Past the end, a looping clip wraps round, and any other ends on its next tick. An animation the centity doesn't have is an error.
---@param name AnimationName
---@param tick number Ticks from the start of the clip, at least 0.
---@return boolean # `false` when it isn't playing.
function Centity:seek_animation(name, tick) end

--- How fast a playing animation runs: 1 is as authored, 2 twice as fast. `nil` when it isn't playing; an animation the centity doesn't have is an error.
---@param name AnimationName
---@return number?
function Centity:animation_speed(name) end

--- Changes how fast a playing animation runs, from where it is now. An animation the centity doesn't have is an error.
---@param name AnimationName
---@param multiplier number 1 is as authored, 0.5 half speed, 0 holds it still (like pausing). Not negative.
---@return boolean # `false` when it isn't playing.
function Centity:set_animation_speed(name, multiplier) end

--- Whether every node that draws something glows (`set_glowing`). `false` once it's removed, or when it draws nothing.
---@return boolean
function Centity:is_glowing() end

--- Outlines every node that draws something, seen through walls, the way the Glowing effect outlines a mob. The colour is `set_glow_color`'s. The same as `set_glowing` on each node.
---@param glowing boolean
---@return boolean # `false` once it's removed.
function Centity:set_glowing(glowing) end

--- The colour its block and item displays glow in, as `#RRGGBB`: `nil` when they don't share one, when none was set (they glow white), or once it's removed.
---@return string?
function Centity:glow_color() end

--- Sets the colour every block and item display node glows in (the same as `set_glow_color` on each). Minecraft draws no glow colour on text displays, so they're left as they are. It doesn't turn the glow on: `set_glowing` does.
---@param color? string `#RRGGBB`. Leave it out for the default, white. Anything else is an error.
---@return boolean # `false` once it's removed, or when it has no block or item display.
function Centity:set_glow_color(color) end

--- Hides the whole centity from one player: they don't see its nodes and can't click its hitboxes. Everyone else still does. It stays hidden from them when they leave and come back, and when its chunk unloads and loads again, until `show_to`, a server restart or the centity's removal. Hiding it from someone offline counts for when they join.
---@param player Player
---@return boolean # `false` once it's removed.
function Centity:hide_from(player) end

--- Shows it to a player it was hidden from.
---@param player Player
---@return boolean # `false` once it's removed.
function Centity:show_to(player) end

--- Whether `hide_from` hid it from a player.
---@param player Player
---@return boolean
function Centity:is_hidden_from(player) end

--- How far away its displays are drawn, in blocks: 64 unless `set_view_range` changed it. `nil` once it's removed.
---@return number?
function Centity:view_range() end

--- How far away its displays are drawn, in blocks. Each player's own entity distance setting scales it further, so it's the distance at 100%. Not saved, like everything a script sets on a node.
---@param blocks number At least 0.
---@return boolean # `false` once it's removed.
function Centity:set_view_range(blocks) end

--- Calls `handler` every time `event` happens to this, until the subscription is cancelled or its script unloads, or this goes away. A handler lives as long as both the script that registered it and the handle it's on. Handlers run in the order they were registered, each with the event object; what one returns means nothing. A handler that errors is logged and the rest still run; after 20 errors in a row it's cancelled. An event this class doesn't have is an error, so a typo is caught where it was made.
---@param event Centity.Event A built-in event's name, or a custom event's: any name with a `:` in it, like `"shop:purchased"`.
---@param handler fun(event: CustomEvent) Called with the event: its payload, plus `stop()`, `cancel()` and the rest of `Event`.
---@param options? EventOptions How to listen: `{ every = n }` for `tick`.
---@return Subscription # Call `:cancel()` on it to stop listening.
---@overload fun(self: Centity, event: "spawn", handler: fun(event: CentityEvent)): Subscription
---@overload fun(self: Centity, event: "remove", handler: fun(event: CentityEvent)): Subscription
---@overload fun(self: Centity, event: "tick", handler: fun(event: TickEvent), options?: EventOptions): Subscription
---@overload fun(self: Centity, event: "click", handler: fun(event: ClickEvent)): Subscription
---@overload fun(self: Centity, event: "animation_start", handler: fun(event: AnimationEvent)): Subscription
---@overload fun(self: Centity, event: "animation_end", handler: fun(event: AnimationEvent)): Subscription
---@overload fun(self: Centity, event: "chunk_load", handler: fun(event: CentityEvent)): Subscription
---@overload fun(self: Centity, event: "chunk_unload", handler: fun(event: CentityEvent)): Subscription
---@overload fun(self: Centity, event: "path_end", handler: fun(event: CentityPathEndEvent)): Subscription
function Centity:on(event, handler, options) end

--- Like `centity:on`, but the handler runs only the next time the event happens, then stops listening.
---@param event Centity.Event A built-in event's name, or a custom event's: any name with a `:` in it, like `"shop:purchased"`.
---@param handler fun(event: CustomEvent) Called with the event.
---@return Subscription # Call `:cancel()` on it to stop waiting before then.
---@overload fun(self: Centity, event: "spawn", handler: fun(event: CentityEvent)): Subscription
---@overload fun(self: Centity, event: "remove", handler: fun(event: CentityEvent)): Subscription
---@overload fun(self: Centity, event: "tick", handler: fun(event: TickEvent)): Subscription
---@overload fun(self: Centity, event: "click", handler: fun(event: ClickEvent)): Subscription
---@overload fun(self: Centity, event: "animation_start", handler: fun(event: AnimationEvent)): Subscription
---@overload fun(self: Centity, event: "animation_end", handler: fun(event: AnimationEvent)): Subscription
---@overload fun(self: Centity, event: "chunk_load", handler: fun(event: CentityEvent)): Subscription
---@overload fun(self: Centity, event: "chunk_unload", handler: fun(event: CentityEvent)): Subscription
---@overload fun(self: Centity, event: "path_end", handler: fun(event: CentityPathEndEvent)): Subscription
function Centity:once(event, handler) end

--- Raises a custom event on this centity: every `centity:on(event)` handler hears it now, in the order they were registered, before this returns. The payload is handed to them as it is, not copied, so a field a handler assigns is there for the caller to read afterwards. A name without a `:` is an error: built-in events are the server's to raise.
---@param event string A custom event's name, with a `:`: `"shop:purchased"`.
---@param payload? table The event's fields. Leave it out for none.
---@return boolean # Whether a handler cancelled it (`event:cancel()`), so a custom event can be vetoed.
function Centity:emit(event, payload) end

--- One node of one centity instance. Its transform (`translation`, `rotation`, `scale`) is in its parent's space, in the same units as `centity.json`: blocks, degrees (X then Y then Z) and scale factors. A root node's parent space is the centity's own: origin at the centity's position, turned by its yaw. `world_position`, `to_world`, `to_local` and `world_direction` convert to and from the world; physics (velocities, impulses and the point an impulse lands at) is always in world space. What a script sets is the node's rest pose; an animation driving the same channel overrides it while it plays. Nothing a script sets is saved: the script's body, which runs every time it starts, is where to set it up. A node with `physics` is moved by it: each tick physics writes its translation and rotation, after animations and before scripts, so a script's write has the last word (setting the rotation re-seeds the body's orientation).
---@class Node
local Node = {}

--- The node's name.
---@return string
function Node:name() end

--- The instance it belongs to.
---@return Centity
function Node:centity() end

--- Its parent node, or `nil` for a root.
---@return Node?
function Node:parent() end

--- Its child nodes, by name.
---@return Node[]
function Node:children() end

--- Adds a node that hangs from this one, so it moves, turns and scales with it. A node a script adds is **runtime state**, like a rest pose a script sets: it takes part in everything a declared node does (its transform follows its parent, a display and an interaction entity are spawned for it and sent to players, a hitbox takes clicks and `click` bubbles up through its parents, a `physics` body is simulated and collides, `raycast` hits it) except what a file names: an animation clip can't drive it, because clips are written against the file's nodes, though it follows its animated parent. It is **not saved**: after a restart, and when its centity is reloaded, it's gone, and the script's body, which runs again, is where to add it. Only nodes a script added can be removed; the nodes `centity.json` declares can't be. The name is unique among the centity's nodes, not only this node's children. A name that's taken, or isn't a usable node name, is an error, and so is a definition the file's rules would refuse. Returns `nil` once its centity or this node is gone.
---@param name string A new name, unique among the centity's nodes.
---@param definition? table A table in the shape of one node of `centity.json`, minus its `parent` (it hangs from the node you call this on), in Lua spelling: `transform` (`{ translation?, rotation?, scale? }`, each a `Vec3`: where it starts, as a rest pose a script can change afterwards), `display` (`{ type = "block", block = "minecraft:stone" }`, `{ type = "item", item = "minecraft:diamond", item_transform? }` or `{ type = "text", text = "<red>Hi", billboard?, alignment?, background?, line_width?, see_through?, shadow? }`), `hitbox` (`{ boxes?, shape?, raycast?, fitted_to? }`, a box being `{ min = Vec3, max = Vec3 }`) and `physics` (`{ gravity?, mass?, bounciness?, friction?, drag?, angular_drag?, max_speed?, max_spin?, collider?, shape?, rotates?, sleeps?, blocks?, entities? }`). Every field is optional: a node with none is a pivot to hang others from. Held to the same rules as `centity.json`, by the same validator: a key it does not take, a value of the wrong type, or a block, item or hitbox the file would be refused for is an error.
---@return Node?
function Node:add_child(name, definition) end

--- Removes this node and every node below it, with their displays and hitboxes (players see them go at the end of the tick), their physics bodies and every handler on them; the handles answer `nil` and `false` after that. Only a node a script added can be removed: removing one `centity.json` declares is an error, since animations, physics and other scripts are written against it. Returns whether it was removed: `false` if it is already gone, or its centity is.
---@return boolean
function Node:remove() end

--- Its offset from its parent (for a root node, from the centity's position), as shown now: animation and physics included. `nil` once its centity is removed.
---@return Vec3?
function Node:translation() end

--- Its rotation relative to its parent, in degrees, applied X then Y then Z. `nil` once its centity is removed.
---@return Vec3?
function Node:rotation() end

--- Its scale on each of its own axes. `nil` once its centity is removed.
---@return Vec3?
function Node:scale() end

--- Moves it relative to its parent (a root node, relative to the centity's position).
---@param translation Vec3 In its parent's space, in blocks.
function Node:set_translation(translation) end

--- Turns it relative to its parent. Displays ease to the new pose over a tick, so setting it every tick looks smooth.
---@param rotation Vec3 Degrees, applied X then Y then Z.
function Node:set_rotation(rotation) end

--- Scales it along its own axes.
---@param scale Vec3 A factor per axis: `vec3.one` is its natural size.
function Node:set_scale(scale) end

--- Where its origin is in the world, as shown now: animation and physics included. `nil` once its centity is removed.
---@return Vec3?
function Node:world_position() end

--- Its rotation relative to the world, in degrees applied X then Y then Z, as shown now: its own and every ancestor's together. Scale doesn't count. `nil` once its centity is removed.
---@return Vec3?
function Node:world_rotation() end

--- A world position in this node's own space: the space its children's translations are in, which moves, turns and scales with it, as shown now. `nil` once its centity is removed, or while it's scaled to nothing on some axis.
---@param point Vec3 A position in the world.
---@return Vec3?
function Node:to_local(point) end

--- A position in this node's own space (where a child at that translation would be) as a world position, as shown now. `nil` once its centity is removed.
---@param point Vec3 A position in the node's space.
---@return Vec3?
function Node:to_world(point) end

--- A direction in this node's own space as a world direction: turned with the node, but not moved or scaled. `node:world_direction(vec3.south)` is where its +Z points. `nil` once its centity is removed.
---@param direction Vec3 A direction in the node's space.
---@return Vec3?
function Node:world_direction(direction) end

--- Turns it so its +Z axis points at a world position, keeping its X axis level (no roll), by setting its rotation as `set_rotation` does. Exact unless an ancestor is scaled unevenly. Pointing at its own origin leaves it as it is.
---@param point Vec3 A position in the world.
---@return boolean # `false` once its centity is removed, or while its parent is scaled to nothing.
function Node:look_at(point) end

--- Turns it further, by an angle about an axis in its parent's space, by the right-hand rule (as `Vec3:rotated` does), by setting its rotation as `set_rotation` does.
---@param axis Vec3 In its parent's space; any length but zero.
---@param degrees number
---@return boolean # `false` once its centity is removed.
function Node:rotate(axis, degrees) end

--- Whether it's set visible (it can still be hidden by a hidden parent).
---@return boolean
function Node:is_visible() end

--- Shows or hides it and everything under it. A hidden node can't be clicked.
---@param visible boolean
function Node:set_visible(visible) end

--- Whether its hitbox takes clicks (`set_clickable`). `true` for a node without one, which has nothing to click anyway.
---@return boolean
function Node:is_clickable() end

--- Turns its hitbox off or back on without hiding the node: an unclickable node is still drawn, but clicks pass through it as if it had no hitbox. Its children keep their own. Physics still treats the hitbox as solid. Not saved.
---@param clickable boolean
function Node:set_clickable(clickable) end

--- What it draws: `"block"`, `"item"` or `"text"`, or `nil` for a node that draws nothing (a pivot, a hitbox alone) or once its centity is removed.
---@return "block"|"item"|"text"?
function Node:display_kind() end

--- The block state a block display shows, like `"minecraft:oak_slab[type=top]"`, or `nil` when the node isn't a block display.
---@return string?
function Node:display_block() end

--- Changes the block a block display shows. A hitbox with `shape: "collision"` follows the new block's shape.
---@param block string A block state, like `"minecraft:oak_slab[type=top]"`.
---@return boolean # `false` when the node isn't a block display or the server has no such block state.
function Node:set_display_block(block) end

--- What a text display says, as MiniMessage, or `nil` when the node isn't a text display.
---@return Text?
function Node:display_text() end

--- Changes what a text display says.
---@param text Text MiniMessage.
---@return boolean # `false` when the node isn't a text display.
function Node:set_display_text(text) end

--- The item an item display shows, or `nil` when the node isn't an item display.
---@return Item?
function Node:display_item() end

--- Changes the item an item display shows: any `Item`, so a name, `item_model` or `glint` shows as it would in a hand. Its `count` makes no difference. An item table with a misspelled field, or an item the server doesn't have, is an error.
---@param item Item
---@return boolean # `false` when the node isn't an item display.
function Node:set_display_item(item) end

--- How a text display's text is drawn, every field filled in (`background` is `nil` for Minecraft's own grey), or `nil` when the node isn't a text display. Change a field and hand it to `set_display_text_style`.
---@return TextStyle?
function Node:display_text_style() end

--- Changes how a text display's text is drawn. The whole style is replaced: a field left out goes back to its default, so to change one thing, read `display_text_style()`, change it and write it back. A bad value (an alignment that isn't one, a background that isn't `#AARRGGBB`) is an error.
---@param style TextStyle
---@return boolean # `false` when the node isn't a text display.
function Node:set_display_text_style(style) end

--- Whether its display glows (`set_glowing`). `false` for a node that draws nothing.
---@return boolean
function Node:is_glowing() end

--- Outlines its display, seen through walls, the way the Glowing effect outlines a mob. Block and item displays glow in `set_glow_color`'s colour (white by default). Not saved.
---@param glowing boolean
---@return boolean # `false` for a node that draws nothing.
function Node:set_glowing(glowing) end

--- The colour its block or item display glows in, as `#RRGGBB`, or `nil` for the default (white), for a text display, or for a node that draws nothing.
---@return string?
function Node:glow_color() end

--- Sets the colour a block or item display glows in. Minecraft draws no glow colour on a text display, so there it does nothing and returns `false`. It doesn't turn the glow on: `set_glowing` does.
---@param color? string `#RRGGBB`. Leave it out for the default, white. Anything else is an error.
---@return boolean # `false` for a text display, or a node that draws nothing.
function Node:set_glow_color(color) end

--- The light its display is drawn with, when `set_brightness` fixed it; `nil` when it's lit by where it stands (the default), or for a node that draws nothing.
---@return Brightness?
function Node:brightness() end

--- Draws its display as if lit by fixed light levels, whatever the light where it stands: `{ block_light = 15, sky_light = 15 }` is full brightness in a dark cave. Leave it out (or `nil`) to go back to the light around it. A level outside 0 to 15, or a missing one, is an error.
---@param brightness? Brightness Both levels, 0 to 15.
---@return boolean # `false` for a node that draws nothing.
function Node:set_brightness(brightness) end

--- Which way its display turns to face whoever looks at it: `"fixed"` (it doesn't), `"vertical"` (about the vertical, like a sign on a post), `"horizontal"` or `"center"` (all the way). `nil` for a node that draws nothing.
---@return "fixed"|"vertical"|"horizontal"|"center"?
function Node:billboard() end

--- Makes its display turn to face each viewer (or stop). A turning display ignores its rotation (its parents' included) about the axes it turns on. A text display starts as its `billboard` in `centity.json` says (`"center"` by default), a block or item display `"fixed"`. Anything else is an error.
---@param billboard "fixed"|"vertical"|"horizontal"|"center"
---@return boolean # `false` for a node that draws nothing.
function Node:set_billboard(billboard) end

--- How many ticks its display takes to ease to a new pose: 1 unless `set_interpolation_ticks` changed it. `nil` once its centity is removed.
---@return integer?
function Node:interpolation_ticks() end

--- How many ticks its display takes to ease to each new pose (its translation, rotation and scale, and its parents'). The client does the easing, so a pose set once with `set_interpolation_ticks(20)` glides there over a second without the script doing anything more. When the whole centity moves (`teleport`, physics carrying it along), its display glides there over as many ticks, up to 59. 0 jumps. Not saved.
---@param ticks integer At least 0.
---@return boolean # `false` once its centity is removed.
function Node:set_interpolation_ticks(ticks) end

--- How fast a physics body is moving, in blocks per second along the world's axes: `vec3.zero` for a node without `physics`, `nil` once its centity is removed.
---@return Vec3?
function Node:velocity() end

--- Replaces a physics body's velocity, and wakes it.
---@param velocity Vec3 Blocks per second, along the world's axes.
---@return boolean # `false` for a node without `physics`.
function Node:set_velocity(velocity) end

--- Adds to a physics body's velocity whatever it weighs ("go this much faster", which is what a jump means), and wakes it.
---@param velocity Vec3 Blocks per second, along the world's axes.
---@return boolean # `false` for a node without `physics`.
function Node:add_velocity(velocity) end

--- How fast a physics body is turning about each world axis, in degrees per second: `vec3.zero` for a node without `physics`, `nil` once its centity is removed.
---@return Vec3?
function Node:angular_velocity() end

--- Replaces a physics body's angular velocity, and wakes it.
---@param angular_velocity Vec3 Degrees per second about each world axis.
---@return boolean # `false` for a node without `physics`.
function Node:set_angular_velocity(angular_velocity) end

--- A physics body's mass: what its `physics` says, or its collider's volume in blocks. 0 for a node without `physics`. `speed * node:mass()` is the impulse that sends it off at `speed`.
---@return number
function Node:mass() end

--- Pushes a physics body through its centre of mass: mass times a change in velocity, so the same push moves a light body further than a heavy one. Through the centre, so it doesn't turn it. Wakes it.
---@param impulse Vec3 Along the world's axes.
---@return boolean # `false` for a node without `physics`.
function Node:apply_impulse(impulse) end

--- The same push, landing at a point instead of through the middle, so it turns the body as well: the further off centre, the more of it becomes angular velocity.
---@param impulse Vec3 Along the world's axes.
---@param point Vec3 Where it lands, as a world position: `node:to_world(...)` turns a point on the node into one.
---@return boolean # `false` for a node without `physics`.
function Node:apply_impulse_at(impulse, point) end

--- Whether a physics body is resting on something: `not node:is_on_ground()` reliably means falling.
---@return boolean
function Node:is_on_ground() end

--- Whether a physics body has settled and stopped being simulated. It wakes when something hits it, when what holds it up goes (checked about once a second), or when a script pushes it.
---@return boolean
function Node:is_asleep() end

--- Puts a settled physics body back to work, for when a script changed something it can't feel (a block placed beside it).
function Node:wake() end

--- Pushes a physics body through its centre of mass for the next tick: a force in mass times blocks per second squared, so calling it every tick is a steady push (and `vec3(0, 32, 0) * node:mass()` holds a body up against default gravity). The same as `apply_impulse(force / 20)`. Wakes it.
---@param force Vec3 Along the world's axes.
---@return boolean # `false` for a node without `physics`.
function Node:apply_force(force) end

--- Whether gravity is on for a physics body: `true` unless `set_gravity(false)` turned it off. How strong it is is the body's `physics.gravity`, which may be 0. `false` for a node without `physics`.
---@return boolean
function Node:has_gravity() end

--- Turns gravity off for a physics body, so it floats where it's put and drifts at the speed it has, or back on. Wakes it. Not saved.
---@param gravity boolean
---@return boolean # `false` for a node without `physics`.
function Node:set_gravity(gravity) end

--- Whether a physics body is kinematic (`set_kinematic`).
---@return boolean
function Node:is_kinematic() end

--- Makes a physics body kinematic: moved only by scripts, never by gravity or what it hits. It keeps moving at the velocity and angular velocity a script gives it (`set_velocity`), and its centity's other bodies collide with it as if it were a wall: a moving platform, a piston. Back to a normal body with `false`. Not saved.
---@param kinematic boolean
---@return boolean # `false` for a node without `physics`.
function Node:set_kinematic(kinematic) end

--- Whether a physics body is simulated (`set_physics_enabled`). `false` for a node without `physics`.
---@return boolean
function Node:is_physics_enabled() end

--- Freezes a physics body in place, or lets it go again. A frozen body doesn't move at all (scripts can still set its pose), and its centity's other bodies collide with it as with a wall. It keeps its velocity, so it carries on where it left off when it's let go. Not saved.
---@param enabled boolean
---@return boolean # `false` for a node without `physics`.
function Node:set_physics_enabled(enabled) end

--- Calls `handler` every time `event` happens to this, until the subscription is cancelled or its script unloads, or this goes away. A handler lives as long as both the script that registered it and the handle it's on. Handlers run in the order they were registered, each with the event object; what one returns means nothing. A handler that errors is logged and the rest still run; after 20 errors in a row it's cancelled. An event this class doesn't have is an error, so a typo is caught where it was made.
---@param event Node.Event The event's name.
---@param handler fun(event: Event) Called with the event: its payload, plus `stop()`, `cancel()` and the rest of `Event`.
---@param options? EventOptions How to listen: `{ every = n }` for `tick`.
---@return Subscription # Call `:cancel()` on it to stop listening.
---@overload fun(self: Node, event: "click", handler: fun(event: ClickEvent)): Subscription
---@overload fun(self: Node, event: "collide", handler: fun(event: CollideEvent)): Subscription
---@overload fun(self: Node, event: "wake", handler: fun(event: NodeEvent)): Subscription
---@overload fun(self: Node, event: "sleep", handler: fun(event: NodeEvent)): Subscription
function Node:on(event, handler, options) end

--- Like `node:on`, but the handler runs only the next time the event happens, then stops listening.
---@param event Node.Event The event's name.
---@param handler fun(event: Event) Called with the event.
---@return Subscription # Call `:cancel()` on it to stop waiting before then.
---@overload fun(self: Node, event: "click", handler: fun(event: ClickEvent)): Subscription
---@overload fun(self: Node, event: "collide", handler: fun(event: CollideEvent)): Subscription
---@overload fun(self: Node, event: "wake", handler: fun(event: NodeEvent)): Subscription
---@overload fun(self: Node, event: "sleep", handler: fun(event: NodeEvent)): Subscription
function Node:once(event, handler) end

--- Something in a world that isn't a block: an arrow, a minecart, a dropped item, a mob, a player. A handle is its UUID, so keeping one is fine: once the entity has gone (killed, removed) or while its chunk is unloaded, its methods answer `nil` or `false` rather than erroring. Its handle is the class it is: a `Living` (health, effects, equipment), a `Mob` (a target, AI, walking, goals), a `Player` or a `DroppedItem`, each with every `Entity` method too, and taken wherever an `Entity` is. A handle to an entity that wasn't in the world when it was handed out becomes its class once it's back. The entities NetherForge draws centities with are never handed out as `Entity`. Positions are world space.
--- A `data()` table, `Item.data` and `nf.json` can keep one: it is saved as what identifies it and comes back as a handle.
---@class Entity
local Entity = {}

--- Its UUID, as a string: the thing to store. A player's gives the same handle back from `nf.players.get(id)`.
---@return string
function Entity:id() end

--- What it is, like `"minecraft:zombie"` or `"minecraft:player"`, or `nil` once it has gone (or while its chunk is unloaded).
---@return string?
function Entity:kind() end

--- Whether it's in the world now: alive, and in a loaded chunk. For a player, whether they're online.
---@return boolean
function Entity:exists() end

--- Whether it's a player: then it's a `Player` handle.
---@return boolean
function Entity:is_player() end

--- Whether it's a living entity (a mob, an animal, an armour stand, a player): then it's a `Living` handle, with health, effects and equipment. `false` while it isn't in the world, unless its handle was handed out as a `Living` already.
---@return boolean
function Entity:is_living() end

--- Whether it's a mob, something with AI (a zombie, a cow, a bat): then it's a `Mob` handle, with a target, walking and goals. `false` while it isn't in the world, unless its handle was handed out as a `Mob` already.
---@return boolean
function Entity:is_mob() end

--- Where it is, in which world, and which way it faces, or `nil` once it has gone (or while its chunk is unloaded).
---@return Location?
function Entity:location() end

--- Where it is (its feet), or `nil` once it has gone (or while its chunk is unloaded).
---@return Vec3?
function Entity:position() end

--- The world it's in, or `nil` once it has gone (or while its chunk is unloaded).
---@return World?
function Entity:world() end

--- Where its eyes are, which is where it looks from, or `nil` once it has gone (or while its chunk is unloaded).
---@return Vec3?
function Entity:eye_position() end

--- Which way it faces horizontally, in degrees, or `nil` once it has gone (or while its chunk is unloaded). Minecraft's convention: 0 faces south (+z), 90 west (−x), 180 north, −90 east.
---@return number?
function Entity:yaw() end

--- How far up or down it looks, in degrees, or `nil` once it has gone (or while its chunk is unloaded): −90 is straight up, 90 straight down.
---@return number?
function Entity:pitch() end

--- The unit direction it looks in, or `nil` once it has gone (or while its chunk is unloaded).
---@return Vec3?
function Entity:direction() end

--- Turns it to face a horizontal direction, keeping its pitch.
---@param degrees number 0 south, 90 west.
---@return boolean # `false` once it has gone (or while its chunk is unloaded).
function Entity:set_yaw(degrees) end

--- Tilts it to look up or down, keeping its yaw.
---@param degrees number −90 up, 90 down.
---@return boolean # `false` once it has gone (or while its chunk is unloaded).
function Entity:set_pitch(degrees) end

--- Turns it to look at a point: its yaw and pitch both.
---@param point Vec3 A world position.
---@return boolean # `false` once it has gone (or while its chunk is unloaded).
function Entity:look_at(point) end

--- Moves it: to a `Location` (facing its way, when it has a facing), or to a position in its current world. It keeps its own facing when the destination has none, and its passengers come along.
---@param location_or_position Location|Vec3 Where it goes.
---@return boolean # Whether it moved: `false` once it has gone (or while its chunk is unloaded), and for a world that doesn't exist.
function Entity:teleport(location_or_position) end

--- How fast it moves, in blocks per tick, or `nil` once it has gone (or while its chunk is unloaded).
---@return Vec3?
function Entity:velocity() end

--- Sets how fast it moves, in blocks per tick: a push. Friction and gravity carry on from there.
---@param velocity Vec3
---@return boolean # `false` once it has gone (or while its chunk is unloaded).
function Entity:set_velocity(velocity) end

--- Adds to how fast it moves, in blocks per tick: a knock.
---@param velocity Vec3
---@return boolean # `false` once it has gone (or while its chunk is unloaded).
function Entity:add_velocity(velocity) end

--- Whether it stands on something. `false` once it has gone (or while its chunk is unloaded).
---@return boolean
function Entity:is_on_ground() end

--- Takes it out of the world for good, without dying: no drops, no death event. Its handlers and its saved table go with it. A player can't be removed: they leave through `kick`.
---@return boolean # `false` once it has gone (or while its chunk is unloaded), and for a player.
function Entity:remove() end

--- What players see it called, as MiniMessage: its custom name, or its kind's name (`"Zombie"`). `nil` once it has gone (or while its chunk is unloaded).
---@return Text?
function Entity:name() end

--- Its custom name, as MiniMessage, or `nil` when it has none, and once it has gone (or while its chunk is unloaded).
---@return Text?
function Entity:custom_name() end

--- Names it, or takes its custom name away. Whether players see the name above it without looking at it is `set_custom_name_visible`.
---@param text? Text MiniMessage. Leave it out (or `nil`) to remove the name.
---@return boolean # `false` once it has gone (or while its chunk is unloaded).
function Entity:set_custom_name(text) end

--- Whether its custom name shows above it all the time, not only when a player looks at it. `false` once it has gone (or while its chunk is unloaded).
---@return boolean
function Entity:is_custom_name_visible() end

--- Shows its custom name above it all the time, or only when a player looks at it.
---@param visible boolean
---@return boolean # `false` once it has gone (or while its chunk is unloaded).
function Entity:set_custom_name_visible(visible) end

--- Its scoreboard tags (what `/tag` adds), sorted. Empty once it has gone.
---@return string[]
function Entity:tags() end

--- Whether it has a scoreboard tag. `false` once it has gone (or while its chunk is unloaded).
---@param tag string
---@return boolean
function Entity:has_tag(tag) end

--- Adds a scoreboard tag, as `/tag <entity> add` does. Tags are saved with the entity and selectors can find them (`@e[tag=boss]`).
---@param tag string Letters, digits, `_`, `-`, `.` and `+`.
---@return boolean # Whether it was added: `false` when it already had it, once it has gone (or while its chunk is unloaded).
function Entity:add_tag(tag) end

--- Takes a scoreboard tag away.
---@param tag string
---@return boolean # Whether it had it: `false` when it didn't, once it has gone (or while its chunk is unloaded).
function Entity:remove_tag(tag) end

--- Its own table for scripts to keep things in, saved on the entity itself (its persistent data container), so it goes where the entity goes and is gone when the entity is: the same table every time, shared by every script. Change it in place; it's saved when the server autosaves, when the entity's chunk unloads and when the server stops. It may hold strings, numbers, booleans, tables of them (keyed by strings, or a list numbered from 1), and the API's own values: `Vec3`s, `Location`s, `Item` tables, and `Player`, `Entity`, `Centity` and `World` handles, which come back as the same types (a handle to something gone answers `nil` or `false`, as handles do). Anything else (a function, a `Menu` window, a `Subscription`) is skipped when it's saved, with a line in the log naming where it was (`data.inventory[3]: a function can't be saved`). `nil` while the entity isn't in the world.
---@return table?
function Entity:data() end

--- Whether it glows: an outline seen through walls. `false` once it has gone (or while its chunk is unloaded).
---@return boolean
function Entity:is_glowing() end

--- Makes it glow, or stops it. The outline is white, or its team's colour (`team:set_color`).
---@param glowing boolean
---@return boolean # `false` once it has gone (or while its chunk is unloaded).
function Entity:set_glowing(glowing) end

--- Whether players can see it at all (`set_visible`), whoever they are. `false` once it has gone (or while its chunk is unloaded).
---@return boolean
function Entity:is_visible() end

--- Hides it from every player, or shows it again. Saved with the entity. To hide it from some players only, use `hide_from`.
---@param visible boolean
---@return boolean # `false` once it has gone (or while its chunk is unloaded).
function Entity:set_visible(visible) end

--- Hides it from one player: they stop seeing it (and can't click it), while everyone else still does. It stays hidden from them when they leave and come back, and when the entity's chunk unloads and loads again, until `show_to`.
---@param player Player
---@return boolean # `false` once it has gone (or while its chunk is unloaded), or when the player is offline.
function Entity:hide_from(player) end

--- Shows it to a player it was hidden from with `hide_from`.
---@param player Player
---@return boolean # `false` once it has gone (or while its chunk is unloaded), or when the player is offline.
function Entity:show_to(player) end

--- Whether `hide_from` hid it from this player.
---@param player Player
---@return boolean
function Entity:is_hidden_from(player) end

--- The project's team it's in (`team:add_member`), or `nil` when it's in none, or in a team the project didn't make (one made with `/team` or by another plugin). For a player it answers while they're offline too; for any other entity, `nil` once it has gone.
---@return Team?
function Entity:team() end

--- Whether it makes no sounds. `false` once it has gone (or while its chunk is unloaded).
---@return boolean
function Entity:is_silent() end

--- Silences it, or lets it make sounds again.
---@param silent boolean
---@return boolean # `false` once it has gone (or while its chunk is unloaded).
function Entity:set_silent(silent) end

--- Whether it falls. `false` once it has gone (or while its chunk is unloaded).
---@return boolean
function Entity:has_gravity() end

--- Lets it fall, or keeps it floating where it is.
---@param gravity boolean
---@return boolean # `false` once it has gone (or while its chunk is unloaded).
function Entity:set_gravity(gravity) end

--- Whether nothing but creative players and the void can hurt it. `false` once it has gone (or while its chunk is unloaded).
---@return boolean
function Entity:is_invulnerable() end

--- Makes it invulnerable, or not.
---@param invulnerable boolean
---@return boolean # `false` once it has gone (or while its chunk is unloaded).
function Entity:set_invulnerable(invulnerable) end

--- What rides on it, in order. Empty when nothing does, or once it has gone.
---@return Entity[]
function Entity:passengers() end

--- Puts something on it to ride: a player on a horse, a zombie on a chicken. It can't ride itself, nor something riding it.
---@param entity Entity The rider.
---@return boolean # Whether it got on: `false` when either has gone, or the game refused.
function Entity:add_passenger(entity) end

--- Takes a rider off it.
---@param entity Entity The rider.
---@return boolean # Whether it was riding it.
function Entity:remove_passenger(entity) end

--- What it rides, or `nil` when it rides nothing, and once it has gone (or while its chunk is unloaded).
---@return Entity?
function Entity:vehicle() end

--- What it carries, as an `Inventory`: a player's own inventory, a chest boat's or a donkey's chest, a villager's. `nil` for an entity without one, or once it has gone.
---@return Inventory?
function Entity:inventory() end

--- The block it's looking at: a ray from its eyes along its direction, the first block whose shape it hits (fluids don't stop it). `nil` when it looks at nothing within reach, or once it has gone.
---@param max_distance? number How far to look, in blocks. Default 20.
---@return Block?
function Entity:target_block(max_distance) end

--- The entity it's looking at: a ray from its eyes along its direction, the first entity it hits, unless a block is in the way. Never itself. `nil` when there's none within reach, or once it has gone.
---@param max_distance? number How far to look, in blocks. Default 20.
---@return Entity?
function Entity:target_entity(max_distance) end

--- The centity it's looking at: a ray from its eyes along its direction, the first centity hitbox it hits (tested with the same shapes clicks are), unless a block is in the way. `nil` when there's none within reach, or once it has gone.
---@param max_distance? number How far to look, in blocks. Default 20.
---@return Centity?
function Entity:target_centity(max_distance) end

--- Calls `handler` every time `event` happens to this, until the subscription is cancelled or its script unloads, or this goes away. A handler lives as long as both the script that registered it and the handle it's on. Handlers run in the order they were registered, each with the event object; what one returns means nothing. A handler that errors is logged and the rest still run; after 20 errors in a row it's cancelled. An event this class doesn't have is an error, so a typo is caught where it was made.
---@param event Entity.Event The event's name.
---@param handler fun(event: Event) Called with the event: its payload, plus `stop()`, `cancel()` and the rest of `Event`.
---@param options? EventOptions How to listen: `{ every = n }` for `tick`.
---@return Subscription # Call `:cancel()` on it to stop listening.
---@overload fun(self: Entity, event: "damage", handler: fun(event: EntityDamageEvent)): Subscription
---@overload fun(self: Entity, event: "interact", handler: fun(event: PlayerInteractEntityEvent)): Subscription
---@overload fun(self: Entity, event: "hit", handler: fun(event: ProjectileHitEvent)): Subscription
---@overload fun(self: Entity, event: "explode", handler: fun(event: ExplodeEvent)): Subscription
---@overload fun(self: Entity, event: "combust", handler: fun(event: EntityCombustEvent)): Subscription
---@overload fun(self: Entity, event: "mount", handler: fun(event: MountEvent)): Subscription
---@overload fun(self: Entity, event: "dismount", handler: fun(event: MountEvent)): Subscription
function Entity:on(event, handler, options) end

--- Like `entity:on`, but the handler runs only the next time the event happens, then stops listening.
---@param event Entity.Event The event's name.
---@param handler fun(event: Event) Called with the event.
---@return Subscription # Call `:cancel()` on it to stop waiting before then.
---@overload fun(self: Entity, event: "damage", handler: fun(event: EntityDamageEvent)): Subscription
---@overload fun(self: Entity, event: "interact", handler: fun(event: PlayerInteractEntityEvent)): Subscription
---@overload fun(self: Entity, event: "hit", handler: fun(event: ProjectileHitEvent)): Subscription
---@overload fun(self: Entity, event: "explode", handler: fun(event: ExplodeEvent)): Subscription
---@overload fun(self: Entity, event: "combust", handler: fun(event: EntityCombustEvent)): Subscription
---@overload fun(self: Entity, event: "mount", handler: fun(event: MountEvent)): Subscription
---@overload fun(self: Entity, event: "dismount", handler: fun(event: MountEvent)): Subscription
function Entity:once(event, handler) end

--- A living entity: a mob, an animal, an armour stand, a player. An `Entity` with health, attributes, status effects and equipment.
---@class Living: Entity
local Living = {}

--- How much health it has, from 0 to `max_health()` (2 is one heart), or `nil` once it has gone (or while its chunk is unloaded).
---@return number?
function Living:health() end

--- Sets its health, up to `max_health()`. 0 kills it.
---@param health number From 0 to `max_health()`.
---@return boolean # `false` once it has gone (or while its chunk is unloaded).
function Living:set_health(health) end

--- The most health it can have, or `nil` once it has gone (or while its chunk is unloaded).
---@return number?
function Living:max_health() end

--- Hurts it, as an attack would: armour and effects count, it flinches, and its `damage` handlers hear it.
---@param amount number How much, before armour: 2 is one heart.
---@param source? Entity Who did it, for knockback, kill credit and angering a mob.
---@return boolean # `false` once it has gone (or while its chunk is unloaded).
function Living:damage(amount, source) end

--- Gives it health back, up to `max_health()`.
---@param amount number 2 is one heart.
---@return boolean # `false` once it has gone (or while its chunk is unloaded).
function Living:heal(amount) end

--- The value of one of its attributes now: its base with every modifier applied (its equipment's, its effects', scripts'), within the attribute's range. `nil` for an attribute it doesn't have (`scale` on a dropped item), or once it has gone (or while its chunk is unloaded). An attribute the server doesn't have is an error.
---@param attribute string Its id: `"minecraft:scale"`, or `"scale"` for short.
---@return number?
function Living:attribute(attribute) end

--- One of its attributes' base value, before modifiers. `nil` for an attribute it doesn't have, or once it has gone (or while its chunk is unloaded). An attribute the server doesn't have is an error.
---@param attribute string Its id: `"minecraft:scale"`, or `"scale"` for short.
---@return number?
function Living:attribute_base(attribute) end

--- Sets one of its attributes' base value. It's saved with the entity. An attribute the server doesn't have is an error.
---@param attribute string Its id: `"minecraft:scale"`, or `"scale"` for short.
---@param value number The new base. The attribute's value stays within the range the game gives it, whatever the base.
---@return boolean # `false` for an attribute it doesn't have, once it has gone (or while its chunk is unloaded).
function Living:set_attribute_base(attribute, value) end

--- Everything changing one of its attributes: the modifiers scripts added, and those from its equipment, effects and the game itself (sprinting), each with its full id. `nil` for an attribute it doesn't have, or once it has gone (or while its chunk is unloaded). An attribute the server doesn't have is an error.
---@param attribute string Its id: `"minecraft:scale"`, or `"scale"` for short.
---@return AttributeModifier[]?
function Living:attribute_modifiers(attribute) end

--- Adds a modifier to one of its attributes, replacing the project's modifier of the same id. Modifiers are saved with the entity, so give each a fixed id and remove it when it's done, rather than making new ones. The id is in the project's namespace (`netherforge.json`'s `namespace`): `"slow_zone"` is `"<namespace>:slow_zone"`, so nothing else's modifiers can be replaced or removed. An attribute the server doesn't have is an error.
---@param attribute string Its id: `"minecraft:scale"`, or `"scale"` for short.
---@param id string What it's called, to remove it later: lowercase letters, digits, `_`, `-`, `.` and `/`. `"<namespace>:"` in front is allowed, and the same.
---@param amount number How much.
---@param operation? "add_value"|"add_multiplied_base"|"add_multiplied_total" How `amount` applies, as an item's `attribute_modifiers`: added; added times the base value; or the total multiplied by `1 + amount`. Default `"add_value"`.
---@return boolean # `false` for an attribute it doesn't have, once it has gone (or while its chunk is unloaded).
function Living:add_attribute_modifier(attribute, id, amount, operation) end

--- Takes one of the project's modifiers off one of its attributes. An attribute the server doesn't have is an error.
---@param attribute string Its id: `"minecraft:scale"`, or `"scale"` for short.
---@param id string The id `add_attribute_modifier` was given, with or without `"<namespace>:"`. Another namespace is an error: only the project's own modifiers can be removed.
---@return boolean # Whether it had it: `false` when it didn't, for an attribute it doesn't have, once it has gone (or while its chunk is unloaded).
function Living:remove_attribute_modifier(attribute, id) end

--- Its status effects, like speed or poison. Empty once it has gone.
---@return StatusEffect[]
function Living:effects() end

--- Whether it has a status effect. An effect the server doesn't have is an error. `false` once it has gone (or while its chunk is unloaded).
---@param effect string Its id: `"minecraft:speed"`.
---@return boolean
function Living:has_effect(effect) end

--- Gives it a status effect, replacing one of the same kind it has. An effect the server doesn't have is an error.
---@param effect string Its id: `"minecraft:speed"`.
---@param ticks integer How long it lasts. -1 is for ever.
---@param options? EffectOptions
---@return boolean # `false` once it has gone (or while its chunk is unloaded).
function Living:add_effect(effect, ticks, options) end

--- Takes a status effect away. An effect the server doesn't have is an error.
---@param effect string Its id.
---@return boolean # Whether it had it.
function Living:remove_effect(effect) end

--- What it holds or wears in a slot, or `nil` for nothing, and once it has gone (or while its chunk is unloaded).
---@param slot "main_hand"|"off_hand"|"head"|"chest"|"legs"|"feet"|"body" `"body"` is a horse's or a wolf's armour.
---@return Item?
function Living:equipment(slot) end

--- Puts an item in one of its slots, or empties it. An item table with a misspelled field, or an item the server doesn't have, is an error.
---@param slot "main_hand"|"off_hand"|"head"|"chest"|"legs"|"feet"|"body"
---@param item? Item Leave it out (or `nil`) to empty the slot.
---@return boolean # `false` once it has gone (or while its chunk is unloaded).
function Living:set_equipment(slot, item) end

--- Calls `handler` every time `event` happens to this, until the subscription is cancelled or its script unloads, or this goes away. A handler lives as long as both the script that registered it and the handle it's on. Handlers run in the order they were registered, each with the event object; what one returns means nothing. A handler that errors is logged and the rest still run; after 20 errors in a row it's cancelled. An event this class doesn't have is an error, so a typo is caught where it was made.
---@param event Living.Event The event's name.
---@param handler fun(event: Event) Called with the event: its payload, plus `stop()`, `cancel()` and the rest of `Event`.
---@param options? EventOptions How to listen: `{ every = n }` for `tick`.
---@return Subscription # Call `:cancel()` on it to stop listening.
---@overload fun(self: Living, event: "death", handler: fun(event: EntityDeathEvent)): Subscription
---@overload fun(self: Living, event: "heal", handler: fun(event: EntityHealEvent)): Subscription
---@overload fun(self: Living, event: "launch_projectile", handler: fun(event: ProjectileLaunchEvent)): Subscription
---@overload fun(self: Living, event: "shoot_bow", handler: fun(event: EntityShootBowEvent)): Subscription
---@overload fun(self: Living, event: "change_effect", handler: fun(event: EntityChangeEffectEvent)): Subscription
---@overload fun(self: Living, event: "damage", handler: fun(event: EntityDamageEvent)): Subscription
---@overload fun(self: Living, event: "interact", handler: fun(event: PlayerInteractEntityEvent)): Subscription
---@overload fun(self: Living, event: "hit", handler: fun(event: ProjectileHitEvent)): Subscription
---@overload fun(self: Living, event: "explode", handler: fun(event: ExplodeEvent)): Subscription
---@overload fun(self: Living, event: "combust", handler: fun(event: EntityCombustEvent)): Subscription
---@overload fun(self: Living, event: "mount", handler: fun(event: MountEvent)): Subscription
---@overload fun(self: Living, event: "dismount", handler: fun(event: MountEvent)): Subscription
function Living:on(event, handler, options) end

--- Like `living:on`, but the handler runs only the next time the event happens, then stops listening.
---@param event Living.Event The event's name.
---@param handler fun(event: Event) Called with the event.
---@return Subscription # Call `:cancel()` on it to stop waiting before then.
---@overload fun(self: Living, event: "death", handler: fun(event: EntityDeathEvent)): Subscription
---@overload fun(self: Living, event: "heal", handler: fun(event: EntityHealEvent)): Subscription
---@overload fun(self: Living, event: "launch_projectile", handler: fun(event: ProjectileLaunchEvent)): Subscription
---@overload fun(self: Living, event: "shoot_bow", handler: fun(event: EntityShootBowEvent)): Subscription
---@overload fun(self: Living, event: "change_effect", handler: fun(event: EntityChangeEffectEvent)): Subscription
---@overload fun(self: Living, event: "damage", handler: fun(event: EntityDamageEvent)): Subscription
---@overload fun(self: Living, event: "interact", handler: fun(event: PlayerInteractEntityEvent)): Subscription
---@overload fun(self: Living, event: "hit", handler: fun(event: ProjectileHitEvent)): Subscription
---@overload fun(self: Living, event: "explode", handler: fun(event: ExplodeEvent)): Subscription
---@overload fun(self: Living, event: "combust", handler: fun(event: EntityCombustEvent)): Subscription
---@overload fun(self: Living, event: "mount", handler: fun(event: MountEvent)): Subscription
---@overload fun(self: Living, event: "dismount", handler: fun(event: MountEvent)): Subscription
function Living:once(event, handler) end

--- A mob: a `Living` with AI, like a zombie, a cow or a bat. It has a target, walks where it's sent (`move_to`), and its AI runs goals, the game's and any written in Lua.
---@class Mob: Living
local Mob = {}

--- What it is after (to attack it, or follow it), or `nil` for nothing, and once it has gone (or while its chunk is unloaded).
---@return Entity?
function Mob:target() end

--- Sets what it is after, or calms it.
---@param entity? Entity Leave it out (or `nil`) to clear it.
---@return boolean # `false` once it has gone (or while its chunk is unloaded).
function Mob:set_target(entity) end

--- Whether it thinks for itself: moves, looks around, attacks. `false` once it has gone (or while its chunk is unloaded).
---@return boolean
function Mob:has_ai() end

--- Lets it think for itself, or freezes it in place (it can still be pushed and hurt).
---@param ai boolean
---@return boolean # `false` once it has gone (or while its chunk is unloaded).
function Mob:set_ai(ai) end

--- Makes it walk somewhere, finding its way round what's in the way: to a place in its own world, or after an entity, looking for a new path to wherever that entity is every 10 ticks. When it stops, its `path_end` handlers hear whether it got there. A new `move_to` replaces the walk it was on. Its own AI still runs, and can send it elsewhere (a zombie that sees a player goes for them), which ends the walk as given up; with `set_ai(false)` it doesn't walk at all.
---@param target Location|Vec3|Entity Where: a position in its world, a `Location` (in its world: another world is `false`), or an entity to go after.
---@param options? PathOptions
---@return boolean # Whether it found a path and set off: `false` when there's no way there from where it is (in the air, just spawned or falling, it finds none until it lands), for a target in another world or gone, once it has gone (or while its chunk is unloaded).
function Mob:move_to(target, options) end

--- Stops it walking where it was going, whoever sent it: a `move_to`, or its own AI. A walk `move_to` started ends without a `path_end`.
---@return boolean # `false` once it has gone (or while its chunk is unloaded).
function Mob:stop_pathing() end

--- Whether it is walking somewhere now, whoever sent it: a `move_to`, or its own AI. `false` once it has gone (or while its chunk is unloaded).
---@return boolean
function Mob:has_path() end

--- Where the path it is walking ends, whoever sent it: as close as it can get to where it was sent, which is short of it when there's no way all the way there. `nil` when it isn't walking, or once it has gone (or while its chunk is unloaded).
---@return Vec3?
function Mob:path_target() end

--- Every goal its AI has, the game's and any added: its regular goals (walking about, attacking, looking at players) and its targeting goals (what it picks a fight with), each with its key, its priority and the controls it claims. In no particular order. `nil` once it has gone (or while its chunk is unloaded).
---@return MobGoal[]?
function Mob:goals() end

--- Takes goals off it: every one with the key, the game's or one a script added. Goals aren't saved, so a mob that unloads and loads again has the game's goals back.
---@param key string A goal's key, as `goals()` lists it: `"minecraft:random_stroll"`, or `"random_stroll"` for short (a key without a namespace is the game's). The project's own goals are `"<namespace>:<id>"`.
---@return boolean # Whether it had one: `false` when it didn't, once it has gone (or while its chunk is unloaded).
function Mob:remove_goal(key) end

--- Takes every goal off it, its targeting goals too, but those `options.keep` names. A mob with no goals stands where it is but for what moves it (`move_to`, being pushed); goals added afterwards run as usual. Goals aren't saved, so a mob that unloads and loads again has the game's goals back.
---@param options? GoalClearOptions
---@return boolean # `false` once it has gone (or while its chunk is unloaded).
function Mob:clear_goals(options) end

--- Gives it a goal written in Lua, which its AI runs with the goals it has. Every tick or two the AI asks `should_start` of each goal that isn't running and whose controls are free, or held by goals of a lower priority (a bigger number), which it then stops; `start` runs when it starts, `tick` while it runs, and `stop` once `should_continue` says no or a more important goal takes one of its controls. A goal that claims `"target"` is a targeting goal: it runs with the mob's targeting goals (`hurt_by_target`, `nearest_attackable_target`) rather than its regular goals, so it claims nothing else. The id is in the project's namespace: `"guard_post"` is `"<namespace>:guard_post"`, and a goal with an id the mob already has replaces it. Goals aren't saved: they go when the mob unloads, and when the script that added them stops (a reload), so set them up again when it spawns or in the script's body. A callback that errors is reported, with its line, and its goal is taken off the mob. Goals added or removed from inside a goal's own callbacks change before the mob's next tick.
---@param id string What it's called, to remove it later: lowercase letters, digits, `_`, `-`, `.` and `/`. `"<namespace>:"` in front is allowed, and the same.
---@param definition GoalDefinition What it claims and does.
---@return boolean # `false` once it has gone (or while its chunk is unloaded).
function Mob:add_goal(id, definition) end

--- Calls `handler` every time `event` happens to this, until the subscription is cancelled or its script unloads, or this goes away. A handler lives as long as both the script that registered it and the handle it's on. Handlers run in the order they were registered, each with the event object; what one returns means nothing. A handler that errors is logged and the rest still run; after 20 errors in a row it's cancelled. An event this class doesn't have is an error, so a typo is caught where it was made.
---@param event Mob.Event The event's name.
---@param handler fun(event: Event) Called with the event: its payload, plus `stop()`, `cancel()` and the rest of `Event`.
---@param options? EventOptions How to listen: `{ every = n }` for `tick`.
---@return Subscription # Call `:cancel()` on it to stop listening.
---@overload fun(self: Mob, event: "path_end", handler: fun(event: EntityPathEndEvent)): Subscription
---@overload fun(self: Mob, event: "target", handler: fun(event: EntityTargetEvent)): Subscription
---@overload fun(self: Mob, event: "death", handler: fun(event: EntityDeathEvent)): Subscription
---@overload fun(self: Mob, event: "heal", handler: fun(event: EntityHealEvent)): Subscription
---@overload fun(self: Mob, event: "launch_projectile", handler: fun(event: ProjectileLaunchEvent)): Subscription
---@overload fun(self: Mob, event: "shoot_bow", handler: fun(event: EntityShootBowEvent)): Subscription
---@overload fun(self: Mob, event: "change_effect", handler: fun(event: EntityChangeEffectEvent)): Subscription
---@overload fun(self: Mob, event: "damage", handler: fun(event: EntityDamageEvent)): Subscription
---@overload fun(self: Mob, event: "interact", handler: fun(event: PlayerInteractEntityEvent)): Subscription
---@overload fun(self: Mob, event: "hit", handler: fun(event: ProjectileHitEvent)): Subscription
---@overload fun(self: Mob, event: "explode", handler: fun(event: ExplodeEvent)): Subscription
---@overload fun(self: Mob, event: "combust", handler: fun(event: EntityCombustEvent)): Subscription
---@overload fun(self: Mob, event: "mount", handler: fun(event: MountEvent)): Subscription
---@overload fun(self: Mob, event: "dismount", handler: fun(event: MountEvent)): Subscription
function Mob:on(event, handler, options) end

--- Like `mob:on`, but the handler runs only the next time the event happens, then stops listening.
---@param event Mob.Event The event's name.
---@param handler fun(event: Event) Called with the event.
---@return Subscription # Call `:cancel()` on it to stop waiting before then.
---@overload fun(self: Mob, event: "path_end", handler: fun(event: EntityPathEndEvent)): Subscription
---@overload fun(self: Mob, event: "target", handler: fun(event: EntityTargetEvent)): Subscription
---@overload fun(self: Mob, event: "death", handler: fun(event: EntityDeathEvent)): Subscription
---@overload fun(self: Mob, event: "heal", handler: fun(event: EntityHealEvent)): Subscription
---@overload fun(self: Mob, event: "launch_projectile", handler: fun(event: ProjectileLaunchEvent)): Subscription
---@overload fun(self: Mob, event: "shoot_bow", handler: fun(event: EntityShootBowEvent)): Subscription
---@overload fun(self: Mob, event: "change_effect", handler: fun(event: EntityChangeEffectEvent)): Subscription
---@overload fun(self: Mob, event: "damage", handler: fun(event: EntityDamageEvent)): Subscription
---@overload fun(self: Mob, event: "interact", handler: fun(event: PlayerInteractEntityEvent)): Subscription
---@overload fun(self: Mob, event: "hit", handler: fun(event: ProjectileHitEvent)): Subscription
---@overload fun(self: Mob, event: "explode", handler: fun(event: ExplodeEvent)): Subscription
---@overload fun(self: Mob, event: "combust", handler: fun(event: EntityCombustEvent)): Subscription
---@overload fun(self: Mob, event: "mount", handler: fun(event: MountEvent)): Subscription
---@overload fun(self: Mob, event: "dismount", handler: fun(event: MountEvent)): Subscription
function Mob:once(event, handler) end

--- An item lying on the ground, which players pick up by walking over it: an `Entity` holding one stack.
---@class DroppedItem: Entity
local DroppedItem = {}

--- The stack it is, or `nil` once it has gone (or while its chunk is unloaded).
---@return Item?
function DroppedItem:item() end

--- Changes the stack it is. An item table with a misspelled field, or an item the server doesn't have, is an error.
---@param item Item
---@return boolean # `false` once it has gone (or while its chunk is unloaded).
function DroppedItem:set_item(item) end

--- How many ticks until it can be picked up, or `nil` once it has gone (or while its chunk is unloaded).
---@return integer?
function DroppedItem:pickup_delay() end

--- Sets how many ticks until it can be picked up.
---@param ticks integer 0 for at once; 32767 for never.
---@return boolean # `false` once it has gone (or while its chunk is unloaded).
function DroppedItem:set_pickup_delay(ticks) end

--- Someone on the server: a `Living`, with everything living entities have, and more. A handle is a UUID, so keeping one is fine: if they leave, its methods answer `nil` or `false` rather than erroring, and work again when they come back. `nf.players.get` hands out handles to people who are offline too. Their real inventory is numbered: the hotbar 0 to 8, the rest of the main inventory 9 to 35, armour 36 (feet), 37 (legs), 38 (chest) and 39 (head), the offhand 40, then body armour 41 and a saddle 42 (what the game keeps there for any entity).
---@class Player: Living
local Player = {}

--- Their name, as they have it now (a player can change theirs between visits).
---@return string
function Player:name() end

--- What chat and other plugins call them, as MiniMessage: their name unless something changed it. `nil` when they're offline.
---@return Text?
function Player:display_name() end

--- Changes what chat calls them, until they leave. Their real name, and what's above their head, stay.
---@param text Text MiniMessage.
---@return boolean # `false` when they're offline.
function Player:set_display_name(text) end

--- Sends them a chat line only they see.
---@param text Text MiniMessage, like `"<green>Done!"`.
---@return boolean # `false` when they're offline.
function Player:send_message(text) end

--- Shows a line above their hotbar for a couple of seconds.
---@param text Text MiniMessage.
---@return boolean # `false` when they're offline.
function Player:send_actionbar(text) end

--- Shows a big title in the middle of their screen, with a smaller line under it.
---@param title Text MiniMessage.
---@param subtitle? Text MiniMessage. Default none.
---@param options? TitleOptions
---@return boolean # `false` when they're offline.
function Player:send_title(title, subtitle, options) end

--- Takes a title off their screen.
---@return boolean # `false` when they're offline.
function Player:clear_title() end

--- Plays a sound only they hear, where they are, following them. The same sounds `world:play_sound` takes: an unknown one is an error.
---@param sound string A Minecraft sound (`"minecraft:ui.button.click"`) or one of the project's resource pack sounds (`"<pack>/<key>"`, like `"ui/click"`).
---@param options? SoundOptions
---@return boolean # `false` when they're offline.
function Player:play_sound(sound, options) end

--- Stops a sound playing for them, or every sound when it's left out. An unknown sound is an error.
---@param sound? string The sound to stop.
---@return boolean # `false` when they're offline.
function Player:stop_sound(sound) end

--- Spawns particles only they see, in their world: `world:spawn_particle` with `viewers = { player }`, so `viewers` isn't an option here.
---@param particle string A particle id: `"minecraft:happy_villager"`.
---@param position Vec3 Where, in their world.
---@param options? ParticleOptions
---@return boolean # `false` when they're offline.
function Player:spawn_particle(particle, position, options) end

--- Runs a command as them, with their permissions. The leading slash is optional.
---@param command string
---@return boolean # Whether it ran.
function Player:run(command) end

--- Whether they have a permission node. `false` when they're offline.
---@param permission string
---@return boolean
function Player:has_permission(permission) end

--- Grants them a permission node (`true`), or denies it (`false`), alongside whatever a permissions plugin gives them. It's kept with the project's other grants and given back every time they join, until `unset_permission`; it works while they're offline too. A package (every project is one) may only grant nodes its own `netherforge.json` lists under `allow.permissions`, held to the package whose code makes the call as `requires` is (each entry allows itself and everything under it: `"shop"` allows `shop` and `shop.vip`): anything else is an error, and grants a later edit no longer allows stop applying.
---@param permission string The node: lowercase letters, digits, `_`, `-` and `.`, like `"shop.vip"`.
---@param value boolean `true` grants it; `false` denies it.
function Player:set_permission(permission, value) end

--- Takes back a node `set_permission` granted or denied: they have it again only if something else gives it to them.
---@param permission string The node.
---@return boolean # `false` when the project hadn't set it.
function Player:unset_permission(permission) end

--- The nodes the project has set for them with `set_permission`, each to `true` (granted) or `false` (denied). Not every permission they have: `has_permission` answers that.
---@return table<string, boolean>
function Player:permissions() end

--- Whether they're a server operator, online or not.
---@return boolean
function Player:is_operator() end

--- Their game mode, or `nil` when they're offline.
---@return "survival"|"creative"|"adventure"|"spectator"|nil
function Player:game_mode() end

--- Changes their game mode.
---@param game_mode "survival"|"creative"|"adventure"|"spectator"
---@return boolean # `false` once it has gone (or while its chunk is unloaded).
function Player:set_game_mode(game_mode) end

--- How full they are, 0 to 20 (20 is a full bar), or `nil` when they're offline.
---@return integer?
function Player:food() end

--- Sets how full they are.
---@param food integer 0 to 20.
---@return boolean # `false` once it has gone (or while its chunk is unloaded).
function Player:set_food(food) end

--- How long until they get hungry again, 0 up to their food level, or `nil` when they're offline.
---@return number?
function Player:saturation() end

--- Sets their saturation, up to their food level.
---@param saturation number
---@return boolean # `false` once it has gone (or while its chunk is unloaded).
function Player:set_saturation(saturation) end

--- Their experience level, or `nil` when they're offline.
---@return integer?
function Player:level() end

--- Sets their experience level.
---@param level integer At least 0.
---@return boolean # `false` once it has gone (or while its chunk is unloaded).
function Player:set_level(level) end

--- How far the experience bar is towards the next level, 0 to 1, or `nil` when they're offline.
---@return number?
function Player:experience_progress() end

--- Moves the experience bar, keeping their level.
---@param progress number 0 to 1.
---@return boolean # `false` once it has gone (or while its chunk is unloaded).
function Player:set_experience_progress(progress) end

--- Gives them experience points, as orbs would: their level goes up when the bar fills.
---@param points integer At least 0.
---@return boolean # `false` when they're offline.
function Player:give_experience(points) end

--- Whether they're flying. `false` when they're offline.
---@return boolean
function Player:is_flying() end

--- Starts or stops them flying. They have to be able to fly (`set_can_fly`) to start.
---@param flying boolean
---@return boolean # `false` once it has gone (or while its chunk is unloaded).
function Player:set_flying(flying) end

--- Whether they may fly (double-tapping jump), as in creative. `false` when they're offline.
---@return boolean
function Player:can_fly() end

--- Lets them fly, or not.
---@param can_fly boolean
---@return boolean # `false` once it has gone (or while its chunk is unloaded).
function Player:set_can_fly(can_fly) end

--- How fast they walk, 0 to 1 (0.2 is normal), or `nil` when they're offline.
---@return number?
function Player:walk_speed() end

--- Sets how fast they walk.
---@param speed number −1 to 1; 0.2 is normal.
---@return boolean # `false` once it has gone (or while its chunk is unloaded).
function Player:set_walk_speed(speed) end

--- How fast they fly, 0 to 1 (0.1 is normal), or `nil` when they're offline.
---@return number?
function Player:fly_speed() end

--- Sets how fast they fly.
---@param speed number −1 to 1; 0.1 is normal.
---@return boolean # `false` once it has gone (or while its chunk is unloaded).
function Player:set_fly_speed(speed) end

--- Whether they're sneaking. `false` when they're offline.
---@return boolean
function Player:is_sneaking() end

--- Whether they're sprinting. `false` when they're offline.
---@return boolean
function Player:is_sprinting() end

--- The language their game is set to, like `"en_us"`, or `nil` when they're offline. Right after they join it can still be the default until their game says.
---@return string?
function Player:locale() end

--- How long a message takes to reach them and come back, in milliseconds, as the server estimates it; `nil` when they're offline.
---@return integer?
function Player:ping() end

--- Disconnects them from the server.
---@param reason? Text What their screen says, MiniMessage. Default the server's own message.
---@return boolean # `false` when they're offline.
function Player:kick(reason) end

--- When they first joined this server, as Unix time in milliseconds (like `nf.server.unix_time()`), or `nil` for someone who never has (a handle from `nf.players.whitelisted()`, say).
---@return integer?
function Player:first_played() end

--- When they were last on this server, as Unix time in milliseconds (like `nf.server.unix_time()`): now while they're online, `nil` for someone who never has been.
---@return integer?
function Player:last_seen() end

--- Whether they're banned from the server (by `ban`, `/ban` or anything else), and the ban hasn't run out.
---@return boolean
function Player:is_banned() end

--- Bans them from the server: they can't join until it runs out or `unban`. Kicks them, with the reason, when they're online. Banning someone already banned replaces their ban.
--- Needs `"requires": { "moderation": true }` in `netherforge.json`: without it, calling it is an error.
---@param options? BanOptions
function Player:ban(options) end

--- Lifts their ban.
--- Needs `"requires": { "moderation": true }` in `netherforge.json`: without it, calling it is an error.
---@return boolean # `false` when they weren't banned.
function Player:unban() end

--- Whether they're on the server's whitelist (which only keeps people out while it's on: `nf.server.is_whitelist_enabled()`).
---@return boolean
function Player:is_whitelisted() end

--- Puts them on the server's whitelist, or takes them off. Taking someone online off doesn't kick them.
--- Needs `"requires": { "moderation": true }` in `netherforge.json`: without it, calling it is an error.
---@param whitelisted boolean
function Player:set_whitelisted(whitelisted) end

--- Their ender chest, as an `Inventory`, or `nil` when they're offline.
---@return Inventory?
function Player:ender_chest() end

--- What's in their main hand (the selected hotbar slot), or `nil` for nothing or when they're offline.
---@return Item?
function Player:held_item() end

--- Puts an item in their main hand, or empties it. An item table with a misspelled field, or an item the server doesn't have, is an error.
---@param item? Item Leave it out (or `nil`) to empty it.
---@return boolean # `false` when they're offline.
function Player:set_held_item(item) end

--- What's in their offhand, or `nil` for nothing or when they're offline.
---@return Item?
function Player:off_hand_item() end

--- Puts an item in their offhand, or empties it.
---@param item? Item Leave it out (or `nil`) to empty it.
---@return boolean # `false` when they're offline.
function Player:set_off_hand_item(item) end

--- Which hotbar slot they have selected, 0 to 8, or `nil` when they're offline.
---@return integer?
function Player:held_slot() end

--- Selects a hotbar slot for them.
---@param index integer 0 to 8.
---@return boolean # `false` once it has gone (or while its chunk is unloaded).
function Player:set_held_slot(index) end

--- Puts an item into their inventory, topping up matching stacks first, and drops what doesn't fit at their feet. An item table with a misspelled field, or an item the server doesn't have, is an error.
---@param item Item
---@return integer? # How many were dropped at their feet: 0 when everything fitted. `nil` when they're offline.
function Player:give_item(item) end

--- Puts a recipe in their recipe book (with the toast that says so). `false` when they're offline, already had it, or the server has no such recipe; a project recipe id the project doesn't have is an error.
---@param recipe RecipeId A project recipe's id (`"ruby_sword"`), or any recipe the server has by its namespaced id (`"minecraft:bread"`).
---@return boolean
function Player:discover_recipe(recipe) end

--- Takes a recipe out of their recipe book. They can still craft it. `false` when they're offline or didn't have it.
---@param recipe RecipeId A project recipe's id (`"ruby_sword"`), or any recipe the server has by its namespaced id (`"minecraft:bread"`).
---@return boolean
function Player:undiscover_recipe(recipe) end

--- Whether a recipe is in their recipe book. `false` when they're offline.
---@param recipe RecipeId A project recipe's id (`"ruby_sword"`), or any recipe the server has by its namespaced id (`"minecraft:bread"`).
---@return boolean
function Player:has_discovered_recipe(recipe) end

--- Meets an advancement's criteria for them, as if they'd earned them: every criterion, or just `criterion`. Once the advancement is complete they get the toast, the chat line and its rewards, as when the game completes it.
---@param advancement AdvancementId One of the project's advancements (`"first_steps"`, `advancements/first_steps.json`; a bare id is the calling package's own), one a package exports (`"acme:quests"`), or one of the game's by its key (`"minecraft:story/mine_diamond"`). One the server doesn't have is an error; so is a project advancement added since the server started, until it restarts.
---@param criterion? string Only this criterion, by the name the advancement's file gives it. One it doesn't have is an error. Leave it out for every criterion.
---@return boolean # `false` when they're offline or had already met it.
function Player:grant_advancement(advancement, criterion) end

--- Takes an advancement back: every criterion of it, or just `criterion`.
---@param advancement AdvancementId One of the project's advancements (`"first_steps"`, `advancements/first_steps.json`; a bare id is the calling package's own), one a package exports (`"acme:quests"`), or one of the game's by its key (`"minecraft:story/mine_diamond"`). One the server doesn't have is an error; so is a project advancement added since the server started, until it restarts.
---@param criterion? string Only this criterion, by the name the advancement's file gives it. One it doesn't have is an error. Leave it out for every criterion.
---@return boolean # `false` when they're offline or hadn't met any of it.
function Player:revoke_advancement(advancement, criterion) end

--- Whether they've completed an advancement. `false` when they're offline.
---@param advancement AdvancementId One of the project's advancements (`"first_steps"`, `advancements/first_steps.json`; a bare id is the calling package's own), one a package exports (`"acme:quests"`), or one of the game's by its key (`"minecraft:story/mine_diamond"`). One the server doesn't have is an error; so is a project advancement added since the server started, until it restarts.
---@return boolean
function Player:has_advancement(advancement) end

--- How far they are with an advancement: the criteria they've met and those left. `nil` when they're offline.
---@param advancement AdvancementId One of the project's advancements (`"first_steps"`, `advancements/first_steps.json`; a bare id is the calling package's own), one a package exports (`"acme:quests"`), or one of the game's by its key (`"minecraft:story/mine_diamond"`). One the server doesn't have is an error; so is a project advancement added since the server started, until it restarts.
---@return AdvancementProgress?
function Player:advancement_progress(advancement) end

--- How many ticks are left before they can use an item again, or 0. `nil` when they're offline.
---@param key string An item kind (`"minecraft:ender_pearl"`), or a cooldown group an item's `cooldown` names.
---@return integer?
function Player:item_cooldown(key) end

--- Makes them wait before using an item again, as an ender pearl does: every item of that kind, or every item in that cooldown group. Items whose own `cooldown` names another group aren't affected by their kind's.
---@param key string An item kind, or a cooldown group: `"<namespace>:<name>"`.
---@param ticks integer How long; 0 ends it.
---@return boolean # `false` when they're offline.
function Player:set_item_cooldown(key, ticks) end

--- Opens a menu for them: the shared window for a `shared` menu, otherwise a new window of their own, filled from the file (or from the template). Its script's body has run (for a new window) and its `open` handlers have heard it by the time this returns. A menu the project doesn't have is an error; a template that's gone opens nothing (`nil`).
---@param menu MenuId|MenuTemplate Its folder name under `menus/`, or a `MenuTemplate` from `nf.menus.create`.
---@param options? MenuOpenOptions What to open it with: `context` for the new window. A key it doesn't take is an error.
---@return Menu? # The window, or `nil` when they're offline.
function Player:open_menu(menu, options) end

--- The project menu window they're looking at, or `nil` (nothing open, or a chest that isn't one of ours).
---@return Menu?
function Player:menu() end

--- Closes the project menu they have open. Anything else on their screen (a chest, their own inventory) stays open.
---@return boolean # `false` when they're offline or have no menu of ours open.
function Player:close_menu() end

--- Shows them a real inventory, as if they'd opened it: a chest's, another player's, their ender chest. Whatever was on their screen closes.
---@param inventory Inventory
---@return boolean # `false` when they're offline, or the inventory has gone.
function Player:open_inventory(inventory) end

--- Closes whatever container screen they have open: a chest, a project menu, their own inventory.
---@return boolean # `false` when they're offline.
function Player:close_inventory() end

--- Puts a dialog on their screen: the same as `dialog:open_for(player, options)`. One of the project's dialogs already on their screen is closed (its `close` handlers hear it). A dialog the project doesn't have is an error; a `Dialog` from `nf.dialogs.create` that's gone opens nothing (`false`).
---@param dialog DialogId|Dialog Its folder name under `dialogs/`, or a `Dialog` (one from `nf.dialogs.create`, say).
---@param options? DialogOpenOptions What to open it with, for this opening only: `context`, input `values`, a `title` or `body` text.
---@return boolean # `false` when they're offline.
function Player:open_dialog(dialog, options) end

--- Takes whatever dialog is on their screen away. When it's one of the project's, its `close` handlers hear it.
---@return boolean # `false` when they're offline.
function Player:close_dialog() end

--- Their sidebar: the panel of lines at the right of their screen. Always the same handle for the same player.
---@return Sidebar
function Player:sidebar() end

--- A border of their own, which only they see: the first call gives them one, a copy of their world's, and they see it instead of their world's until `reset_border()` or they leave (it follows them from world to world). The server draws it for them but doesn't enforce it: it doesn't hurt them. `nil` when they're offline.
---@return WorldBorder?
function Player:border() end

--- Whether they see a border of their own (`player:border()`) rather than their world's. `false` when they're offline.
---@return boolean
function Player:has_own_border() end

--- Takes their own border away: they see their world's again, and the handle `border()` gave answers `nil`.
---@return boolean # `false` when they're offline or had no border of their own.
function Player:reset_border() end

--- The text above the player list (what tab shows), as MiniMessage, or `nil` when there's none or they're offline.
---@return Text?
function Player:tab_header() end

--- Sets the text above the player list.
---@param text Text MiniMessage; `""` for none.
---@return boolean # `false` when they're offline.
function Player:set_tab_header(text) end

--- The text below the player list, as MiniMessage, or `nil` when there's none or they're offline.
---@return Text?
function Player:tab_footer() end

--- Sets the text below the player list.
---@param text Text MiniMessage; `""` for none.
---@return boolean # `false` when they're offline.
function Player:set_tab_footer(text) end

--- What the player list (tab) calls them, as MiniMessage: their name unless `set_tab_name` changed it. Their team's prefix and suffix go around it. `nil` when they're offline.
---@return Text?
function Player:tab_name() end

--- Changes what the player list calls them, until they leave. Their real name, what chat calls them and what's over their head stay.
---@param text? Text MiniMessage. Leave it out to go back to their name.
---@return boolean # `false` when they're offline.
function Player:set_tab_name(text) end

--- Where they come in the player list: higher comes first, and equal ones are sorted as Minecraft sorts them (by team, then name). 0 unless `set_tab_order` changed it; `nil` when they're offline.
---@return integer?
function Player:tab_order() end

--- Moves them up or down the player list, until they leave.
---@param order integer Higher comes first; default 0.
---@return boolean # `false` when they're offline.
function Player:set_tab_order(order) end

--- Whether they're in another player's player list (`set_listed_for`). `false` when either is offline.
---@param viewer Player Whose list.
---@return boolean
function Player:is_listed_for(viewer) end

--- Takes them out of another player's player list, or puts them back. Only the list's entry goes: the viewer still sees them in the world and hears them in chat (to hide them altogether, `hide_from`). It stays that way when either of them leaves and comes back, until it's set back.
---@param viewer Player Whose list.
---@param listed boolean
---@return boolean # `false` when either is offline.
function Player:set_listed_for(viewer, listed) end

--- The line under their name tag (`set_below_name`), as MiniMessage, or `nil` when they have none (or they're offline).
---@return Text?
function Player:below_name() end

--- Puts a line of text under their name tag, which every player sees: a rank, their health, a level. It goes when they leave. Minecraft draws this line for every player as soon as anyone has one, so while someone does, players without one show an empty line. It's shown through the scoreboard's below-name slot, which it takes over from anything else showing there.
---@param text? Text MiniMessage. Leave it out to take the line away.
---@return boolean # `false` when they're offline.
function Player:set_below_name(text) end

--- The project's boss bars they're among the viewers of (`bossbar:show_to`), offline or not.
---@return BossBar[]
function Player:bossbars() end

--- What became of the project's resource pack on their game: `"loaded"`, `"declined"`, `"failed"`, `"pending"` (accepted, still downloading), or `nil` before their game has answered (and when they're offline, or there's no resource pack).
---@return "loaded"|"declined"|"failed"|"pending"|nil
function Player:resource_pack_status() end

--- Shows them a block that isn't there: only their game draws it, and the world doesn't change. It lasts until the real block updates (something changes it, or their game reloads the chunk), or `reset_block`. They can't walk through or stand on it any differently from the real block. A block state the server doesn't have is an error.
---@param location_or_position Location|Vec3 Any point in the block; a bare vector is in their world.
---@param state string A block state, as `world:set_block` takes it: `"minecraft:gold_block"`.
---@return boolean # `false` when they're offline.
function Player:send_block_change(location_or_position, state) end

--- Shows them the real block again, after `send_block_change`.
---@param location_or_position Location|Vec3 Any point in the block; a bare vector is in their world.
---@return boolean # `false` when they're offline.
function Player:reset_block(location_or_position) end

--- Shows them another entity (or player) wearing or holding something it isn't: only their game draws it. It lasts until that entity's equipment really changes, or it goes out of their sight and comes back. An item table with a misspelled field, or an item the server doesn't have, is an error.
---@param entity Entity Whose equipment.
---@param slot "main_hand"|"off_hand"|"head"|"chest"|"legs"|"feet"|"body" Which slot.
---@param item? Item What to show there. Leave it out (or `nil`) to show it empty.
---@return boolean # `false` when they're offline or the entity has gone.
function Player:send_equipment_change(entity, slot, item) end

--- The entity they're watching through, as a spectator (`set_camera`), or `nil` when they're looking through their own eyes (or offline).
---@return Entity?
function Player:camera() end

--- Makes them watch through another entity's eyes, as a spectator does when they click one: they see what it sees until they sneak, or this is called without one. They have to be in spectator mode (`set_game_mode("spectator")`).
---@param entity? Entity Whose eyes. Leave it out (or `nil`) to give them their own back.
---@return boolean # `false` when they're offline, not in spectator mode, or the entity has gone.
function Player:set_camera(entity) end

--- Where their compasses point (those that aren't tied to a lodestone): the world's spawn unless `set_compass_target` changed it. `nil` when they're offline.
---@return Location?
function Player:compass_target() end

--- Points their compasses somewhere: a quest, a base, a meeting point. It lasts until they leave.
---@param location Location Where.
---@return boolean # `false` when they're offline.
function Player:set_compass_target(location) end

--- Opens a book on their screen, as if they'd opened a written book, without giving them one.
---@param pages Text[] Each page, MiniMessage, in order. At most 100.
---@return boolean # `false` when they're offline.
function Player:open_book(pages) end

--- How many chunks around them the server is sending: the server's own view distance, or what `set_view_distance` asked for, either of them lowered by their own game's setting. A change shows here from the next tick. `nil` when they're offline.
---@return integer?
function Player:view_distance() end

--- Changes how many chunks around them the server sends, until they leave, from the next tick. Their own game's setting still limits it: `view_distance()` says what they get.
---@param distance integer In chunks, 2 to 32.
---@return boolean # `false` when they're offline.
function Player:set_view_distance(distance) end

--- Their saved table: one per player, by id, kept across restarts and shared by every script. By convention each module keeps its own part of it under its name (`player:data().shop`), so modules don't trip over each other. It works for someone offline too (`nf.players.get` finds anyone who has joined). It may hold strings, numbers, booleans, tables of them (keyed by strings, or a list numbered from 1), and the API's own values: `Vec3`s, `Location`s, `Item` tables, and `Player`, `Entity`, `Centity` and `World` handles, which come back as the same types (a handle to something gone answers `nil` or `false`, as handles do). Anything else (a function, a `Menu` window, a `Subscription`) is skipped when it's saved, with a line in the log naming where it was (`data.inventory[3]: a function can't be saved`). The table is live: change it and the change is saved, at the next autosave (every five minutes), when the project reloads and when the server stops. It stays in memory across hot reloads rather than being read again, so a script that reloads finds it as it left it. A table that would take more than 1 MiB isn't saved (the last save stands), and the log says so. It's also saved when they leave.
---@return table
function Player:data() end

--- Calls `handler` every time `event` happens to this, until the subscription is cancelled or its script unloads, or this goes away. A handler lives as long as both the script that registered it and the handle it's on. Handlers run in the order they were registered, each with the event object; what one returns means nothing. A handler that errors is logged and the rest still run; after 20 errors in a row it's cancelled. An event this class doesn't have is an error, so a typo is caught where it was made.
---@param event Player.Event The event's name.
---@param handler fun(event: Event) Called with the event: its payload, plus `stop()`, `cancel()` and the rest of `Event`.
---@param options? EventOptions How to listen: `{ every = n }` for `tick`.
---@return Subscription # Call `:cancel()` on it to stop listening.
---@overload fun(self: Player, event: "join", handler: fun(event: PlayerJoinEvent)): Subscription
---@overload fun(self: Player, event: "quit", handler: fun(event: PlayerQuitEvent)): Subscription
---@overload fun(self: Player, event: "chat", handler: fun(event: PlayerChatEvent)): Subscription
---@overload fun(self: Player, event: "interact", handler: fun(event: PlayerInteractEvent)): Subscription
---@overload fun(self: Player, event: "interact_entity", handler: fun(event: PlayerInteractEntityEvent)): Subscription
---@overload fun(self: Player, event: "move", handler: fun(event: PlayerMoveEvent)): Subscription
---@overload fun(self: Player, event: "teleport", handler: fun(event: PlayerTeleportEvent)): Subscription
---@overload fun(self: Player, event: "change_world", handler: fun(event: PlayerChangeWorldEvent)): Subscription
---@overload fun(self: Player, event: "death", handler: fun(event: PlayerDeathEvent)): Subscription
---@overload fun(self: Player, event: "respawn", handler: fun(event: PlayerRespawnEvent)): Subscription
---@overload fun(self: Player, event: "drop_item", handler: fun(event: PlayerItemEvent)): Subscription
---@overload fun(self: Player, event: "pickup_item", handler: fun(event: PlayerPickupItemEvent)): Subscription
---@overload fun(self: Player, event: "use_item", handler: fun(event: PlayerUseItemEvent)): Subscription
---@overload fun(self: Player, event: "consume_item", handler: fun(event: PlayerItemEvent)): Subscription
---@overload fun(self: Player, event: "swap_hands", handler: fun(event: PlayerEvent)): Subscription
---@overload fun(self: Player, event: "sneak", handler: fun(event: PlayerSneakEvent)): Subscription
---@overload fun(self: Player, event: "command", handler: fun(event: PlayerCommandEvent)): Subscription
---@overload fun(self: Player, event: "input", handler: fun(event: PlayerInputEvent)): Subscription
---@overload fun(self: Player, event: "change_slot", handler: fun(event: PlayerChangeSlotEvent)): Subscription
---@overload fun(self: Player, event: "swing", handler: fun(event: PlayerSwingEvent)): Subscription
---@overload fun(self: Player, event: "sprint", handler: fun(event: PlayerSprintEvent)): Subscription
---@overload fun(self: Player, event: "fly", handler: fun(event: PlayerFlyEvent)): Subscription
---@overload fun(self: Player, event: "jump", handler: fun(event: PlayerJumpEvent)): Subscription
---@overload fun(self: Player, event: "change_game_mode", handler: fun(event: PlayerChangeGameModeEvent)): Subscription
