function World:once(event, handler) end

--- A border: the wall at the edge of a square area, which players can't walk through and which hurts them outside it. Either a world's (`world:border()`), which everyone in the world sees, or one player's own (`player:border()`), which only they see and which the server draws for them but doesn't enforce: it doesn't hurt them, and doesn't stop what isn't them. Sizes and positions are blocks; times are ticks (20 a second). Its methods answer `nil` or `false` once its world has gone, or for a player's own, once they've left or gone back to their world's (`player:reset_border()`).
---@class WorldBorder
local WorldBorder = {}

--- Its centre, with `y` 0, or `nil` once it has gone.
---@return Vec3?
function WorldBorder:center() end

--- Moves its centre. `y` is ignored. More than 29,999,984 blocks out is an error.
---@param center Vec3 Where.
---@return boolean # `false` once it has gone.
function WorldBorder:set_center(center) end

--- How wide it is, side to side, in blocks: while it's growing or shrinking, how wide it is now. `nil` once it has gone.
---@return number?
function WorldBorder:size() end

--- Changes how wide it is: at once, or moving steadily there over `options.ticks`. A size below 1 or over 59,999,968, or negative `ticks`, is an error.
---@param size number Side to side, in blocks.
---@param options? BorderSizeOptions
---@return boolean # `false` once it has gone.
function WorldBorder:set_size(size, options) end

--- How it hurts players outside it, or `nil` once it has gone.
---@return BorderDamage?
function WorldBorder:damage() end

--- Changes how it hurts players outside it; what `damage` leaves out stays as it was. A negative value is an error. A player's own border is only drawn, so this changes nothing for them.
---@param damage BorderDamage
---@return boolean # `false` once it has gone.
function WorldBorder:set_damage(damage) end

--- When players' screens turn red as it comes near, or `nil` once it has gone.
---@return BorderWarning?
function WorldBorder:warning() end

--- Changes when players' screens turn red; what `warning` leaves out stays as it was. A negative value is an error.
---@param warning BorderWarning
---@return boolean # `false` once it has gone.
function WorldBorder:set_warning(warning) end

--- Whether a place is inside it. A `Location` in another world is outside a world's border; a player's own border has no world, so only the position counts. `false` once it has gone.
---@param location_or_position Location|Vec3 Where: a `Location`, or a position.
---@return boolean
function WorldBorder:contains(location_or_position) end

--- One block position in one world. The handle is the position, not what's there: it reads the world live, so a `Block` kept from earlier sees later changes. Blocks in chunks that aren't loaded can't be read or changed: that answers `nil` or `false`, and never loads the chunk (`world:load_chunk` does).
---@class Block
local Block = {}

--- The world it's in.
---@return World
function Block:world() end

--- Its corner with the smallest x, y and z: whole numbers, in the world.
---@return Vec3
function Block:position() end

--- Its corner as a `Location`, with no facing.
---@return Location
function Block:location() end

--- What block it is, like `"minecraft:stone"`, or `nil` in an unloaded chunk.
---@return string?
function Block:kind() end

--- Its whole block state, like `"minecraft:oak_stairs[facing=east,half=bottom,shape=straight,waterlogged=false]"` (properties sorted), or `nil` in an unloaded chunk.
---@return string?
function Block:state() end

--- One property's value, like `"east"` for `facing`, or `nil` when the block has no such property or its chunk isn't loaded.
---@param name string The property: `"facing"`.
---@return string?
function Block:property(name) end

--- Every property and its value; empty for a block without any, and `nil` in an unloaded chunk.
---@return table<string, string>?
function Block:properties() end

--- Whether it's air (any kind). `false` in an unloaded chunk.
---@return boolean
function Block:is_air() end

--- Whether it's solid: something players stand on and can't walk through. `false` in an unloaded chunk.
---@return boolean
function Block:is_solid() end

--- Whether it's water or lava. `false` in an unloaded chunk.
---@return boolean
function Block:is_liquid() end

--- Changes it, as `world:set_block` does. A block state the server doesn't have is an error.
---@param state string A block state: `"minecraft:stone"`.
---@param options? BlockSetOptions
---@return boolean # `false` in an unloaded chunk.
function Block:set_state(state, options) end

--- Changes one property, keeping the rest of its state. A property the block doesn't have, or a value it can't take, is an error.
---@param name string The property: `"facing"`.
---@param value string Its new value: `"north"`.
---@return boolean # `false` in an unloaded chunk.
function Block:set_property(name, value) end

--- Breaks it as a player would: it drops what it would drop (for that tool, when given) and turns to air.
---@param tool? Item What it was broken with, for its drops: silk touch, fortune, or the wrong tool dropping nothing.
---@return boolean # Whether it broke: `false` for air, or in an unloaded chunk.
function Block:break_naturally(tool) end

--- The block at an offset from this one.
---@param offset Vec3|"up"|"down"|"north"|"south"|"east"|"west" An offset in blocks (floored), or the face of the neighbour: `"up"` is the block above.
---@return Block
function Block:relative(offset) end

--- What a container block holds (a chest, a barrel, a hopper, a furnace, a shulker box), as an `Inventory`: the same one players see. A double chest's is both halves. `nil` for a block that isn't a container, and in an unloaded chunk.
---@return Inventory?
function Block:inventory() end

--- The project block that's here, as a `CustomBlock` (the same handle: one position, one block), or `nil` when what's here isn't one of the project's blocks, or its chunk isn't loaded.
---@return CustomBlock?
function Block:custom() end

--- How bright it is there, 0 to 15: the brighter of block light and sky light. `nil` in an unloaded chunk.
---@return integer?
function Block:light_level() end

--- How much sky light reaches it, 0 to 15, whatever the time of day. `nil` in an unloaded chunk.
---@return integer?
function Block:sky_light_level() end

--- The biome here, named as `locate_biome` takes one: a biome of the project reads as its id (`"ruby_grove"`), a package's as `"acme:grove"` (to a package's own scripts, its own is bare), the game's as `"minecraft:plains"`. The game keeps biomes per 4×4×4 blocks, so neighbouring blocks share one. `nil` in an unloaded chunk.
---@return string?
function Block:biome() end

--- This position's own table for scripts to keep things in, saved with the chunk: the same table every time, shared by every script. Change it in place; it's saved when the server autosaves, when the chunk unloads and when the server stops. It stays with the position, whatever happens to the block. It may hold strings, numbers, booleans, tables of them (keyed by strings, or a list numbered from 1), and the API's own values: `Vec3`s, `Location`s, `Item` tables, and `Player`, `Entity`, `Centity` and `World` handles, which come back as the same types (a handle to something gone answers `nil` or `false`, as handles do). Anything else (a function, a `Menu` window, a `Subscription`) is skipped when it's saved, with a line in the log naming where it was (`data.inventory[3]: a function can't be saved`). `nil` in an unloaded chunk.
---@return table?
function Block:data() end

--- A block of the project's own at a position, which is a `Block` like any other: it reads the world live, so one kept from earlier turns into whatever is there now (`block:custom()` says whether it still is one). The game holds it as a note block state its resource pack draws as the block, so `kind()` and `state()` answer that; `id()` and `project()` say which block it is. `data()` is its own table: it starts empty when the block is placed and is gone when the block is, whatever replaced it. `break_naturally` rolls its loot table; replacing or filling over it with `set_state` or `world:fill_blocks` removes it without drops.
---@class CustomBlock: Block
local CustomBlock = {}

--- Its id: its project block's folder name under `blocks/`, or `namespace:id` for one a package it depends on exports, as the calling script names it. `nil` once what's here isn't a block of the project's.
---@return string?
function CustomBlock:id() end

--- The project block it's one of, or `nil` once what's here isn't one.
---@return ProjectBlock?
function CustomBlock:project() end

--- A project block from `blocks/<id>/block.json`: `this` in the block's script, and what `nf.blocks.get` hands out. It's the kind of block, not one placed in the world: its handlers hear what players do with any block of it (see `CustomBlock` for one placed). If the block is deleted, its methods answer `nil` or `false` and its handlers go.
---@class ProjectBlock
local ProjectBlock = {}

--- Its id: its folder name under `blocks/`.
---@return string
function ProjectBlock:id() end

--- Whether the project still has it.
---@return boolean
function ProjectBlock:exists() end

--- Places one at the block position a location is in, as `world:set_block` would: whatever is there is replaced, and nothing drops. Its `place` handlers are not asked (a script placing a block isn't a player placing one), and it isn't a `block_place` either. If the block has a centity, it's spawned over the block. `nil` when the chunk isn't loaded, or when the game has no note block state left to hold the block in (the project has more blocks than that).
---@param location Location Where.
---@return CustomBlock?
function ProjectBlock:place(location) end

--- Calls `handler` every time `event` happens to this, until the subscription is cancelled or its script unloads, or this goes away. A handler lives as long as both the script that registered it and the handle it's on. Handlers run in the order they were registered, each with the event object; what one returns means nothing. A handler that errors is logged and the rest still run; after 20 errors in a row it's cancelled. An event this class doesn't have is an error, so a typo is caught where it was made.
---@param event ProjectBlock.Event The event's name.
---@param handler fun(event: Event) Called with the event: its payload, plus `stop()`, `cancel()` and the rest of `Event`.
---@param options? EventOptions How to listen: `{ every = n }` for `tick`.
---@return Subscription # Call `:cancel()` on it to stop listening.
---@overload fun(self: ProjectBlock, event: "place", handler: fun(event: ProjectBlockPlaceEvent)): Subscription
---@overload fun(self: ProjectBlock, event: "break", handler: fun(event: BlockBreakEvent)): Subscription
---@overload fun(self: ProjectBlock, event: "click", handler: fun(event: ProjectBlockClickEvent)): Subscription
---@overload fun(self: ProjectBlock, event: "tick", handler: fun(event: ProjectBlockTickEvent)): Subscription
function ProjectBlock:on(event, handler, options) end

--- Like `block:on`, but the handler runs only the next time the event happens, then stops listening.
---@param event ProjectBlock.Event The event's name.
---@param handler fun(event: Event) Called with the event.
---@return Subscription # Call `:cancel()` on it to stop waiting before then.
---@overload fun(self: ProjectBlock, event: "place", handler: fun(event: ProjectBlockPlaceEvent)): Subscription
---@overload fun(self: ProjectBlock, event: "break", handler: fun(event: BlockBreakEvent)): Subscription
---@overload fun(self: ProjectBlock, event: "click", handler: fun(event: ProjectBlockClickEvent)): Subscription
---@overload fun(self: ProjectBlock, event: "tick", handler: fun(event: ProjectBlockTickEvent)): Subscription
function ProjectBlock:once(event, handler) end

--- A real inventory: a player's (`player:inventory()`), their ender chest, a container block's (`block:inventory()`: a chest, a barrel, a hopper, a furnace) or an entity's (a chest boat, a donkey, a villager). Its item methods work like `Menu`'s. The handle is where the inventory is, so it reads live; once that's gone (a chest broken, a player offline, a chunk unloaded) its methods answer `nil`, `false` or nothing. Slots are numbered from 0. A player's: the hotbar 0 to 8, the rest of the main inventory 9 to 35, armour 36 (feet), 37 (legs), 38 (chest) and 39 (head), the offhand 40, then body armour 41 and a saddle 42 (what the game keeps there for any entity). A double chest's inventory is both halves, 0 to 53.
---@class Inventory
local Inventory = {}

--- Whether it's there now: the player online, the container still a container, the entity in the world and its chunk loaded.
---@return boolean
function Inventory:exists() end

--- What kind of inventory it is: `"player"`, `"ender_chest"`, `"chest"`, `"barrel"`, `"shulker_box"`, `"hopper"`, `"dispenser"`, `"dropper"`, `"furnace"`, `"brewing"`, … `nil` once it's gone.
---@return string?
function Inventory:kind() end

--- How many slots it has: 43 for a player, 27 for a chest, 54 for a double chest. 0 once it's gone.
---@return integer
function Inventory:size() end

--- Whose it is: the `Player` (for a player's inventory and ender chest), the `Entity`, or the `Block`. `nil` once it's gone.
---@return Entity|Block|nil
function Inventory:holder() end

--- What is in a slot, or `nil` for an empty one (or once it's gone). A slot outside it is an error.
---@param index integer From 0.
---@return Item?
function Inventory:item(index) end

--- Puts an item in a slot, replacing what was there, or empties it. A slot outside it, an item table with a misspelled field, or an item the server doesn't have is an error.
---@param index integer From 0.
---@param item? Item Leave it out (or `nil`) to empty the slot.
---@return boolean # `false` once it's gone.
function Inventory:set_item(index, item) end

--- Every item in it, keyed by slot. Empty slots are left out, so `pairs` visits only what is there.
---@return table<integer, Item>
function Inventory:items() end

--- Puts an item wherever it fits, topping up matching stacks first, the way picking something up does. In a player's inventory only the hotbar and main inventory take it, never armour or the offhand.
---@param item Item
---@return integer # How many didn't fit: 0 when all of it went in, all of it once the inventory's gone.
function Inventory:add_item(item) end

--- Takes away items that match, from the first slot on, until `count` are gone or none are left.
---@param match string|ItemMatch An item kind (`"minecraft:diamond"`), or a partial `Item` that matches a stack when every field it gives matches: `{ kind = "minecraft:paper", name = "<gold>Ticket" }`. Its `data` matches as a subset, so `{ data = { coin = true } }` finds coins whatever else they carry. `count` and `raw` have no place in a match; a field `Item` doesn't have is an error.
---@param count? integer How many at most. Leave it out for every one that matches.
---@return integer # How many it took.
function Inventory:remove_item(match, count) end

--- How many items match, across every slot.
---@param match string|ItemMatch An item kind (`"minecraft:diamond"`), or a partial `Item` that matches a stack when every field it gives matches: `{ kind = "minecraft:paper", name = "<gold>Ticket" }`. Its `data` matches as a subset, so `{ data = { coin = true } }` finds coins whatever else they carry. `count` and `raw` have no place in a match; a field `Item` doesn't have is an error.
---@return integer
function Inventory:count_item(match) end

--- Whether at least `count` items match.
---@param match string|ItemMatch An item kind (`"minecraft:diamond"`), or a partial `Item` that matches a stack when every field it gives matches: `{ kind = "minecraft:paper", name = "<gold>Ticket" }`. Its `data` matches as a subset, so `{ data = { coin = true } }` finds coins whatever else they carry. `count` and `raw` have no place in a match; a field `Item` doesn't have is an error.
---@param count? integer Default 1.
---@return boolean
function Inventory:has_item(match, count) end

--- The first slot holding an item that matches, or `nil` when none does.
---@param match string|ItemMatch An item kind (`"minecraft:diamond"`), or a partial `Item` that matches a stack when every field it gives matches: `{ kind = "minecraft:paper", name = "<gold>Ticket" }`. Its `data` matches as a subset, so `{ data = { coin = true } }` finds coins whatever else they carry. `count` and `raw` have no place in a match; a field `Item` doesn't have is an error.
---@return integer?
function Inventory:first_slot(match) end

--- Empties every slot. To empty one, `set_item(index, nil)`.
---@return boolean # `false` once it's gone.
function Inventory:clear() end

--- The players who have it open on their screen now. (A player's own inventory, open in their own inventory screen, doesn't count: the server can't tell.)
---@return Player[]
function Inventory:viewers() end

--- A bar across the top of players' screens, made by `nf.bossbars.create`. It shows to the players it's shown to (`show_to`), and stays theirs when they leave and come back. It belongs to the script that made it and goes when that script unloads (or `remove()`): from then on its methods answer `nil` or `false`.
---@class BossBar
local BossBar = {}

--- Whether it's still there: `false` once it's removed or its script has unloaded.
---@return boolean
function BossBar:exists() end

--- Shows it to a player, now if they're online and again whenever they join.
---@param player Player
---@return boolean # `false` once it's gone.
function BossBar:show_to(player) end

--- Stops showing it to a player.
---@param player Player
---@return boolean # Whether they were among its viewers: `false` once it's gone.
function BossBar:hide_from(player) end

--- Everyone it's shown to, online or not. Empty once it's gone.
---@return Player[]
function BossBar:viewers() end

--- What it says, as MiniMessage, or `nil` once it's gone.
---@return Text?
function BossBar:text() end

--- Changes what it says.
---@param text Text MiniMessage.
---@return boolean # `false` once it's gone.
function BossBar:set_text(text) end

--- How full it is, 0 to 1, or `nil` once it's gone.
---@return number?
function BossBar:progress() end

--- Fills it to a fraction. Outside 0 to 1 is an error.
---@param progress number 0 to 1.
---@return boolean # `false` once it's gone.
function BossBar:set_progress(progress) end

--- Its colour, or `nil` once it's gone.
---@return "pink"|"blue"|"red"|"green"|"yellow"|"purple"|"white"|nil
function BossBar:color() end

--- Changes its colour.
---@param color "pink"|"blue"|"red"|"green"|"yellow"|"purple"|"white"
---@return boolean # `false` once it's gone.
function BossBar:set_color(color) end

--- Whether it's one bar or split into notches, or `nil` once it's gone.
---@return "progress"|"notched_6"|"notched_10"|"notched_12"|"notched_20"|nil
function BossBar:style() end

--- Makes it one bar, or splits it into 6, 10, 12 or 20 notches.
---@param style "progress"|"notched_6"|"notched_10"|"notched_12"|"notched_20"
---@return boolean # `false` once it's gone.
function BossBar:set_style(style) end

--- Takes it off every screen for good.
---@return boolean # `false` once it's gone already.
function BossBar:remove() end

--- The panel of lines at the right of one player's screen, from `player:sidebar()`: up to 15 lines of MiniMessage under a title, without the score numbers vanilla shows. Setting its title or lines shows it. A sidebar needs a scoreboard of the player's own, so while it shows NetherForge gives them one that copies the server's teams and the lines under name tags (the project's own changes at once, anyone else's within a second), keeping team colours and name tags working; hiding it gives them the server's back. It's cleared when they leave. For someone offline its methods answer `nil` or `false`.
---@class Sidebar
local Sidebar = {}

--- Its title, as MiniMessage, or `nil` when it has none (or they're offline).
---@return Text?
function Sidebar:title() end

--- Changes its title, and shows it.
---@param text Text MiniMessage.
---@return boolean # `false` when they're offline.
function Sidebar:set_title(text) end

--- Its lines, top first, as MiniMessage. Empty when it has none (or they're offline).
---@return Text[]
function Sidebar:lines() end

--- Replaces its lines, and shows it. More than 15 is an error.
---@param lines Text[] MiniMessage, top first. Lines may repeat; an empty string is a blank line.
---@return boolean # `false` when they're offline.
function Sidebar:set_lines(lines) end

--- Whether they see it now. `false` when they're offline.
---@return boolean
function Sidebar:is_visible() end

--- Shows it, or hides it and gives them the server's scoreboard back, keeping its title and lines for next time.
---@param visible boolean
---@return boolean # `false` when they're offline.
function Sidebar:set_visible(visible) end

--- Takes its title and lines away and hides it, giving them the server's scoreboard back.
---@return boolean # `false` when they're offline.
function Sidebar:clear() end

--- A scoreboard team, made by `nf.teams.create`: what goes before and after its members' names (over their heads and in the player list), the colour of those names and of their glow, whether members can hurt each other, see each other while invisible, see name tags and push each other. Members are players and other entities. Teams live on the server's scoreboard, so every player sees them (while a player's sidebar shows, its scoreboard copies them). A team belongs to the script that made it and goes when that script unloads (a reload, the server stopping), members and all, so the script's body makes it again; from then on its methods answer `nil` or `false`. On the server's scoreboard it's called `nf.<name>` (what `/team` commands name it), so it never collides with a team made with `/team` or by another plugin, and NetherForge never touches those.
---@class Team
local Team = {}

--- Its name, as `nf.teams.create` was given it (without the `nf.` the scoreboard adds).
---@return string
function Team:name() end

--- Whether it's still there: `false` once it's removed or its script has unloaded.
---@return boolean
function Team:exists() end

--- Puts a player or entity in it, taking them out of any other team they're in (one team at a time is Minecraft's rule). A player stays in it when they leave and come back, and can be added while offline.
---@param member Entity A player or any other entity.
---@return boolean # `false` once it's gone, or once the entity has.
function Team:add_member(member) end

--- Takes a player or entity out of it.
---@param member Entity A player or any other entity.
---@return boolean # Whether they were in it: `false` once it's gone.
function Team:remove_member(member) end

--- Whether a player or entity is in it. `false` once it's gone.
---@param member Entity A player or any other entity.
---@return boolean
function Team:has_member(member) end

--- Its members that are here: players online (as `Player`) and entities in loaded chunks. Empty once it has gone.
---@return Entity[]
function Team:members() end

--- What it's called where Minecraft names a team (the `/team list` output), as MiniMessage; `nil` once it's gone.
---@return Text?
function Team:display_name() end

--- Changes what it's called.
---@param text Text MiniMessage.
---@return boolean # `false` once it's gone.
function Team:set_display_name(text) end

--- What goes before each member's name, over their head and in the player list, as MiniMessage; `nil` once it's gone.
---@return Text?
function Team:prefix() end

--- Changes what goes before each member's name.
---@param text Text MiniMessage; `""` for none.
---@return boolean # `false` once it's gone.
function Team:set_prefix(text) end

--- What goes after each member's name, as MiniMessage; `nil` once it's gone.
---@return Text?
function Team:suffix() end

--- Changes what goes after each member's name.
---@param text Text MiniMessage; `""` for none.
---@return boolean # `false` once it's gone.
function Team:set_suffix(text) end

--- The colour of its members' names, and of their outline when they glow; `nil` when it has none (or once it's gone).
---@return "black"|"dark_blue"|"dark_green"|"dark_aqua"|"dark_red"|"dark_purple"|"gold"|"gray"|"dark_gray"|"blue"|"green"|"aqua"|"red"|"light_purple"|"yellow"|"white"|nil
function Team:color() end

--- Colours its members' names, and their outline when they glow (a glowing entity's outline is white otherwise). A prefix's own colours still win over it for the prefix.
---@param color? "black"|"dark_blue"|"dark_green"|"dark_aqua"|"dark_red"|"dark_purple"|"gold"|"gray"|"dark_gray"|"blue"|"green"|"aqua"|"red"|"light_purple"|"yellow"|"white" Leave it out for none.
---@return boolean # `false` once it's gone.
function Team:set_color(color) end

--- Whether members can hurt each other. `false` once it's gone.
---@return boolean
function Team:has_friendly_fire() end

--- Lets members hurt each other, or stops them.
---@param enabled boolean
---@return boolean # `false` once it's gone.
function Team:set_friendly_fire(enabled) end

--- Whether members see each other, faintly, while invisible. `false` once it's gone.
---@return boolean
function Team:can_see_invisible_teammates() end

--- Lets members see each other while invisible, or stops them.
---@param enabled boolean
---@return boolean # `false` once it's gone.
function Team:set_can_see_invisible_teammates(enabled) end

--- Who sees its members' name tags; `nil` once it's gone.
---@return "always"|"never"|"hide_for_other_teams"|"hide_for_own_team"|nil
function Team:nametags() end

--- Changes who sees its members' name tags.
---@param visibility "always"|"never"|"hide_for_other_teams"|"hide_for_own_team" `"always"` (the default), `"never"`, `"hide_for_other_teams"` (only teammates see them) or `"hide_for_own_team"` (only players outside the team see them).
---@return boolean # `false` once it's gone.
function Team:set_nametags(visibility) end

--- Who its members push, and are pushed by; `nil` once it's gone.
---@return "always"|"never"|"push_other_teams"|"push_own_team"|nil
function Team:collision() end

--- Changes who its members push, and are pushed by.
---@param rule "always"|"never"|"push_other_teams"|"push_own_team" `"always"` (the default), `"never"`, `"push_other_teams"` (members pass through each other) or `"push_own_team"` (members push only each other).
---@return boolean # `false` once it's gone.
function Team:set_collision(rule) end

--- Takes it off the scoreboard for good; its members are in no team any more.
---@return boolean # `false` once it's gone already.
function Team:remove() end

--- A database, from `nf.db()` or `nf.db('network')`. `nf.db()` is this package's own: one SQLite file per package, kept across restarts, which every other package (a dependency, or the project using one) never sees; its tables are made by the package's `migrations/NNN_name.sql` files, applied in order when the project loads. `nf.db('network')` (a name) is a connection to a MySQL or PostgreSQL server that the server's owner set up under `databases:` in the plugin's `config.yml`: shared by every package the owner lists for it, so give tables a name no other package would (`shop_scores`), create them yourself (`CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS`: migrations belong to the package's own database only), and write the SQL in the server's dialect. The handle is the same either way: the same calls, `?` and `params`, rows and `value, err` answers. Every function is asynchronous (see `nf.task`): the work runs on a background thread, so a slow query never holds up the server, and one database runs its work in the order it was given, so a task's `db:execute` is always done before its next `db:query`. Use it from a task (`local rows, err = db:query(...)`) or give a callback. A mistake in the SQL, a constraint a row breaks or a database that couldn't be migrated is `nil, err`, never a thrown error.
---@class Database
local Database = {}

--- Runs a statement that reads rows and gives them all. Each row is a table keyed by column name; a `NULL` column is left out of its row (so `row.nickname` is `nil`), integers come as `integer`s, reals as `number`s and text as `string`s; what a MySQL or PostgreSQL server has beyond those arrives as one of them (a boolean as `1` or `0`, a `DECIMAL` as a `number`, a date or time as its text, an integer too big for an `integer` as text; `CAST(column AS CHAR)` keeps a decimal exact). A column that holds a blob is an error: `hex(column)` it in the SQL (`encode(column, 'hex')` on PostgreSQL). One SQL statement in the database's own dialect (SQLite's for the package's own database; MySQL's or PostgreSQL's for a connection the server owner set up), with `?` where a value goes: the values come in `params`, never inside the text, so a name a player typed can't become SQL. A second statement after a `;` is an error: use `db:transaction` for several. On PostgreSQL a literal `?` (the JSON operators) is written `??`. It works off the main thread, so its rows arrives later: give `callback`, which is called with `rows, err` once it's done, or call it inside a task (`nf.task`) without one, and it waits and returns `rows, err`. Without a callback outside a task, it's an error. When it fails, `rows` is `nil` and `err` says why.
---@param sql string The statement: `"SELECT name, score FROM scores WHERE score > ?"`.
---@param params? table The values for the `?`s, in order: `{ player_name, 5 }`. Each is a `string`, `number`, `integer` or `boolean` (stored as 1 or 0); a hole in the list (`{ 1, nil, 3 }`) is SQL `NULL`, and so are the last `?`s when the list is shorter (Lua can't tell `{ name, nil }` from `{ name }`). Any other kind of value is an error, and more values than `?`s fails the statement.
---@param callback? fun(rows: table<string, any>[]?, err: string?) Called on the main thread once it's done, as this script's code, with the rows, in the order the statement gave them (empty when there are none) (or `nil` and why not). Not called if the script has stopped by then.
---@return table<string, any>[]? # Only without a callback, in a task: the rows, in the order the statement gave them (empty when there are none), or `nil`.
---@return string? # Why it failed, when it did; `nil` when it worked.
---@overload async fun(self: Database, sql: string, params?: table): table<string, any>[]?, string?
---@overload fun(self: Database, sql: string, params?: table, callback: fun(rows: table<string, any>[]?, err: string?))
function Database:query(sql, params, callback) end

--- Runs a statement that changes data (`INSERT`, `UPDATE`, `DELETE`) and says what it changed. It's committed on its own. One SQL statement in the database's own dialect (SQLite's for the package's own database; MySQL's or PostgreSQL's for a connection the server owner set up), with `?` where a value goes: the values come in `params`, never inside the text, so a name a player typed can't become SQL. A second statement after a `;` is an error: use `db:transaction` for several. On PostgreSQL a literal `?` (the JSON operators) is written `??`. It works off the main thread, so its result arrives later: give `callback`, which is called with `result, err` once it's done, or call it inside a task (`nf.task`) without one, and it waits and returns `result, err`. Without a callback outside a task, it's an error. When it fails, `result` is `nil` and `err` says why.
---@param sql string The statement: `"INSERT INTO scores (name, score) VALUES (?, ?)"`.
---@param params? table The values for the `?`s, in order: `{ player_name, 5 }`. Each is a `string`, `number`, `integer` or `boolean` (stored as 1 or 0); a hole in the list (`{ 1, nil, 3 }`) is SQL `NULL`, and so are the last `?`s when the list is shorter (Lua can't tell `{ name, nil }` from `{ name }`). Any other kind of value is an error, and more values than `?`s fails the statement.
---@param callback? fun(result: DatabaseResult?, err: string?) Called on the main thread once it's done, as this script's code, with how many rows it changed and the last row it inserted (or `nil` and why not). Not called if the script has stopped by then.
---@return DatabaseResult? # Only without a callback, in a task: how many rows it changed and the last row it inserted, or `nil`.
---@return string? # Why it failed, when it did; `nil` when it worked.
---@overload async fun(self: Database, sql: string, params?: table): DatabaseResult?, string?
---@overload fun(self: Database, sql: string, params?: table, callback: fun(result: DatabaseResult?, err: string?))
function Database:execute(sql, params, callback) end

--- Runs several statements as one: all of them happen, or, when one fails, none do (the ones before it are undone) and the error says which failed. Other work on the database waits until it is done, so nothing sees it half-way. It works off the main thread, so its results arrives later: give `callback`, which is called with `results, err` once it's done, or call it inside a task (`nf.task`) without one, and it waits and returns `results, err`. Without a callback outside a task, it's an error. When it fails, `results` is `nil` and `err` says why.
---@param statements DatabaseStatement[] The statements, run in order. Rows they would give are ignored: a transaction changes data.
---@param callback? fun(results: DatabaseResult[]?, err: string?) Called on the main thread once it's done, as this script's code, with what each statement changed, in the same order (or `nil` and why not). Not called if the script has stopped by then.
---@return DatabaseResult[]? # Only without a callback, in a task: what each statement changed, in the same order, or `nil`.
---@return string? # Why it failed, when it did; `nil` when it worked.
---@overload async fun(self: Database, statements: DatabaseStatement[]): DatabaseResult[]?, string?
---@overload fun(self: Database, statements: DatabaseStatement[], callback: fun(results: DatabaseResult[]?, err: string?))
function Database:transaction(statements, callback) end

--- A live menu window from `menus/<id>/`: `this` in the menu's script, and what `player:open_menu`, `nf.menus.shared` and every menu event hand out. One that isn't `shared` exists from the moment it's opened for someone until its last viewer closes it; after that its methods return `nil` or `false` rather than erroring, and its handlers (and its slots') are gone. A shared one lives as long as the project.
---@class Menu
local Menu = {}

--- This window's id: the menu's own id for a shared one, a fresh one for each window of a menu that isn't.
---@return string
function Menu:id() end

--- Which menu it is: its folder name under `menus/`. Nil once the window is gone, and for a window made from a `MenuTemplate` (see `template()`).
---@return string?
function Menu:kind() end

--- The `MenuTemplate` this window was made from (`player:open_menu(template)`), or `nil` for a window of a menu file, and once the window or the template is gone.
---@return MenuTemplate?
function Menu:template() end

--- Whether the window is still there. One that isn't shared goes when its last viewer closes it.
---@return boolean
function Menu:exists() end

--- Whether this is the one window the whole server shares.
---@return boolean
function Menu:is_shared() end

--- What the script that opened this window passed along: `player:open_menu(menu, { context = value })`. The very value, not a copy, so a table can carry anything (the item being bought, a callback, the centity that was clicked). Every event on the window carries it too, as `event.context`. `nil` when nothing was passed, and always for a shared window, which nobody opens with a context.
---@return any
function Menu:context() end

--- How many slots it has. Slots are numbered from 0, left to right, then down.
---@return integer
function Menu:size() end

--- How many rows of slots it has: a chest's 1 to 6, a hopper's 1, a dispenser's 3. `size() / rows()` is how many are in a row.
---@return integer
function Menu:rows() end

--- The title, as MiniMessage, without the skin drawn in front of it.
---@return Text?
function Menu:title() end

--- Renames the window. Minecraft can't rename a window that's open, so everyone looking sees it close and open again: fine between clicks, wrong every tick.
---@param text Text MiniMessage.
---@return boolean # `false` once the window is gone.
function Menu:set_title(text) end

--- The resource pack skin drawn behind it, as `<pack>/<key>`, or `nil` for the vanilla look.
---@return string?
function Menu:skin() end

--- Changes the background. A skin is drawn in the title, so this costs the same close-and-open `set_title` does. A skin the project's resource packs don't have is an error.
---@param skin? SkinRef `<pack>/<key>`, or leave it out for the vanilla look.
---@return boolean # `false` once the window is gone.
function Menu:set_skin(skin) end

--- Whether clicks are kept from moving items into or out of it: such a click reaches handlers already cancelled. Scripts hear the clicks either way.
---@return boolean
function Menu:is_locked() end

--- Locks or unlocks this window. Not saved: a reload goes back to what the file says.
---@param locked boolean
function Menu:set_locked(locked) end

--- One of its slots, as a handle. A slot outside the window is an error. A handle is the window's id and the index, so it's fine to keep while the window lasts.
---@param index integer From 0.
---@return Slot
function Menu:slot(index) end

--- What is in a slot, or `nil` for an empty one. A slot outside the window is an error.
---@param index integer From 0.
---@return Item?
function Menu:item(index) end

--- Puts an item in a slot, replacing what was there, or empties it. An item table with a misspelled field, or an item id the server doesn't have, is an error.
---@param index integer From 0.
---@param item? Item Leave it out (or `nil`) to empty the slot.
---@return boolean # `false` once the window is gone.
function Menu:set_item(index, item) end

--- Puts an item wherever it fits, topping up matching stacks first, the way picking something up does.
---@param item Item
---@return integer # How many didn't fit: 0 when all of it went in.
function Menu:add_item(item) end

--- Every item in it, keyed by slot. Empty slots are left out, so `pairs` visits only what is there.
---@return table<integer, Item>
function Menu:items() end

--- Puts several items in at once, by slot, replacing what was in those slots; the others are left alone. Every item is checked before any slot changes, so a mistake (a slot outside the window, a misspelled field, an item the server doesn't have) is an error that leaves the window as it was.
---@param items table<integer, Item> Items by slot, from 0: `{ [11] = bread, [15] = cake }`.
---@return boolean # `false` once the window is gone.
function Menu:set_items(items) end

--- Puts a copy of one item in every empty slot, or in each of the listed slots (empty or not): a border of glass panes, say. A slot outside the window is an error.
---@param item Item
---@param indices? integer[] The slots to fill, from 0. Leave it out for every empty one.
---@return boolean # `false` once the window is gone.
function Menu:fill(item, indices) end

--- Empties every slot. To empty one, `set_item(index, nil)`.
---@return boolean # `false` once the window is gone.
function Menu:clear() end

--- Everyone looking at it now.
---@return Player[]
function Menu:viewers() end

--- What a viewer is carrying on their cursor while they have this window open, or `nil` for nothing (and when they aren't looking at it).
---@param player Player
---@return Item?
function Menu:cursor_item(player) end

--- Puts an item on a viewer's cursor, replacing what they were carrying, or empties it. What's on the cursor goes back to their inventory (or drops) when they close the window.
---@param player Player
---@param item? Item Leave it out (or `nil`) to empty the cursor.
---@return boolean # `false` when they aren't looking at this window or the window is gone.
function Menu:set_cursor_item(player, item) end

--- Sends the whole window to everyone looking at it again. Changes are sent by themselves, so this is only for when a viewer's screen may be out of step: after a cancelled click left a ghost item on it, say.
---@return boolean # `false` once the window is gone.
function Menu:refresh() end

--- Shows this very window to someone too: a second pair of eyes on one menu. Its `open` handlers hear it. To give someone a window of their own, use `player:open_menu`.
---@param player Player
---@return boolean # `false` when they're offline or the window is gone.
function Menu:open_for(player) end

--- Closes it for one viewer.
---@param player Player
---@return boolean # `false` when the window is gone or they weren't looking at it.
function Menu:close_for(player) end

--- Closes it for everyone looking at it.
---@return boolean # `false` once the window is gone.
function Menu:close_all() end

--- Calls `handler` every time `event` happens to this, until the subscription is cancelled or its script unloads, or this goes away. A handler lives as long as both the script that registered it and the handle it's on. Handlers run in the order they were registered, each with the event object; what one returns means nothing. A handler that errors is logged and the rest still run; after 20 errors in a row it's cancelled. An event this class doesn't have is an error, so a typo is caught where it was made.
---@param event Menu.Event The event's name.
---@param handler fun(event: Event) Called with the event: its payload, plus `stop()`, `cancel()` and the rest of `Event`.
---@param options? EventOptions How to listen: `{ every = n }` for `tick`.
---@return Subscription # Call `:cancel()` on it to stop listening.
---@overload fun(self: Menu, event: "open", handler: fun(event: MenuEvent)): Subscription
---@overload fun(self: Menu, event: "close", handler: fun(event: MenuEvent)): Subscription
---@overload fun(self: Menu, event: "click", handler: fun(event: MenuClickEvent)): Subscription
---@overload fun(self: Menu, event: "drag", handler: fun(event: MenuDragEvent)): Subscription
function Menu:on(event, handler, options) end

--- Like `menu:on`, but the handler runs only the next time the event happens, then stops listening.
---@param event Menu.Event The event's name.
---@param handler fun(event: Event) Called with the event.
---@return Subscription # Call `:cancel()` on it to stop waiting before then.
---@overload fun(self: Menu, event: "open", handler: fun(event: MenuEvent)): Subscription
---@overload fun(self: Menu, event: "close", handler: fun(event: MenuEvent)): Subscription
---@overload fun(self: Menu, event: "click", handler: fun(event: MenuClickEvent)): Subscription
---@overload fun(self: Menu, event: "drag", handler: fun(event: MenuDragEvent)): Subscription
function Menu:once(event, handler) end

--- One slot of one menu window, from `menu:slot(index)` (`this:slot(index)` in the menu's script). After the window goes, its methods return `nil` or `false` rather than erroring, and its handlers are gone.
---@class Slot
local Slot = {}

--- Its number in the window, from 0.
---@return integer
function Slot:index() end

--- The window it belongs to.
---@return Menu
function Slot:menu() end

--- What is in it, or `nil` when it's empty or the window is gone.
---@return Item?
function Slot:item() end

--- Puts an item in it, replacing what was there, or empties it. An item table with a misspelled field, or an item id the server doesn't have, is an error.
---@param item? Item Leave it out (or `nil`) to empty the slot.
---@return boolean # `false` once the window is gone.
function Slot:set_item(item) end

--- Calls `handler` every time `event` happens to this, until the subscription is cancelled or its script unloads, or this goes away. A handler lives as long as both the script that registered it and the handle it's on. Handlers run in the order they were registered, each with the event object; what one returns means nothing. A handler that errors is logged and the rest still run; after 20 errors in a row it's cancelled. An event this class doesn't have is an error, so a typo is caught where it was made.
---@param event Slot.Event The event's name.
---@param handler fun(event: MenuClickEvent) Called with the event: its payload, plus `stop()`, `cancel()` and the rest of `Event`.
---@param options? EventOptions How to listen: `{ every = n }` for `tick`.
---@return Subscription # Call `:cancel()` on it to stop listening.
function Slot:on(event, handler, options) end

--- Like `slot:on`, but the handler runs only the next time the event happens, then stops listening.
---@param event Slot.Event The event's name.
---@param handler fun(event: MenuClickEvent) Called with the event.
---@return Subscription # Call `:cancel()` on it to stop waiting before then.
function Slot:once(event, handler) end

--- A menu made by a script with `nf.menus.create` rather than read from `menus/<id>/`: what a window opened from it starts as. Open a window of it with `player:open_menu(template)`, as many as you like, each a `Menu` of its own; handlers on the template hear every one of its windows (after the window's own handlers). It lives as long as the script that created it: when that script unloads, or `remove()` is called, its windows close and its handlers go, and its methods answer `nil` or `false` rather than erroring.
---@class MenuTemplate
local MenuTemplate = {}

--- Its id, made up when it was created. It never names a folder under `menus/`, and it's only good while the template lasts.
---@return string
function MenuTemplate:id() end

--- Whether it's still there: until `remove()`, or until the script that created it unloads.
---@return boolean
function MenuTemplate:exists() end

--- How many slots its windows have.
---@return integer
function MenuTemplate:size() end

--- How many rows of slots its windows have: a chest's 1 to 6, a hopper's 1, a dispenser's 3.
---@return integer
function MenuTemplate:rows() end

--- The title its windows start with, as MiniMessage, without the skin.
---@return Text?
function MenuTemplate:title() end

--- The resource pack skin its windows start with, as `<pack>/<key>`, or `nil` for the vanilla look.
---@return string?
function MenuTemplate:skin() end

--- Whether its windows start locked: clicks that would move items into or out of them reach handlers already cancelled.
---@return boolean
function MenuTemplate:is_locked() end

--- What a slot of a new window starts with, or `nil` for an empty one. A slot outside its windows is an error.
---@param index integer From 0.
---@return Item?
function MenuTemplate:item(index) end

--- Every window made from it that is still open.
---@return Menu[]
function MenuTemplate:windows() end

--- Takes it away before its script unloads: its windows close, and its handlers go.
---@return boolean # `false` when it was already gone.
function MenuTemplate:remove() end

--- Calls `handler` every time `event` happens to this, until the subscription is cancelled or its script unloads, or this goes away. A handler lives as long as both the script that registered it and the handle it's on. Handlers run in the order they were registered, each with the event object; what one returns means nothing. A handler that errors is logged and the rest still run; after 20 errors in a row it's cancelled. An event this class doesn't have is an error, so a typo is caught where it was made.
---@param event MenuTemplate.Event The event's name.
---@param handler fun(event: Event) Called with the event: its payload, plus `stop()`, `cancel()` and the rest of `Event`.
---@param options? EventOptions How to listen: `{ every = n }` for `tick`.
---@return Subscription # Call `:cancel()` on it to stop listening.
---@overload fun(self: MenuTemplate, event: "open", handler: fun(event: MenuEvent)): Subscription
---@overload fun(self: MenuTemplate, event: "close", handler: fun(event: MenuEvent)): Subscription
---@overload fun(self: MenuTemplate, event: "click", handler: fun(event: MenuClickEvent)): Subscription
---@overload fun(self: MenuTemplate, event: "drag", handler: fun(event: MenuDragEvent)): Subscription
function MenuTemplate:on(event, handler, options) end

--- Like `template:on`, but the handler runs only the next time the event happens, then stops listening.
---@param event MenuTemplate.Event The event's name.
---@param handler fun(event: Event) Called with the event.
---@return Subscription # Call `:cancel()` on it to stop waiting before then.
---@overload fun(self: MenuTemplate, event: "open", handler: fun(event: MenuEvent)): Subscription
---@overload fun(self: MenuTemplate, event: "close", handler: fun(event: MenuEvent)): Subscription
---@overload fun(self: MenuTemplate, event: "click", handler: fun(event: MenuClickEvent)): Subscription
---@overload fun(self: MenuTemplate, event: "drag", handler: fun(event: MenuDragEvent)): Subscription
function MenuTemplate:once(event, handler) end

--- A dialog from `dialogs/<id>/`: `this` in the dialog's script, and what `nf.dialogs.get` returns. A dialog holds nothing, so there is one of each for the whole server and showing it is all there is to do with it. Every button runs Lua with the answers to the inputs in hand; none of them is a command. Handlers on it last while the project has the dialog, across reloads of it. One made by `nf.dialogs.create` works the same way, and lasts as long as the script that made it.
---@class Dialog
local Dialog = {}

--- Its folder name under `dialogs/`, or for one from `nf.dialogs.create`, an id made up for it.
---@return string
function Dialog:id() end

--- Whether the project still has this dialog (a reload can take it away), or, for one from `nf.dialogs.create`, whether the script that made it is still running.
---@return boolean
function Dialog:exists() end

--- Its title, as MiniMessage.
---@return Text?
function Dialog:title() end

--- The button keys, in the order they are drawn: what `event.key` can be.
---@return string[]
function Dialog:buttons() end

--- The input keys, in the order they are asked: the keys of `event.values`.
---@return string[]
function Dialog:inputs() end

--- One of its buttons, as a handle. A key the dialog has no button for is an error.
---@param key ButtonKey The button's `key` in `dialog.json`.
---@return Button
function Dialog:button(key) end

--- Puts it on someone's screen. It's built fresh each time, so what they see is the dialog as it is now, with whatever `options` change for this opening. A project dialog already on their screen is closed (its `close` handlers hear it).
---@param player Player
---@param options? DialogOpenOptions What to open it with, for this opening only. A key it doesn't take, an input or body element the dialog doesn't have, or a value of the wrong kind for its input is an error.
---@return boolean # `false` when they're offline or the dialog is gone.
function Dialog:open_for(player, options) end

