---@field tick integer The server tick it is, the same as `nf.server.tick()`.

--- A player clicked a centity's hitbox. Heard by the clicked node, then each node above it, then the centity, then `nf.on("centity_click")`.
---@class ClickEvent: Event
---@field player Player Who it was.
---@field target Node The node whose hitbox was clicked. `event.current` is the node or centity whose handler is running.
---@field click "left"|"right" Which kind of click: the left (attack) or right (use) button.
---@field hit_position Vec3? Where the player's line of sight met the hitbox, as a world position: what `node:apply_impulse_at` takes. `nil` when the server couldn't tell.
---@field hit_normal Vec3? The unit direction straight out of the face that was hit, in world space. `nil` when the server couldn't tell.

--- Something happened to a centity instance as a whole.
---@class CentityEvent: Event
---@field centity Centity The instance.

--- A centity is about to appear by itself, from its `spawning` rules, near a player.
---@class CentityNaturalSpawnEvent: Event
---@field centity string Which centity, as the project names it: its folder name under `centities/` (`wisp`), or `acme:wisp` for one a package brings.
---@field location Location Where it will stand, facing included (its yaw is the way the centity turns). Writable: assign another `Location` to put it somewhere else, in any world the server has; the spawner does not check its rules again there.
---@field player Player The player it is appearing near.

--- One of a centity's animations started or ended.
---@class AnimationEvent: Event
---@field centity Centity The instance.
---@field animation string The animation's name.

--- Something happened to a node's physics body.
---@class NodeEvent: Event
---@field node Node The node.

--- A physics body started touching something it wasn't touching the tick before: the moment of impact, not every tick while it rests there.
---@class CollideEvent: Event
---@field node Node The body that hit something.
---@field other Node? The other body when it was one of the same centity's, or `nil` for a block or another centity's hitbox.
---@field hit_position Vec3 Where they touched, as a world position.
---@field hit_normal Vec3 The unit direction from what it hit towards the body, in world space.
---@field speed number How fast they were closing, in blocks per second: small for a touch, large for a crash. Handy for how loud a sound should be.

--- A player joined the server.
---@class PlayerJoinEvent: Event
---@field player Player Who it was.
---@field first_join boolean Whether they have never played here before.
---@field message Text? The join message everyone sees, as MiniMessage. Writable: assign another, or `nil` for none.

--- A player left the server.
---@class PlayerQuitEvent: Event
---@field player Player Who it was.
---@field message Text? The leave message everyone sees, as MiniMessage. Writable: assign another, or `nil` for none.

--- A player is saying something in chat.
---@class PlayerChatEvent: Event
---@field player Player Who it was.
---@field message string What they said, as plain text: never MiniMessage, so it can be shown as it is. Writable: assign other text to change what everyone sees.
---@field format Text How the line is shown, as MiniMessage, where `<player>` is the player's display name and `<message>` what they said (both shown as they are, never read as tags). Starts as Minecraft's own look, `"\\<<player>> <message>"`. Writable: assign another, like `"<gray><player>: <white><message>"`.

--- A player clicked a block or the air with one hand. The game asks for each hand in turn, so one right click can arrive twice: once for the main hand and, unless that did something, once for the off hand.
---@class PlayerInteractEvent: Event
---@field player Player Who it was.
---@field click "left"|"right" Which kind of click: the left (attack) or right (use) button.
---@field block Block? The block, or `nil` for a click on the air.
---@field face "up"|"down"|"north"|"south"|"east"|"west"? Which face of the block was clicked, or `nil` for a click on the air.
---@field item Item? What's in the hand the click was with, or `nil` for an empty hand.
---@field hand "main_hand"|"off_hand" Which hand: the game asks for each hand in turn.

--- A player is breaking a block: heard by its world, then `nf.on("block_break")`.
---@class BlockBreakEvent: Event
---@field player Player Who it was.
---@field block Block The block. A handle reads the world live, so what it says depends on when you ask: `state` is what it was when this happened.
---@field state string Its whole block state as it was being broken, like `"minecraft:oak_stairs[facing=east,half=bottom,shape=straight,waterlogged=false]"`.
---@field drops Item[] What it will drop, for the tool they're breaking it with: an empty list for nothing. Writable: assign another list of items (or change this one) to drop those instead.
---@field experience integer The experience it will drop (an ore's, say). Writable: assign another to change it.

--- A player is placing a block: heard by its world, then `nf.on("block_place")`.
---@class BlockPlaceEvent: Event
---@field player Player Who it was.
---@field block Block The block. A handle reads the world live, so what it says depends on when you ask: `state` is what it was when this happened.
---@field state string The whole block state being placed, like `"minecraft:oak_stairs[facing=east,half=bottom,shape=straight,waterlogged=false]"`.
---@field against Block The block it was placed against: the one they clicked.

--- Something a player did that needs no more said.
---@class PlayerEvent: Event
---@field player Player Who it was.

--- A player walked, swam, flew or fell into another block.
---@class PlayerMoveEvent: Event
---@field player Player Who it was.
---@field from Location Where they were, facing included: in the block they are leaving.
---@field to Location Where they are going, facing included: in another block from `from`.

--- A player is being teleported.
---@class PlayerTeleportEvent: Event
---@field player Player Who it was.
---@field from Location Where they are, facing included.
---@field to Location Where they are going, facing included. Writable: assign another `Location` to send them there instead (a world the server doesn't have leaves it as it was).
---@field cause string Why, as Minecraft names it: `"command"`, `"plugin"` (a plugin or a script), `"ender_pearl"`, `"chorus_fruit"`, `"nether_portal"`, `"end_portal"`, `"end_gateway"`, `"spectate"`, `"dismount"`, `"exit_bed"`, `"unknown"`, …

--- A player went from one world to another.
---@class PlayerChangeWorldEvent: Event
---@field player Player Who it was.
---@field from World The world they left.
---@field to World The world they are in now.

--- A player died.
---@class PlayerDeathEvent: Event
---@field player Player Who it was.
---@field killer Entity? Who killed them, when someone did.
---@field cause string What killed them, as Minecraft names it, the same as `EntityDamageEvent`'s `cause`: `"entity_attack"`, `"fall"`, `"lava"`, `"void"`, …
---@field message Text? The death message everyone sees, as MiniMessage. Writable: assign another, or `nil` for none.
---@field keep_inventory boolean Whether they keep what they carry. Writable: `true` keeps their inventory, and then nothing from it drops (unless `drops` is assigned too).
---@field drops Item[] What they drop where they died. Writable: assign another list of items (or change this one) to drop those instead.

--- A player is coming back after dying (or leaving the end).
---@class PlayerRespawnEvent: Event
---@field player Player Who it was.
---@field location Location Where they will appear: their bed, respawn anchor or the world's spawn. Writable: assign another `Location` to bring them back there (a world the server doesn't have leaves it as it was).

--- A player did something with an item stack.
---@class PlayerItemEvent: Event
---@field player Player Who it was.
---@field item Item The item stack.

--- A player is picking up a dropped item.
---@class PlayerPickupItemEvent: Event
---@field player Player Who it was.
---@field item Item The stack they are picking up.
---@field entity DroppedItem The dropped item it lies in.

--- A player is using the item in one hand.
---@class PlayerUseItemEvent: Event
---@field player Player Who it was.
---@field item Item What's in that hand.
---@field hand "main_hand"|"off_hand" Which hand: the game asks for each hand in turn.

--- A player started or stopped sneaking.
---@class PlayerSneakEvent: Event
---@field player Player Who it was.
---@field sneaking boolean Whether they are sneaking now.

--- A player typed a command.
---@class PlayerCommandEvent: Event
---@field player Player Who it was.
---@field input string What they typed, without the leading `/`: `"give @s minecraft:diamond 2"`.

--- Which centities `nf.centities.all` lists. Every key is optional; an instance is listed when it matches all of those given. A key that isn't one of these is an error.
---@class CentityFilter
---@field kind string? Only instances of this centity: its folder name.
---@field world World? Only instances in this world.
---@field near Vec3? Only instances whose position is within `radius` of this point (in any world, unless `world` says which). Needs `radius`.
---@field radius number? How far from `near`, in blocks. Needs `near`.

--- A projectile was launched.
---@class ProjectileLaunchEvent: Event
---@field projectile Entity The projectile. It isn't in the world yet, but its methods already work: a handler can tag it, give it data, or change its velocity.
---@field shooter Entity? Who launched it, or `nil` for a dispenser.

--- A projectile hit something.
---@class ProjectileHitEvent: Event
---@field projectile Entity The projectile.
---@field shooter Entity? Who launched it, when someone did.
---@field entity Entity? The entity it hit, or `nil` for a block.
---@field block Block? The block it hit, or `nil` for an entity.
---@field face "up"|"down"|"north"|"south"|"east"|"west"|nil Which face of the block it hit, or `nil` for an entity.

--- A bow or crossbow was shot.
---@class EntityShootBowEvent: Event
---@field entity Living Who shot it.
---@field bow Item? The bow or crossbow, or `nil` for a mob without one.
---@field projectile Entity What it shot: an arrow, or a firework from a crossbow. Its methods already work.
---@field force number How far the bow was drawn, 0 to 1 (always 1 for a crossbow).
---@field hand "main_hand"|"off_hand" Which hand.

--- A mob is picking a target.
---@class EntityTargetEvent: Event
---@field entity Mob The mob.
---@field target Entity? What it's going after, or `nil` when it's forgetting its target.
---@field cause string Why, as Minecraft names it: `"closest_player"`, `"target_attacked_entity"`, `"target_attacked_nearby_entity"`, `"forgot_target"`, `"target_died"`, `"temptation"`, `"custom"` (a plugin or a script), …

--- Something exploded: an entity (`entity_explode`) or a block (`block_explode`).
---@class ExplodeEvent: Event
---@field entity Entity? What exploded, for `entity_explode`; `nil` for a block.
---@field block Block? Where the block that exploded was, for `block_explode` (it has gone already); `nil` for an entity.
---@field state string? The whole block state of the block that exploded, for `block_explode`; `nil` for an entity.
---@field location Location Where it exploded.
---@field blocks Block[] The blocks it will break, as their handles: read them to see what would go.
---@field breaks_blocks boolean Whether `blocks` will break. Writable: `false` keeps every block standing, but the explosion still hurts and pushes what is near.
---@field yield number The chance each broken block drops as an item, 0 to 1. Writable: assign another (1 drops everything).

--- An entity is catching fire.
---@class EntityCombustEvent: Event
---@field entity Entity What is catching fire.
---@field ticks integer How long it will burn, in ticks. Writable: assign another.
---@field source Entity? The entity that set it alight (a flaming arrow, whoever swung a fire aspect sword), when one did.
---@field block Block? The block that set it alight (fire, lava), when one did.

--- One of an entity's status effects is changing.
---@class EntityChangeEffectEvent: Event
---@field entity Living Whose effect it is.
---@field effect string Which effect: `"minecraft:speed"`.
---@field action "added"|"changed"|"cleared"|"removed" `"added"`: it starts; `"changed"`: it gets stronger or longer; `"cleared"`: everything was taken away at once (milk, `/effect clear`); `"removed"`: this one was taken away or ran out.
---@field cause string Why, as Minecraft names it: `"potion_drink"`, `"potion_splash"`, `"beacon"`, `"milk"`, `"expiration"`, `"command"`, `"plugin"` (a plugin or a script), `"food"`, `"attack"`, …
---@field from StatusEffect? The effect as it was, or `nil` when it is new.
---@field to StatusEffect? The effect as it will be, or `nil` when it goes.

--- Something happened to a block.
---@class BlockEvent: Event
---@field block Block The block.

--- A block is catching fire.
---@class BlockIgniteEvent: Event
---@field block Block The block.
---@field cause string Why, as Minecraft names it: `"flint_and_steel"`, `"fireball"`, `"lava"`, `"lightning"`, `"spread"`, `"explosion"`, `"arrow"`, `"ender_crystal"`, …
---@field player Player? The player who set it alight, when one did.
---@field entity Entity? The entity that set it alight, when one did (a player too).
---@field source Block? The block it caught from (fire, lava), when one did.

--- A block is burning away.
---@class BlockBurnEvent: Event
---@field block Block The block.
---@field source Block? The fire it's burning in, when the server knows.

--- A block is changing by itself.
---@class BlockChangeEvent: Event
---@field block Block The block that is changing.
---@field state string The whole block state it is changing to: `"minecraft:wheat[age=7]"`.
---@field source Block? For `block_spread`: the block it spreads from.
---@field entity Entity? For `block_form`: the entity that formed it (a player's frost walker boots, a snow golem), when one did.

--- A liquid is flowing into another block.
---@class BlockFlowEvent: Event
---@field block Block Where it flows from.
---@field to Block Where it flows to.
---@field face "up"|"down"|"north"|"south"|"east"|"west" Which way, from `block` to `to`.

--- A piston is moving.
---@class PistonEvent: Event
---@field block Block The piston.
---@field direction "up"|"down"|"north"|"south"|"east"|"west" Which way the piston faces: blocks move this way as it extends, and back towards it as it retracts.
---@field sticky boolean Whether it is a sticky piston.
---@field blocks Block[] The blocks it will move, where they are now.

--- A block's redstone power is changing.
---@class BlockRedstoneEvent: Event
---@field block Block The block.
---@field from integer The power it had, 0 to 15.
---@field to integer The power it is changing to, 0 to 15. Writable: assign another.

--- A player finished writing on a sign.
---@class SignChangeEvent: Event
---@field player Player Who it was.
---@field block Block The block.
---@field side "front"|"back" Which side they wrote on.
---@field lines string[] What they wrote, line by line, as plain text (four lines, empty ones included).

--- A player started hitting a block.
---@class BlockStartBreakEvent: Event
---@field player Player Who it was.
---@field block Block The block.
---@field item Item? What they're hitting it with, or `nil` for a bare hand.
---@field instant boolean Whether it will break at once (in creative, or a block that breaks in one hit). Writable: `true` breaks it now.

--- A broken block is dropping its items.
---@class BlockDropItemEvent: Event
---@field player Player Who it was.
---@field block Block Where it was: it has gone already.
---@field state string Its whole block state, as it was.
---@field items Item[] What it drops, its contents included.

--- It is starting or stopping raining in a world.
---@class WeatherChangeEvent: Event
---@field world World The world.
---@field raining boolean Whether it will be raining (or snowing).
---@field cause string Why, as Minecraft names it: `"natural"`, `"command"`, `"sleep"`, `"plugin"` (a plugin or a script), `"unknown"`.

--- A thunderstorm is starting or stopping in a world.
---@class ThunderChangeEvent: Event
---@field world World The world.
---@field thundering boolean Whether there will be a thunderstorm.
---@field cause string Why, as Minecraft names it: `"natural"`, `"command"`, `"sleep"`, `"plugin"` (a plugin or a script), `"unknown"`.

--- Lightning is striking.
---@class LightningStrikeEvent: Event
---@field world World The world.
---@field location Location Where it strikes.
---@field entity Entity The lightning bolt.
---@field cause string Why, as Minecraft names it: `"weather"`, `"trident"`, `"command"`, `"spawner"` (a skeleton horse trap), `"custom"` (a plugin or a script), `"unknown"`.

--- A chunk: one just generated, or one leaving memory.
---@class ChunkEvent: Event
---@field world World The world.
---@field x integer Its x, in chunks: a block x divided by 16, rounded down.
---@field z integer Its z, in chunks.

--- A chunk came into memory.
---@class ChunkLoadEvent: Event
---@field world World The world.
---@field x integer Its x, in chunks: a block x divided by 16, rounded down.
---@field z integer Its z, in chunks.
---@field generated boolean Whether it was just generated, for the first time.

--- A world was loaded or is being unloaded.
---@class WorldEvent: Event
---@field world World The world.

--- An entity is getting on or off something it rides.
---@class MountEvent: Event
---@field entity Entity The rider.
---@field vehicle Entity What it rides.

--- A vehicle moved into another block.
---@class VehicleMoveEvent: Event
---@field vehicle Entity The boat or minecart.
---@field from Location Where it was: in the block it is leaving.
---@field to Location Where it is now: in another block from `from`.

--- Something is hitting a vehicle.
---@class VehicleDamageEvent: Event
---@field vehicle Entity The boat or minecart.
---@field attacker Entity? Who hit it, when someone did.
---@field amount number How much damage it takes. Writable: assign another to change it.

--- A vehicle is being broken.
---@class VehicleDestroyEvent: Event
---@field vehicle Entity The boat or minecart.
---@field attacker Entity? Who broke it, when someone did.

--- A player opened or closed a vanilla inventory.
---@class PlayerInventoryEvent: Event
---@field player Player Who it was.
---@field kind string What kind of inventory it is: `"chest"`, `"barrel"`, `"shulker_box"`, `"ender_chest"`, `"furnace"`, `"workbench"` (a crafting table), `"anvil"`, `"enchanting"`, `"merchant"` (a villager), …
---@field inventory Inventory? The inventory, when it's a real one (a container block's, an entity's, their ender chest); `nil` for one with nothing kept in it (a crafting table, an anvil).
---@field block Block? The block it belongs to (a chest, a crafting table), or `nil` for an entity's.

--- A player is taking what they crafted.
---@class PlayerCraftEvent: Event
---@field player Player Who it was.
---@field recipe string? Its recipe's id, like `"minecraft:oak_planks"`, or `nil` when no recipe matches.
---@field item Item What one craft makes.
---@field shift boolean Whether they shift-clicked, to make as many as they can.

--- What's in a crafting grid changed.
---@class PlayerPrepareCraftEvent: Event
---@field player Player Who it was.
---@field recipe string? Its recipe's id, like `"minecraft:oak_planks"`, or `nil` when no recipe matches.
---@field ingredients table<integer, Item> What's in the grid, by slot: 0 to 8 for a crafting table, row by row from the top left, or 0 to 3 for the 2×2. Empty slots are left out.
---@field result Item? What the result slot shows, or `nil` for nothing. Writable: assign another item to make that the result, or `nil` for none.
---@field repair boolean Whether it is two damaged tools of a kind being combined into one.

--- What is in an anvil changed.
---@class PlayerPrepareAnvilEvent: Event
---@field player Player Who it was.
---@field left Item? What is in the first slot.
---@field right Item? What is in the second slot.
---@field rename string? The name typed in, or `nil` for none.
---@field result Item? What the result slot shows, or `nil` for nothing. Writable: assign another item to make that the result, or `nil` for none.
---@field cost integer How many experience levels it takes. Writable: assign another.

--- What is in a smithing table changed.
---@class PlayerPrepareSmithingEvent: Event
---@field player Player Who it was.
---@field template Item? The smithing template.
---@field base Item? What is being upgraded or trimmed.
---@field addition Item? The ingot or material.
---@field result Item? What the result slot shows, or `nil` for nothing. Writable: assign another item to make that the result, or `nil` for none.

--- What is in a grindstone changed.
---@class PlayerPrepareGrindstoneEvent: Event
---@field player Player Who it was.
---@field top Item? What is in the top slot.
---@field bottom Item? What is in the bottom slot.
---@field result Item? What the result slot shows, or `nil` for nothing. Writable: assign another item to make that the result, or `nil` for none.

--- A player is enchanting an item.
---@class PlayerEnchantItemEvent: Event
---@field player Player Who it was.
---@field block Block The enchanting table.
---@field item Item What is being enchanted.
---@field enchantments table<string, integer> What it gets, by id with its level: `{ ["minecraft:sharpness"] = 3 }`.
---@field cost integer How many experience levels it takes. Writable: assign another.

--- A furnace finished smelting an item.
---@class FurnaceSmeltEvent: Event
---@field block Block The furnace, smoker or blast furnace.
---@field source Item What was smelted (one of it).
---@field result Item What it makes. Writable: assign another item to make that instead.

--- A server list is asking about the server.
---@class ServerListPingEvent: Event
---@field address string The address it asks from, like `"203.0.113.7"`.
---@field description Text The server's description under its name (its MOTD), as MiniMessage: two lines at most. Writable: assign another.
---@field online integer How many players it says are on. Writable: assign another number.
---@field max_players integer How many it says fit. Writable: assign another number.
---@field hide_players boolean Whether it hides the player counts (showing `???`) and who is on. Writable.

--- What movement keys a player holds down now.
---@class PlayerInputEvent: Event
---@field player Player Who it was.
---@field forward boolean Forward (W).
---@field backward boolean Back (S).
---@field left boolean Left (A).
---@field right boolean Right (D).
---@field jump boolean Jump (space).
---@field sneak boolean Sneak (shift).
---@field sprint boolean Sprint (control).

--- A player picked another hotbar slot.
---@class PlayerChangeSlotEvent: Event
---@field player Player Who it was.
---@field from integer The slot they had, 0 to 8.
---@field to integer The slot they picked, 0 to 8.

--- A player swung an arm.
---@class PlayerSwingEvent: Event
---@field player Player Who it was.
---@field hand "main_hand"|"off_hand" Which hand.

--- A player started or stopped sprinting.
---@class PlayerSprintEvent: Event
---@field player Player Who it was.
---@field sprinting boolean Whether they are sprinting now.

--- A player started or stopped flying.
---@class PlayerFlyEvent: Event
---@field player Player Who it was.
---@field flying boolean Whether they are flying now.

--- A player jumped.
---@class PlayerJumpEvent: Event
---@field player Player Who it was.
---@field from Location Where they jumped from, facing included.
---@field to Location Where the jump has taken them so far.

--- A player's game mode is changing.
---@class PlayerChangeGameModeEvent: Event
---@field player Player Who it was.
---@field game_mode "survival"|"creative"|"adventure"|"spectator" The game mode they are changing to.
---@field cause string Why, as Minecraft names it: `"command"`, `"plugin"` (a plugin or a script), `"default_game_mode"`, `"hardcore_death"`, `"unknown"`, …

--- A player's food level is changing.
---@class PlayerChangeFoodEvent: Event
---@field player Player Who it was.
---@field food integer The level it's changing to, 0 to 20. Writable: assign another to set that instead.
---@field item Item? What they ate, or `nil` when they are getting hungry.

--- An entity is getting health back: heard by the entity, then `nf.on("entity_heal")`.
---@class EntityHealEvent: Event
---@field entity Living Who is healing.
---@field amount number How much: 2 is one heart. Writable: assign another to change it.
---@field cause string Why, as Minecraft names it: `"satiated"` (a full food bar), `"regen"`, `"eating"`, `"magic"`, `"magic_regen"`, `"ender_crystal"`, `"custom"` (a plugin or a script), …

--- A player is getting experience points.
---@class PlayerGainExperienceEvent: Event
---@field player Player Who it was.
---@field amount integer How many points. Writable: assign another to give that many instead.

--- A player's experience level changed.
---@class PlayerChangeLevelEvent: Event
---@field player Player Who it was.
---@field from integer The level they had.
---@field to integer The level they have now.

--- A player is being kicked.
---@class PlayerKickEvent: Event
---@field player Player Who it was.
---@field text Text What their screen will say, as MiniMessage. Writable: assign other text.
---@field message Text? The leave message everyone sees, as MiniMessage. Writable: assign another, or `nil` for none.
---@field cause string Why, as Minecraft names it: `"kick_command"`, `"plugin"` (a plugin or a script), `"flying_player"`, `"timeout"`, `"whitelist"`, `"ban"`, `"idling"`, `"unknown"`, …

--- A player completed an advancement.
---@class PlayerAdvancementEvent: Event
---@field player Player Who it was.
---@field advancement string Its key, always with its namespace: the game's `"minecraft:story/mine_diamond"`, or one of the project's own as `"<namespace>:<id>"` (`"basic:treasure_hunter"`).
---@field message Text? The message everyone sees, as MiniMessage, or `nil` when there's none (a recipe, or an advancement that doesn't announce itself). Writable: assign another, or `nil` for none.

--- A player let go of an item before it finished.
---@class PlayerStopUsingItemEvent: Event
---@field player Player Who it was.
---@field item Item What they were using.
---@field ticks integer How long they held it, in ticks.

--- How a centity walks where `centity:move_to` sends it. A key that isn't one of these is an error.
---@class CentityPathOptions
---@field speed number? How fast, in blocks a second: more than 0, at most 20. Default 4.3, a player's walk.
---@field fly boolean? Flies there, through the air in any direction, rather than walking on the ground: no steps, drops or falling. Finding a way costs the server more. Default false.
---@field face boolean? Turns it to face the way it goes, about its feet, a little each tick. Default true.
---@field width number? How wide a gap it needs, in blocks: more than 0, at most 8. Default: its hitboxes' wider side, as they're placed when it sets off (1 without hitboxes). It's taken as square, whichever way it faces.
---@field height number? How tall a gap it needs, in blocks: more than 0, at most 16. Default: its hitboxes' height (1 without hitboxes).
---@field step_height number? Walking: how high a step it goes straight up, in blocks: at least 0. Default 1, a block, as a mob jumps one.
---@field max_drop number? Walking: how far down it will drop off an edge, in blocks: at least 0. Default 3.
---@field range number? How far from where it sets off it looks for a way, in blocks: more than 0, at most 128. A target further away than this is `false`. Default 48. One search looks at no more than 4096 places, so a long way round may only take it part of the way.

--- A centity stopped walking where `move_to` sent it.
---@class CentityPathEndEvent: Event
---@field centity Centity The instance.
---@field reached boolean Whether it got there: to the end of its path, within 2 blocks of where it was sent (or of what it went after, which it stops short of); `false` when it gave up.

--- How `centity:play_animation` plays an animation this time. The file says how it plays by default.
---@class AnimationOptions
---@field speed number? 1 is as authored (the default), 2 twice as fast, 0.5 half. Not negative.
---@field from_tick number? Where in the clip to start, in ticks from its beginning. Default 0.
---@field loop boolean? `true` loops it whatever its file says; `false` plays it through once (a `hold` clip still holds its last pose). Default: the file's `loop`.
---@field blend_ticks integer? Eases from the pose the nodes have now into the animation over this many ticks, instead of jumping to its first frame. Default 0.

--- How a text display draws its text: what `node:display_text_style()` gives and `node:set_display_text_style` takes. Every field is optional; one left out is its default.
---@class TextStyle
---@field background string? `#AARRGGBB`, alpha first: `"#00000000"` is none at all. Default: Minecraft's translucent grey.
---@field opacity integer? The text's own opacity, 0 to 255. Default 255.
---@field shadow boolean? A drop shadow under the text. Default false.
---@field alignment "center"|"left"|"right"? How lines of different lengths line up. Default `"center"`.
---@field line_width integer? How wide a line gets, in pixels, before it wraps. Default 200.
---@field see_through boolean? Drawn through blocks in front of it. Default false.

--- Fixed light levels a display is drawn with: `node:set_brightness`.
---@class Brightness
---@field block_light integer? Light from blocks (torches, lamps), 0 to 15. Required.
---@field sky_light integer? Light from the sky, 0 to 15. Required.

--- Which entities `world:entities` lists. Every key is optional; an entity is listed when it matches all of those given. A key that isn't one of these is an error.
---@class EntityFilter
---@field kind string? Only this kind: `"minecraft:zombie"`.
---@field tag string? Only entities with this scoreboard tag.
---@field near Vec3? Only entities within `radius` of this point. Needs `radius`.
---@field radius number? How far from `near`, in blocks. Needs `near`.
---@field living boolean? `true`: only living entities (mobs, animals, players); `false`: only the rest.

--- How `world:spawn_entity` sets up what it spawns, before anything (an `entity_spawn` handler included) sees it. A key that isn't one of these is an error.
---@class EntitySpawnOptions
---@field custom_name Text? Its custom name, MiniMessage.
---@field tags string[]? Scoreboard tags to give it.
---@field data any A table its `data()` table starts with: the same values a `data()` table may hold. Something that can't be saved is an error.
---@field velocity Vec3? How fast it starts moving, in blocks per tick.

--- How a status effect is given. A key that isn't one of these is an error.
---@class EffectOptions
---@field amplifier integer? Its strength above the first: 0 is speed I, 1 speed II. Default 0.
---@field ambient boolean? Fainter particles, as from a beacon. Default `false`.
---@field particles boolean? Whether it shows particles. Default `true`.
---@field icon boolean? Whether it shows an icon on their screen. Default `true`.

--- One status effect on a living entity: what `living:effects()` lists.
---@class StatusEffect
---@field effect string Its id: `"minecraft:speed"`.
---@field ticks integer How long it has left; -1 for ever.
---@field amplifier integer Its strength above the first: 0 is level I.
---@field ambient boolean Whether its particles are faint, as from a beacon.
---@field particles boolean Whether it shows particles.
---@field icon boolean Whether it shows an icon.

--- How a mob walks where `move_to` sends it. A key that isn't one of these is an error.
---@class PathOptions
---@field speed number? How fast, times its own walking speed (its `movement_speed` attribute): 1 walks, 1.5 hurries. More than 0. Default 1.

--- One of a mob's goals: what `mob:goals` lists.
---@class MobGoal
---@field key string Its key: `"minecraft:random_stroll"` for one of the game's, `"<namespace>:guard_post"` for one the project added, or another plugin's.
---@field priority integer Its priority, the game's goals' too: smaller is more important. A goal takes its controls from running goals with a bigger number.
---@field controls ("move"|"look"|"jump"|"target")[] What it claims while it runs, so no less important goal claiming the same runs with it: `"move"`, `"look"`, `"jump"`, `"target"` (a targeting goal). Empty for a goal that claims none of these.
---@field running boolean Whether it runs now.

--- A goal written in Lua, for `mob:add_goal`. Each callback gets the mob. A key that isn't one of these is an error.
---@class GoalDefinition
---@field priority integer How important it is: smaller goes first. A goal takes its controls from running goals with a bigger number, and waits for those with a smaller one or the same. The game's goals use about 0 (swimming up in water) to 8 (looking about).
---@field controls ("move"|"look"|"jump"|"target")[]? What it claims while it runs, so no less important goal claiming the same runs with it: `"move"` (it walks the mob), `"look"`, `"jump"`, or `"target"` alone (it picks what the mob fights). Default none: it runs alongside the goals that claim something, and one at a time with the others that claim nothing.
---@field should_start (fun(mob: Mob): boolean)? Whether to start now, asked every tick or two while it isn't running. Default: always.
---@field should_continue (fun(mob: Mob): boolean)? Whether to keep running, asked every tick or two while it runs. Default: what `should_start` says.
---@field start (fun(mob: Mob))? It starts running.
---@field tick (fun(mob: Mob))? While it runs: every tick or two, as the game updates its goals.
---@field stop (fun(mob: Mob))? It stopped: `should_continue` said no, a more important goal took one of its controls, or it was removed while running.

--- Which goals `mob:clear_goals` leaves. A key that isn't one of these is an error.
---@class GoalClearOptions
---@field keep string[]? The keys of goals to keep, as `remove_goal` takes them: `{ "float" }` keeps `"minecraft:float"`, so a mob still swims.

--- One modifier on an entity's attribute: what `living:attribute_modifiers` lists.
---@class AttributeModifier
---@field id string Its full id: `"<namespace>:slow_zone"` for one a script added, or the game's own (`"minecraft:sprinting"`), an item's, another plugin's.
---@field amount number How much.
---@field operation "add_value"|"add_multiplied_base"|"add_multiplied_total" How `amount` applies: added; added times the base value; or the total multiplied by `1 + amount`.

--- How long a title takes. A key that isn't one of these is an error.
---@class TitleOptions
---@field fade_in integer? Ticks it takes to appear. Default 10.
---@field stay integer? Ticks it stays. Default 70.
---@field fade_out integer? Ticks it takes to go. Default 20.

--- Something is hurting an entity: heard by the entity, then `nf.on("entity_damage")`.
---@class EntityDamageEvent: Event
---@field entity Entity Who is hurt.
---@field amount number How much, before armour: 2 is one heart. Writable: assign another to change it.
---@field cause string What hurt it, as Minecraft names it: `"entity_attack"`, `"projectile"`, `"fall"`, `"fire"`, `"lava"`, `"drowning"`, `"void"`, …
---@field attacker Entity? Who did it, when someone did: for an arrow, whoever shot it.

--- A living entity died: heard by it, then `nf.on("entity_death")`.
---@class EntityDeathEvent: Event
---@field entity Living Who died. Its handle answers `nil` from now on.
---@field killer Entity? Who killed it, when someone did.
---@field drops Item[] What it drops. Writable: assign another list of items (or change this one) to drop those instead.
---@field experience integer The experience it drops. Writable: assign another to change it.

--- A mob stopped walking where `move_to` sent it.
---@class EntityPathEndEvent: Event
---@field entity Mob The mob.
---@field reached boolean Whether it got there, within 2 blocks; `false` when it gave up.

--- A player right-clicked an entity: heard by the entity, then the player, then `nf.on("player_interact_entity")`.
---@class PlayerInteractEntityEvent: Event
---@field player Player Who clicked.
---@field entity Entity What they clicked.
---@field hand "main_hand"|"off_hand" Which hand the click was with: the game asks for each hand in turn.

--- An entity is coming into a world: heard by the world, then `nf.on("entity_spawn")`.
---@class EntitySpawnEvent: Event
---@field entity Entity What's coming. Its methods already work, so a handler can name it or tag it.
---@field world World The world it's coming into.
---@field cause string What caused it, as Minecraft names it: `"natural"`, `"spawner"`, `"breeding"`, `"command"`, `"custom"` (a plugin or a script), …

--- How `player:ban` bans. Every key is optional; a key that isn't one of these is an error.
---@class BanOptions
---@field reason string? What they're told when they're kicked and when they try to join, as plain text. Default the server's own message.
---@field expires integer? When it runs out, as Unix time in milliseconds (like `nf.server.unix_time()`): a time already past is an error. Default never.
---@field source string? Who banned them, as the ban list shows it. Default `"NetherForge"`.

--- How far a player is with an advancement, by its criteria's names as the advancement's own file calls them.
---@class AdvancementProgress
---@field done string[] The criteria they've met.
---@field remaining string[] The criteria left.

--- An item stack as a plain table: what `menu:item` returns and what `menu:set_item` takes. Only `kind` is required, or `item` instead. Read one, change a field and write it back, and nothing else about it changes: whatever the stack carries that these fields can't say (a book's pages, another plugin's data) rides along in `raw`, which you never write yourself. A field that isn't one of these is an error.
---@class Item
---@field kind string? The item type: `"minecraft:diamond_sword"`, or `"diamond_sword"`. Required unless `item` names a project item, whose kind it then is; an item read from the server always has it.
---@field item string? The project item (`items/<id>/`) this stack is: `{ item = "ruby", count = 3 }`. Its file fills in every field the table leaves out (`nf.items.create` does the same), and the stack carries the item's namespaced id wherever it goes, so it's still a ruby in a chest, on the ground, or after a restart. A project item the project doesn't have is an error, and so is a `kind` that isn't the item's.
---@field count integer? 1 to 99. Defaults to 1.
---@field name Text? Display name, MiniMessage. Not italic unless you say so.
---@field lore Text[]? Lines under the name, each MiniMessage.
---@field enchantments table<string, integer>? Enchantment id → level: `{ ["minecraft:sharpness"] = 5 }`.
---@field glint boolean? The enchanted shimmer without an enchantment, as menus use for "selected".
---@field hide_tooltip boolean? No tooltip at all, for filler panes.
---@field unbreakable boolean?
---@field damage integer? Durability used: 0 is pristine.
---@field item_model string? `<pack>/<key>`: a look from one of the project's resource packs.
---@field tooltip_style string? `<pack>/<key>`: a tooltip frame from one of the project's resource packs.
---@field equipment ItemEquipment? Worn as armour or equipment, drawn as one of the project's resource packs' equipment looks: `{ asset = "gear/ruby", slot = "head" }`.
---@field color string? `#RRGGBB`, for dyed leather, potions and maps.
---@field profile string? Whose head, for a player head: a name or a UUID.
---@field max_stack_size integer? How many fit in one stack, 1 to 99, instead of the kind's own. More than 1 on an item that takes damage (a sword, armour) is an error.
---@field rarity "common"|"uncommon"|"rare"|"epic"? The colour of its name when it has no colour of its own: white, yellow, aqua or light purple.
---@field attribute_modifiers ItemAttributeModifier[]? What it changes about whoever wears or holds it: `{ { attribute = "minecraft:attack_damage", amount = 6, operation = "add_value", slot = "main_hand" } }`. These replace the kind's own (a sword's damage) rather than adding to them.
---@field can_break string[]? Block kinds a player in adventure mode can break with it: `{ "minecraft:stone" }`.
---@field can_place_on string[]? Block kinds a player in adventure mode can place it on.
---@field food ItemFood? What eating it does. Any item with `food` can be eaten, even one that isn't food: `{ nutrition = 4, saturation = 2.4 }`.
---@field cooldown ItemCooldown? A cooldown after using it (an ender pearl's): `{ seconds = 2 }`. Items with the same `group` share one.
---@field data table<string, any>? Your script's own data, saved on the stack itself (its persistent data container), so it goes wherever the item goes: tag a coin with `data = { coin = true }`. A table keyed by strings, holding what a `data()` table may: strings, numbers, booleans, tables of them, `Vec3`s, `Location`s, `Item` tables, and `Player` and `Centity` handles, which come back as the same types. Absent when the stack carries none. Anything else (a function, a `Menu` window) is an error, naming where it was.
---@field raw string? Everything else the stack carries, opaque. Present only when the fields above don't say it all. Leave it alone: it's how a read-change-write keeps what you didn't change.

--- One of an item's `attribute_modifiers`: how much it changes one attribute of whoever wears or holds it.
---@class ItemAttributeModifier
---@field id string? A namespaced id telling it apart from the item's other modifiers. Default `"<namespace>:<attribute>_<index>"` (the project's namespace, and its place in the list from 0), which is what you get back when it has that one.
---@field attribute string The attribute: `"minecraft:attack_damage"`, `"minecraft:movement_speed"`, `"minecraft:max_health"`, …
---@field amount number How much.
---@field operation "add_value"|"add_multiplied_base"|"add_multiplied_total" How `amount` applies: added; added times the base value; or the total multiplied by `1 + amount`.
---@field slot "any"|"hand"|"main_hand"|"off_hand"|"armor"|"head"|"chest"|"legs"|"feet"|"body"|"saddle"? Where the item has to be for it to count. Default `"any"`.

--- What eating an item does: an `Item`'s `food`.
---@class ItemFood
---@field nutrition integer Hunger points it restores (a full bar is 20).
---@field saturation number Saturation it adds.
---@field can_always_eat boolean? Whether it can be eaten when full. Default false.
---@field eat_seconds number? How long eating it takes. Default 1.6.

--- What an item looks like worn, and where: an `Item`'s `equipment` (the `equippable` component).
---@class ItemEquipment
---@field asset string `<pack>/<key>`: an equipment look from one of the project's resource packs.
---@field slot "head"|"chest"|"legs"|"feet"|"body"|"saddle" The slot it is worn in: `"body"` is a horse's or wolf's armour.

--- A cooldown after using an item: an `Item`'s `cooldown`.
---@class ItemCooldown
---@field seconds number How long, more than 0.
---@field group string? A namespaced id: items in one group share a cooldown (`player:set_item_cooldown` takes it too). Default: the item's kind.

--- What `player:open_menu` opens a window with.
---@class MenuOpenOptions
---@field context any Anything at all, handed to the new window: its script reads it with `this:context()`, and every event on the window carries it as `event.context`. It's the very value, kept on the server, so a table can carry handles and functions. A shared menu has one window for everyone, so giving it a context is an error.

--- What `player:open_dialog`, `dialog:open_for` and `dialog:ask` open a dialog with. Everything here is for this one opening: the dialog's file doesn't change, and the next opening starts from it again.
---@class DialogOpenOptions
---@field context any Anything at all, handed back as `event.context` on every press (and on `close`) of this opening. Kept on the server for this player until the dialog leaves their screen, never sent to them, so it can carry handles and functions.
---@field values table<string, string|number|boolean>? What its inputs start at, by input key, instead of the file's `initial`: a string for a text input, the option's `id` for a `single_option`, a number within the range for a `number_range`, `true` or `false` for a `boolean`. An input the dialog doesn't have is an error.
---@field title Text? The title for this opening, MiniMessage, instead of the file's.
---@field body table<string, Text>? Body text for this opening, MiniMessage, by the body element's `key`: a message's text, or an item's description. A key no body element of the dialog has is an error.

--- Someone opened or closed a menu window.
---@class MenuEvent: Event
---@field player Player Who.
---@field menu Menu The window.
---@field context any What the script that opened the window passed along (`player:open_menu(menu, { context = ... })`), the same as `menu:context()`: the very value, not a copy. `nil` when nothing was, and always for a shared window.

--- Someone clicked while a menu window was open. A click on one of the window's slots is heard by the slot, then the menu, then `nf.on("menu_click")`; a click anywhere else starts at the menu.
---@class MenuClickEvent: Event
---@field player Player Who clicked.
---@field menu Menu The window.
---@field target Slot? The window's slot that was clicked, or `nil` for a click in the player's own inventory or outside both.
---@field index integer? The slot clicked, from 0: in the window when `in_menu` is true, and in the player's own inventory below it otherwise. `nil` for a click outside both.
---@field in_menu boolean Whether the click landed in the window rather than in the player's own inventory below it.
---@field click "left"|"right"|"shift_left"|"shift_right"|"middle"|"number_key"|"double"|"drop"|"control_drop"|"other" What kind of click it was.
---@field hotbar_index integer? For `number_key`: the hotbar slot, 0 to 8.
---@field item Item? What was in the slot clicked.
---@field cursor_item Item? What the player was carrying on the cursor.
---@field context any What the script that opened the window passed along (`player:open_menu(menu, { context = ... })`), the same as `menu:context()`: the very value, not a copy. `nil` when nothing was, and always for a shared window.

--- Someone dragged a stack across slots of a menu window.
---@class MenuDragEvent: Event
---@field player Player Who dragged.
---@field menu Menu The window.
---@field indices integer[] The window's slots the stack is spread over, from 0, in order.
---@field cursor_item Item? The stack being dragged.
---@field context any What the script that opened the window passed along (`player:open_menu(menu, { context = ... })`), the same as `menu:context()`: the very value, not a copy. `nil` when nothing was, and always for a shared window.

--- Someone pressed a dialog's button: heard by the button, then the dialog, then `nf.on("dialog_press")`.
---@class DialogPressEvent: Event
---@field player Player Who pressed it.
---@field dialog Dialog The dialog.
---@field target Button The button pressed.
---@field key string The button's `key`.
---@field values table<string, string|number> Every input's answer, by its `key`: the text typed, the option's `id`, the slider's number, or a checkbox's `onTrue`/`onFalse` string (`"true"`/`"false"` unless the file says otherwise).
---@field context any What the script that opened the dialog passed along for this opening (`player:open_dialog(dialog, { context = ... })`): the very value, not a copy. It's kept on the server, never sent to the player, so it can be anything at all. `nil` when nothing was.

--- Someone left a dialog without pressing a button, or a script closed it.
---@class DialogEvent: Event
---@field player Player Who.
---@field dialog Dialog The dialog.
---@field context any What the script that opened the dialog passed along for this opening (`player:open_dialog(dialog, { context = ... })`): the very value, not a copy. It's kept on the server, never sent to the player, so it can be anything at all. `nil` when nothing was.

--- A partial `Item` that picks stacks: a stack matches when every field given matches, so `{ kind = "minecraft:paper", name = "<gold>Ticket" }` finds gold tickets and nothing else. `kind` may be left out (any kind), and `data` matches as a subset: `{ data = { coin = true } }` finds coins whatever else they carry. `count` and `raw` have no place in a match; a field `Item` doesn't have is an error.
---@class ItemMatch
---@field kind string? The item type: `"minecraft:diamond_sword"`, or `"diamond_sword"`. Required unless `item` names a project item, whose kind it then is; an item read from the server always has it.
---@field item string? The project item (`items/<id>/`) this stack is: `{ item = "ruby", count = 3 }`. Its file fills in every field the table leaves out (`nf.items.create` does the same), and the stack carries the item's namespaced id wherever it goes, so it's still a ruby in a chest, on the ground, or after a restart. A project item the project doesn't have is an error, and so is a `kind` that isn't the item's.
---@field name Text? Display name, MiniMessage. Not italic unless you say so.
---@field lore Text[]? Lines under the name, each MiniMessage.
---@field enchantments table<string, integer>? Enchantment id → level: `{ ["minecraft:sharpness"] = 5 }`.
---@field glint boolean? The enchanted shimmer without an enchantment, as menus use for "selected".
---@field hide_tooltip boolean? No tooltip at all, for filler panes.
---@field unbreakable boolean?
---@field damage integer? Durability used: 0 is pristine.
---@field item_model string? `<pack>/<key>`: a look from one of the project's resource packs.
---@field tooltip_style string? `<pack>/<key>`: a tooltip frame from one of the project's resource packs.
---@field equipment ItemEquipment? Worn as armour or equipment, drawn as one of the project's resource packs' equipment looks: `{ asset = "gear/ruby", slot = "head" }`.
---@field color string? `#RRGGBB`, for dyed leather, potions and maps.
---@field profile string? Whose head, for a player head: a name or a UUID.
---@field max_stack_size integer? How many fit in one stack, 1 to 99, instead of the kind's own. More than 1 on an item that takes damage (a sword, armour) is an error.
---@field rarity "common"|"uncommon"|"rare"|"epic"? The colour of its name when it has no colour of its own: white, yellow, aqua or light purple.
---@field attribute_modifiers ItemAttributeModifier[]? What it changes about whoever wears or holds it: `{ { attribute = "minecraft:attack_damage", amount = 6, operation = "add_value", slot = "main_hand" } }`. These replace the kind's own (a sword's damage) rather than adding to them.
---@field can_break string[]? Block kinds a player in adventure mode can break with it: `{ "minecraft:stone" }`.
---@field can_place_on string[]? Block kinds a player in adventure mode can place it on.
---@field food ItemFood? What eating it does. Any item with `food` can be eaten, even one that isn't food: `{ nutrition = 4, saturation = 2.4 }`.
---@field cooldown ItemCooldown? A cooldown after using it (an ender pearl's): `{ seconds = 2 }`. Items with the same `group` share one.
---@field data table<string, any>? Your script's own data, saved on the stack itself (its persistent data container), so it goes wherever the item goes: tag a coin with `data = { coin = true }`. A table keyed by strings, holding what a `data()` table may: strings, numbers, booleans, tables of them, `Vec3`s, `Location`s, `Item` tables, and `Player` and `Centity` handles, which come back as the same types. Absent when the stack carries none. Anything else (a function, a `Menu` window) is an error, naming where it was.

--- How `nf.commands.register` describes a command: everything optional. A key that isn't one of these is an error.
---@class CommandDefinition
---@field description string? One line for the server's help.
---@field permission string? A permission node the sender needs. On Paper the command (or subcommand) doesn't exist for a player without it: not in tab completion, not when typed. The console has every permission.
---@field aliases string[]? Other names for it.
---@field arguments CommandArgument[]? What comes after it, in order. Arguments with a `default` are optional and must come last; a `text` argument takes the rest of the line, so it must be the last one.
---@field subcommands table<string, Subcommand>? Words that can come next, each with its own arguments, permission and handler: `{ add = { ... }, remove = { ... } }`. Names are lowercase letters, digits, `_` and `-`. A subcommand wins over an argument when both could take the word.
---@field players_only boolean? Refuses the console (and command blocks) with a message, so the handler can count on `event.player`. A subcommand under a `players_only` command is players-only too.

--- A word after a command (or after another subcommand) with its own arguments and handler: `/shop give <player>`. A key that isn't one of these is an error.
---@class Subcommand
---@field description string? One line for the server's help.
---@field permission string? A permission node the sender needs. On Paper the command (or subcommand) doesn't exist for a player without it: not in tab completion, not when typed. The console has every permission.
---@field arguments CommandArgument[]? What comes after it, in order. Arguments with a `default` are optional and must come last; a `text` argument takes the rest of the line, so it must be the last one.
---@field subcommands table<string, Subcommand>? Words that can come next, each with its own arguments, permission and handler: `{ add = { ... }, remove = { ... } }`. Names are lowercase letters, digits, `_` and `-`. A subcommand wins over an argument when both could take the word.
---@field players_only boolean? Refuses the console (and command blocks) with a message, so the handler can count on `event.player`. A subcommand under a `players_only` command is players-only too.
---@field handler (fun(event: CommandEvent))? Runs when someone types it. A handler that errors is logged and whoever typed the command is told; the command stays.

--- One argument of a command. Its `type` decides what may be typed, what tab completion offers, the usage message, and the value the handler gets in `event.arguments[name]`. On Paper it's a real Brigadier argument, so the player's client checks and completes it as they type. A key that isn't one of these is an error.
---@class CommandArgument
---@field name string Its key in `event.arguments`, and what the usage message shows (`<amount>`). Letters, digits and `_`, not starting with a digit; unique within the command.
---@field type "word"|"text"|"integer"|"number"|"boolean"|"choice"|"player"|"players"|"entity"|"entities"|"world"|"position"|"location"|"block_state"|"item"|"centity"|"centity_kind"|"menu"|"dialog" What it takes, and what the handler gets: `"word"`: one word, a `string`. `"text"`: the rest of the line, spaces and all, a `string`. `"integer"`: a whole number, within `min` and `max` when given, a `integer`. `"number"`: any number, within `min` and `max` when given, a `number`. `"boolean"`: `true` or `false`, a `boolean`. `"choice"`: one of `choices`, as typed, a `string`. `"player"`: an online player by name, or a selector that picks exactly one (`@p`, `@s`), a `Player`. `"players"`: a name or a selector (`@a`, `@a[distance=..10]`) picking at least one player, a `Player[]`. `"entity"`: a selector that picks exactly one entity, an online player's name, or a loaded entity's UUID (a `Player` for a player), an `Entity`. `"entities"`: a selector, name or UUID picking at least one entity, an `Entity[]`. `"world"`: a loaded world by name, a `World`. `"position"`: three coordinates, each a number, `~` (relative to the sender) or `^` (local to where they look); whole numbers are block centres in x and z, as in vanilla commands, a `Vec3`. `"location"`: the same three coordinates, in the sender's world facing the way they face (the default world and no facing from the console), a `Location`. `"block_state"`: a block state like `oak_stairs[facing=east]`, given back in full with every property (`"minecraft:oak_stairs[facing=east,half=bottom,…]"`), a `string`. `"item"`: an item like `diamond_sword`, or with components on Paper (`diamond_sword[enchantments={sharpness:3}]`), an `Item`. `"centity"`: a live centity by its instance id (completed from the live ones), a `Centity`. `"centity_kind"`: one of the project's centities, by its folder name, a `string`. `"menu"`: one of the project's menus, by its id, a `string`. `"dialog"`: one of the project's dialogs, by its id, a `Dialog`.
---@field default any Makes it optional: the value the handler gets when it's left out. A value of the argument's type (a number for `integer`, a `Vec3` for `position`, one of `choices` for `choice`), or `false` for "nothing", the way to make a `player` or `item` argument optional: then `event.arguments.target or event.player`.
---@field min number? The smallest value an `integer` or `number` argument takes.
---@field max number? The largest value an `integer` or `number` argument takes.
---@field choices string[]? What a `choice` argument takes: single words. Required for `choice`, and only for it.
---@field complete (fun(event: CommandEvent, partial: string): string[])? Tab completion of its own, instead of what its type offers. Gets the event so far (`event.arguments` holds the arguments before this one) and what has been typed of this argument; returns the suggestions. NetherForge keeps those starting with `partial`. Runs in the module's scope, within its instruction budget, every time a player's client asks, so keep it cheap.

--- What a command handler, and an argument's `complete`, receives.
---@class CommandEvent
---@field name string The command's name, as registered.
---@field label string What they typed to run it: the name, or one of its aliases.
---@field arguments table<string, any> The arguments by name, typed by their declaration: a `Player` for a `player` argument, a `Vec3` for a `position`, an `integer` for an `integer`. An optional argument left out has its `default`.
---@field input string Everything typed after the command's name, subcommands included, as typed.
---@field sender Sender Who ran it, player or console. Answer them with `event.sender:send_message(text)`.
---@field player Player? The player who ran it, or `nil` from the console. Never `nil` in a `players_only` command.

--- How `nf.worlds.create` makes a world. A key that isn't one of these is an error.
---@class WorldCreateOptions
---@field generator "normal"|"flat"|"void"|"amplified"|"large_biomes"? Which of the game's own world types generates it: the usual (`"normal"`), the default superflat (`"flat"`), nothing at all (`"void"`, for maps built or placed by scripts), or one of the other vanilla world types (`"amplified"`, `"large_biomes"`). Default `"normal"`. Not with `terrain`, which is one of the project's instead. Only a `"normal"` environment takes it: a nether or an end always generates as the game's own.
---@field terrain string? One of the project's terrains (`terrain/<id>.json`; `"acme:hills"` for a package's), which generates it in place of the game's own. Not with `generator`. Kept with the world, so it generates the same way when it's loaded again; a terrain the project changes later only shapes chunks generated afterwards. A world with a terrain is a `"normal"` environment.
---@field environment "normal"|"nether"|"end"? Which kind of dimension: its sky, its light, whether water boils. Default `"normal"`.
---@field dimension_type string? One of the project's dimension types (`dimension_types/<id>.json`; `"acme:deep"` for a package's): the world's build limits, light, sky and rules (beds, respawn anchors, piglins, raids) are its. Kept with the world, so it loads the same again. The server learns dimension types only as it starts: one it didn't start with (added or fixed since) is an error until it restarts. Default: the environment's own (the game's overworld, nether or end).
---@field seed integer? Its seed: a project generator makes the same world for the same seed. Default: a random one.
---@field structures boolean? Whether villages, temples and the like generate. Default `true`.
---@field keep_spawn_loaded boolean? Whether the chunks around its spawn stay loaded with nobody near. Default `false`.

--- How `world:unload` lets a world go. A key that isn't one of these is an error.
---@class WorldUnloadOptions
---@field save boolean? Whether it saves first. Default `true`; `false` throws away what changed since the last save (with `delete`, nothing is saved either way).
---@field delete boolean? Whether its files are deleted afterwards, and the project forgets it. Default `false`.
---@field move_players_to World? Which world the players in it go to, at its spawn. Default: the main world. To send them somewhere exact, teleport them before unloading.

--- How `border:set_size` changes it. A key that isn't one of these is an error.
---@class BorderSizeOptions
---@field ticks integer? How long it takes to get there, moving steadily. Default 0: at once.

--- How a border hurts players outside it: what `border:damage()` gives and `border:set_damage` takes. A key that isn't one of these is an error.
---@class BorderDamage
---@field amount number? Damage each second for every block past `buffer` (half a heart is 1). The game starts at 0.2.
---@field buffer number? How many blocks outside the border are safe. The game starts at 5.

--- When players' screens turn red as a border comes near: what `border:warning()` gives and `border:set_warning` takes. A key that isn't one of these is an error.
---@class BorderWarning
---@field distance integer? Within this many blocks of it. The game starts at 5.
---@field ticks integer? When a shrinking border will reach them within this many ticks. The game starts at 300 (15 seconds).

--- What `world:save_structure` keeps. A key that isn't one of these is an error.
---@class StructureSaveOptions
---@field entities boolean? Whether the entities in the box are saved with the blocks (never players, and never what centities are drawn with). Default `false`.

--- How `world:place_structure` places one. A key that isn't one of these is an error.
---@class StructurePlaceOptions
---@field rotation integer? How far it turns clockwise (seen from above) about its smallest corner, in degrees: 0, 90, 180 or 270 (any multiple of 90). Default 0.
---@field mirror "none"|"left_right"|"front_back"? Whether it's mirrored first: `"left_right"` flips it along z, `"front_back"` along x. Default `"none"`.
---@field integrity number? How much of it is placed, 0 to 1: each block is kept with this chance. Default 1: all of it.
---@field entities boolean? Whether the entities saved with it are placed too. Default `true`.

--- How a block is changed. A key that isn't one of these is an error.
---@class BlockSetOptions
---@field update boolean? Whether its neighbours react (sand falls, redstone updates, water flows), as when a player places a block. Default `true`; `false` sets it quietly.

--- How particles are spawned. Every key is optional; which ones a particle takes depends on its kind, and one it doesn't take is an error, as is any key that isn't one of these.
---@class ParticleOptions
---@field count integer? How many, scattered around the position by `spread`. Default 1. With 0, one particle is sent moving along `spread` (a direction) at `speed`.
---@field spread Vec3? How far they scatter along each axis (a Gaussian spread), in blocks. Default none. With `count = 0`, the direction the one particle moves in.
---@field speed number? How fast they move, for particles that move. Default 0.
---@field color string? `"#rrggbb"`: dust, dust that changes colour, and coloured particles. Default white.
---@field to_color string? `"#rrggbb"`: what dust that changes colour fades to. Default white.
---@field size number? How big dust is, 0.01 to 4. Default 1.
---@field block_state string? Which block block particles are made of: `"minecraft:stone"`. Required for those.
---@field item Item? Which item item particles show. Required for those.
---@field viewers Player[]? Only these players see them (those near enough). Default: everyone near enough.
---@field force boolean? Show them even to players who turned particles down, and from up to 128 blocks away. Default `false`.

--- How a sound plays. A key that isn't one of these is an error.
---@class SoundOptions
---@field volume number? How loud, from 0. Default 1. Above 1 it carries further rather than getting louder: 16 blocks per 1.
---@field pitch number? How high, 0.5 (an octave down) to 2 (an octave up). Default 1.
---@field category "master"|"music"|"record"|"weather"|"block"|"hostile"|"neutral"|"player"|"ambient"|"voice"|"ui"? Which of the player's volume sliders it follows. Default `"master"`.

--- How weather set with `world:set_weather` lasts. A key that isn't one of these is an error.
---@class WeatherOptions
---@field ticks integer? How long it lasts before the game picks again (at least 1). Leave it out for a random length, as the game chooses.

--- How an explosion behaves. A key that isn't one of these is an error.
---@class ExplosionOptions
---@field fire boolean? Whether it sets fires. Default `false`.
---@field break_blocks boolean? Whether it breaks blocks. Default `true`.

--- Where `world:locate_biome` looks. A key that isn't one of these is an error.
---@class BiomeSearchOptions
---@field near Vec3? Where it looks from, in this world. Default the world's spawn.
---@field radius integer? How far it looks, in blocks across: 1 to 6400. Default 6400, as `/locate biome`.

--- How lightning strikes. A key that isn't one of these is an error.
---@class LightningOptions
---@field effect_only boolean? Only the flash and the thunder: no fire, no damage. Default `false`.

--- What a ray can hit. A key that isn't one of these is an error.
---@class RaycastOptions
---@field blocks boolean? Whether blocks stop it. Default `true`.
---@field entities boolean? Whether entities (players included) stop it, by their hitboxes. Default `true`.
---@field ignore Entity[]? Entities it goes through: the one it's cast from, usually. Default none.
---@field centities boolean? Whether centities' hitboxes stop it. Default `true`.
---@field fluids boolean? Whether water and lava stop it, as solid blocks. Default `false`: it goes through them.

--- What `world:raycast` hit: where, and what. Exactly one of `block`, `entity` and `centity` is set.
---@class RaycastHit
---@field position Vec3 Where the ray met it, in the world.
---@field normal Vec3 The unit direction straight out of the face it hit, in the world.
---@field distance number How far along the ray that is, in blocks.
---@field block Block? The block it hit, or `nil`.
---@field entity Entity? The entity it hit, or `nil`.
---@field centity Centity? The centity whose hitbox it hit, or `nil`.
---@field node Node? Which node of `centity` it hit (its hitbox), or `nil` for anything else.

--- What `nf.bossbars.create` makes a bar with. Every key is optional; a key that isn't one of these is an error.
---@class BossBarOptions
---@field text Text? What it says, MiniMessage. Default none.
---@field color "pink"|"blue"|"red"|"green"|"yellow"|"purple"|"white"? Default `"pink"`, the way vanilla bars start.
---@field style "progress"|"notched_6"|"notched_10"|"notched_12"|"notched_20"? One bar, or split into notches. Default `"progress"`, one bar.
---@field progress number? How full, 0 to 1. Default 1.

--- What `nf.loot.roll` and `nf.loot.fill` roll with: who and what the roll is for, which the table's conditions ask about. Every key is optional; a key that isn't one of these is an error.
---@class LootContext
---@field player Player? The player behind the roll: who killed the mob, broke the block or opened the chest. A `player` condition passes only with one. Default none.
---@field tool Item? What the player killed or broke it with, for `tool` and `enchantment` conditions. Default none: those conditions don't pass.
---@field luck number? The roll's luck: more `bonusRolls`, and each entry's weight shifted by its `quality`. Default 0.
---@field looted Entity? The entity whose loot this is, which a game table for a mob needs. Default none.
---@field location Location? Where a game table is rolled. Default the player's place, else the looted entity's.
---@field seed integer? Rolls with this seed give the same items every time, on the server and in the editor. Default a new one each roll.

--- A block is about to be placed.
---@class ProjectBlockPlaceEvent: Event
---@field player Player? Who is placing it, or `nil` for a script.
---@field block CustomBlock Where it goes: what is there now is replaced, so `block:custom()` is `nil` until it has been placed (`this:id()` says which block is being placed).
---@field item Item? The stack placing it, or `nil` for a script.

--- A block was clicked.
---@class ProjectBlockClickEvent: Event
---@field player Player Who clicked.
---@field block CustomBlock The block.
---@field click "left"|"right" Which button: the left (hit) or right (use).
---@field face "up"|"down"|"north"|"south"|"east"|"west" Which face of the block was clicked.
---@field item Item? What's in the hand the click was with, or `nil` for an empty hand.
---@field hand "main_hand"|"off_hand" Which hand: the game asks for each hand in turn.

--- A block ticked.
---@class ProjectBlockTickEvent: Event
---@field block CustomBlock The block that ticked.

--- What `nf.teams.create` makes a team with. Every key is optional; a key that isn't one of these is an error.
---@class TeamOptions
---@field display_name Text? What it's called where Minecraft names a team, MiniMessage. Default its name.
---@field prefix Text? What goes before each member's name, MiniMessage. Default none.
---@field suffix Text? What goes after each member's name, MiniMessage. Default none.
---@field color "black"|"dark_blue"|"dark_green"|"dark_aqua"|"dark_red"|"dark_purple"|"gold"|"gray"|"dark_gray"|"blue"|"green"|"aqua"|"red"|"light_purple"|"yellow"|"white"? The colour of members' names and glow. Default none.
---@field friendly_fire boolean? Whether members can hurt each other. Default `true`.
---@field see_invisible_teammates boolean? Whether members see each other while invisible. Default `true`.
---@field nametags "always"|"never"|"hide_for_other_teams"|"hide_for_own_team"? `"always"` (the default), `"never"`, `"hide_for_other_teams"` (only teammates see them) or `"hide_for_own_team"` (only players outside the team see them).
---@field collision "always"|"never"|"push_other_teams"|"push_own_team"? `"always"` (the default), `"never"`, `"push_other_teams"` (members pass through each other) or `"push_own_team"` (members push only each other).

--- What a schedule is called and what to do about a run the server missed. A key that isn't one of these is an error.
---@class ScheduleOptions
---@field id string? A name for the schedule, which what it last ran is kept under, so it's known across restarts. Lowercase letters, digits and `_` (starting with a letter or digit, at most 64 characters), and no two of a package's live schedules share one. Default none: the schedule is forgotten when it stops.
---@field catch_up boolean? Run once when the script starts if a run was missed since the schedule last ran (the server was off over its time). Needs an `id`; a schedule seen for the first time has missed nothing. Default `false`: a missed run is skipped.

--- One statement of `db:transaction`.
---@class DatabaseStatement
---@field sql string One SQL statement in the database's own dialect (SQLite's for the package's own database; MySQL's or PostgreSQL's for a connection the server owner set up), with `?` where a value goes: the values come in `params`, never inside the text, so a name a player typed can't become SQL. A second statement after a `;` is an error: use `db:transaction` for several. On PostgreSQL a literal `?` (the JSON operators) is written `??`.
---@field params table? The values for the `?`s, in order: `{ player_name, 5 }`. Each is a `string`, `number`, `integer` or `boolean` (stored as 1 or 0); a hole in the list (`{ 1, nil, 3 }`) is SQL `NULL`, and so are the last `?`s when the list is shorter (Lua can't tell `{ name, nil }` from `{ name }`). Any other kind of value is an error, and more values than `?`s fails the statement.

--- What `db:execute` (and each statement of `db:transaction`) did.
---@class DatabaseResult
---@field changes integer How many rows it inserted, updated or deleted.
---@field last_insert_id integer? On the package's own database, the `rowid` of the last row it inserted (the new row's `INTEGER PRIMARY KEY`), or `nil` when nothing was ever inserted. On a MySQL connection, the `AUTO_INCREMENT` key the statement generated, or `nil` when it generated none. On PostgreSQL always `nil`: ask for the key with `INSERT ... RETURNING id` through `db:query`.

--- What `nf.http.request` takes.
---@class HttpRequestOptions
---@field url string An `http://` or `https://` URL, without a user name or password in it (send credentials in a header). Its host must be one the calling package declares in `requires`.
---@field method "GET"|"POST"|"PUT"|"PATCH"|"DELETE"|"HEAD"|"OPTIONS"? The HTTP method; `"GET"` when left out.
---@field headers table<string, string>? Request headers by name. `Host`, `Content-Length`, `Transfer-Encoding`, `Connection`, `Upgrade` and the like are set by the server and refused here. A `User-Agent` of `NetherForge` is sent unless you give one. `Authorization`, `Cookie` and `Proxy-Authorization` are dropped when a redirect leads to another host.
---@field body string? The request body, as text (UTF-8). Its size is limited by `http.max-request-bytes` in the server's `config.yml`. A `GET`, `HEAD` or `OPTIONS` can't have one.

--- What `nf.http.request` gives.
---@class HttpResponse
---@field status integer The status code: `200`, `404`.
---@field headers table<string, string> The response headers by name in lowercase (`response.headers["content-type"]`); a header sent more than once is its values joined with `, `.
---@field body string The body as text, decoded by the charset the response names (UTF-8 when it names none), so a response that isn't text (an image) doesn't survive. Its size is limited by `http.max-response-bytes` in the server's `config.yml`.
---@field url string The URL the response came from: the request's own, or where its redirects ended.

--- How `nf.particles.play` plays an effect. Every key is optional; a key that isn't one of these is an error.
---@class ParticlePlayOptions
---@field viewers Player[]? Only these players see it (still only those within range). Players who leave drop out. Default: everyone in range.
---@field scale number? Multiplies its distances: shape sizes, offsets, spreads and speeds (not dust sizes). More than 0, at most 16. Default 1.
---@field loop boolean? Whether it starts again after its last tick, until it's stopped. Default: what its file says.
---@field follow Centity|Node|Player|Entity? Follow this from the start, as `effect:follow` does. Each tick, before it spawns anything, the effect moves to the target: its position (a node's `world_position()`), facing the target's yaw (a node's centity's), pitch 0. When the target stops existing (a removed centity, a player who left, an entity that died or was removed, a centity or entity whose chunk unloaded) the effect ends with reason `"target_gone"`. `location_or_position` is still needed: it's where the effect is if the target is already gone, which ends it on its first tick.
---@field offset Vec3? With `follow`: where the effect sits from the target, as for `effect:follow`. Without `follow` it's an error.

--- A particle effect ended.
---@class EffectEndEvent: Event
---@field effect Effect The effect.
---@field reason "finished"|"stopped"|"target_gone"|"removed"|"unloaded" Why: it played to its end; `stop()` was called; what it followed went; its file was deleted; or the script that played it unloaded (a reload, its centity removed, the server stopping).

--- How `nf.cutscenes.play` plays a cutscene. Every key is optional; a key that isn't one of these is an error.
---@class CutscenePlayOptions
---@field origin Location|Vec3? Where the path's coordinates are measured from: every camera position is added to it (the path is moved, not turned). A `Location` also says which world the cutscene is in (its `yaw` and `pitch` are not used); a `Vec3` is in the player's world. Default: the path's coordinates are the world's, in the player's world.
---@field skippable boolean? Whether the player may end it early by sneaking (reason `"skipped"`). Default: what the cutscene's file says, which defaults to `false`.

--- A cutscene ended.
---@class CutsceneEndEvent: Event
---@field cutscene Cutscene The cutscene.
---@field player Player Who was watching.
---@field reason "finished"|"stopped"|"skipped"|"replaced"|"player_left"|"unloaded" Why: it played to its end; `stop()` was called; the player sneaked, in a skippable one; another cutscene was played for the player; the player left the server; or the script that played it unloaded (a reload, the server stopping).

--- A cutscene reached a named cue.
---@class CutsceneCueEvent: Event
---@field cutscene Cutscene The cutscene.
---@field player Player Who is watching.
---@field cue string The cue's `event` name in the cutscene's file.

--- How `nf.time.format` and `nf.time.parse` read a clock time. A key that isn't one of these is an error.
---@class TimeOptions
---@field zone string? Which time zone the clock time is in: an IANA name like `"Europe/London"` or `"America/New_York"`, `"UTC"`, or an offset like `"+02:00"`. Default the server owner's zone (`schedules.time-zone` in `config.yml`, the server's own by default), the one `nf.schedule` runs in. A zone the server doesn't know is an error.

--- Which noise `nf.random.noise2` and `noise3` give. A key that isn't one of these is an error.
---@class NoiseOptions
---@field seed integer? Which noise: each seed is a different pattern. Default 0.
---@field scale number? What the coordinates are multiplied by first: smaller is smoother and wider (0.05 changes over about 20 blocks). Default 1.

--- What `nf.test.raise` gives back.
---@class RaisedEvent
---@field cancelled boolean Whether a handler called `event:cancel()` (always `false` for an event that can't be cancelled).
---@field event table The payload as the handlers left it: a writable field they assigned (`message`) holds what they set.

--- A custom event (a name with a `:`, raised with `emit`): an `Event` plus whatever fields its payload had, so any field reads as `any`.
---@class CustomEvent: Event
---@field [string] any

--- Writes a line to the server console (and the editor's, with the file and line it came from). Arguments are converted with `tostring` and joined with tabs.
---@type fun(...: any)
log = nil

--- The script's own handle: `Centity` in a centity script, `Menu` in a menu script, `Dialog` in a dialog script, `ProjectItem` in a item script, `ProjectBlock` in a block script. Nothing in a module.
---
--- Start a script with `local this = this --[[@as Centity]]` so lua-language-server knows which.
---@type Centity|Menu|Dialog|ProjectItem|ProjectBlock
this = nil
